var e={level:`C1`,type:`multiple-choice`,questions:[{id:`c1-mc-001`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not only ___ late, but he also forgot the documents.`,explanation:`After 'Not only' at the start of a sentence, the verb comes before the subject: Not only was he late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'Not only' the verb must come before the subject.`,2:`'did he' needs a main verb. With 'be', invert it directly: was he.`,3:`After 'Not only' we need inversion.`},options:[`he was`,`was he`,`did he`,`he did`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-002`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`She is said ___ the most talented pianist of her generation.`,explanation:`Passive reporting structure: is said + to-infinitive.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'being' does not follow 'is said' in this structure.`,2:`After 'is said' we need 'to be'.`,3:`'that she' would need a full clause: it is said that she is...`},options:[`to be`,`being`,`be`,`that she`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-003`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Seldom ___ such a generous offer from a competitor.`,explanation:`After a negative adverb like 'Seldom' at the start, put the auxiliary before the subject: Seldom have we seen such rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion, the word order is wrong after a fronted 'Seldom'.`,1:`The past simple without 'did' is not inverted, so the clause does not follow 'Seldom' correctly.`,3:`A main verb cannot move before the subject in modern English; we need an auxiliary.`},options:[`we have received`,`we received`,`have we received`,`received we`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-004`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What surprised me most ___ how calm everyone remained during the storm.`,explanation:`In a 'What' cleft sentence, the 'What' clause is singular and is followed by 'is/was': What I need is time.`,wrongReasons:{1:`A 'What' clause takes a singular verb, so 'were' does not agree.`,2:`'it' adds an extra subject; the 'What' clause is already the subject.`,3:`Repeating 'what' makes the sentence ungrammatical.`},options:[`was`,`were`,`it was`,`what was`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-005`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If she had accepted the job in Tokyo, she ___ fluent in Japanese now.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: past condition (if + past perfect) with a present result (would + base form): If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would have been' refers to the past, but 'now' shows a present result.`,1:`'will be' is used for real future conditions, not unreal ones.`,2:`'had been' cannot be used in the result clause of a conditional.`},options:[`would have been`,`will be`,`had been`,`would be`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-006`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The new manager quickly ___ a good rapport with her team.`,explanation:`'Establish a rapport (with someone)' is a fixed collocation meaning to build a good relationship: He established a rapport with his students.`,wrongReasons:{0:`We do not say 'do a rapport'; 'do' collocates with tasks and duties.`,1:`'make up' means to invent or to reconcile, not to build a rapport.`,2:`'take a rapport' is not a natural collocation in English.`},options:[`did`,`made up`,`took`,`established`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-007`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a job application: 'I am writing to ___ for the position of sales director.'`,explanation:`In formal letters, use neutral or formal verbs instead of phrasal or informal ones: I am writing to apply for...`,wrongReasons:{1:`'go for' is an informal phrasal verb, too casual for an application letter.`,2:`'have a shot' is very informal and also needs 'at'.`,3:`'try out' means to test something and is informal.`},options:[`apply`,`go for`,`have a shot`,`try out`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-008`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the report twice, she finally sent it to her manager.`,explanation:`A perfect participle clause (Having + past participle) shows an action completed before the main action: Having finished dinner, we went out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Checked' alone has a passive meaning, but she did the checking.`,2:`'Having been checked' is passive and would mean she was checked.`,3:`'To have checked' does not express a completed earlier action here.`},options:[`Checked`,`Having checked`,`Having been checked`,`To have checked`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-009`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Under no circumstances ___ the laboratory without protective glasses.`,explanation:`'Under no circumstances' at the start triggers inversion: Under no circumstances should you open this door.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The modal must come before the subject after 'Under no circumstances'.`,1:`There is no inversion at all here, and a fronted negative phrase requires it.`,2:`The subject must sit between the modal and the main verb, not after the verb.`},options:[`visitors should enter`,`visitors enter`,`should enter visitors`,`should visitors enter`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-010`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was the cleaner, not the guard, ___ noticed the broken window.`,explanation:`In an 'It was ... who' cleft, use 'who' or 'that' for a person who is the subject: It was Tom who called.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' is used for things, not people.`,1:`'whom' is an object form; here the person is the subject of 'noticed'.`,2:`'what' cannot introduce the second part of an 'It was...' cleft.`},options:[`which`,`whom`,`what`,`who`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-011`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If he weren't so stubborn, he ___ the argument with his sister last week.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a present general situation (if + past simple) with a past result (would have + past participle): If I were braver, I would have spoken up yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'wouldn't start' refers to the present or future, but 'last week' is past.`,1:`'won't start' is for real future conditions.`,2:`The past perfect alone is not used in the result clause.`},options:[`wouldn't start`,`won't start`,`hadn't started`,`wouldn't have started`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-012`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The scientists hope to ___ light on why some birds migrate at night.`,explanation:`'Shed/throw light on something' means to help explain it: The letters shed light on his early life.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put light on' is not the idiom; we say 'shed light on'.`,2:`'give light' refers to producing light, not to explaining something.`,3:`'open light' is not an English collocation.`},options:[`put`,`shed`,`give`,`open`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-013`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most appropriate option for a formal email: 'Please find ___ the documents you requested.'`,explanation:`'Please find attached...' is a standard formula in formal emails: Please find attached my CV.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'stuck on' is informal and does not describe email attachments.`,2:`'here's' is a contraction used in informal writing and breaks the grammar of 'Please find'.`,3:`'put in' sounds casual and does not form the set phrase.`},options:[`attached`,`stuck on`,`here's`,`put in`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-014`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ in 1890, the bridge is still used by thousands of cars every day.`,explanation:`A past participle clause has a passive meaning: Built in 1890 = It was built in 1890.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Building' is active, but the bridge did not build anything.`,1:`'Having built' is active and suggests the bridge built something.`,2:`'To build' expresses purpose, not a past fact about the bridge.`},options:[`Building`,`Having built`,`To build`,`Built`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-015`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only after the audit had been completed ___ the true scale of the losses.`,explanation:`'Only after + clause' at the start causes inversion in the main clause: Only after he left did I notice the mistake.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The main clause keeps normal word order, but 'Only after...' requires inversion.`,2:`Emphatic 'did' is still placed after the subject here, so this is not inversion.`,3:`Moving a main verb before its subject is not possible; use 'did' + subject + base verb.`},options:[`the board realised`,`did the board realise`,`the board did realise`,`realised the board`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-016`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`All I want to do this weekend ___ catch up on sleep.`,explanation:`'All + clause' works like 'the only thing' and is followed by singular 'is' and often a bare infinitive: All I did was ask.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The 'All' clause is singular, so 'are' does not agree.`,1:`'it' adds an unnecessary second subject.`,2:`'that is' means 'in other words' and does not complete this structure.`},options:[`are`,`it is`,`that is`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-017`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`___ you leave now, you'll miss the rush-hour traffic.`,explanation:`'Provided (that)' means 'only if' and introduces a real condition: Provided you pay on time, there is no fee.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Unless' means 'if not', which reverses the intended meaning.`,2:`'Even though' expresses contrast, not a condition.`,3:`'Supposing not' is not a correct way to start a conditional clause.`},options:[`Provided`,`Unless`,`Even though`,`Supposing not`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-018`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The company has come under ___ criticism for its treatment of workers.`,explanation:`'Fierce criticism' is a strong, natural collocation: The plan faced fierce criticism.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'hard criticism' is unusual; we prefer 'harsh' or 'fierce'.`,2:`'tall' describes height and does not collocate with 'criticism'.`,3:`'thick' describes physical density, not the strength of criticism.`},options:[`hard`,`fierce`,`tall`,`thick`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-019`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a report: 'Sales ___ significantly in the third quarter.'`,explanation:`Formal reports use single precise verbs rather than informal phrases: Profits declined by 5%.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'went down a lot' is conversational and vague.`,2:`'took a nosedive' is an informal idiom.`,3:`'dropped like a stone' is a colourful informal simile, not suitable for a report.`},options:[`went down a lot`,`declined`,`took a nosedive`,`dropped like a stone`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-020`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ from the top of the tower, the city looks surprisingly small.`,explanation:`A past participle clause can describe the subject passively: Seen from above, the fields look like a chessboard.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Seeing' would mean the city is doing the seeing.`,1:`'Having seen' is active and implies the city saw something.`,2:`'To be seeing' is not used to describe how something appears.`},options:[`Seeing`,`Having seen`,`To be seeing`,`Seen`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-021`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Little ___ that the stranger on the train would become her business partner.`,explanation:`Negative 'Little' at the start means 'not at all' and needs inversion: Little did they know what was coming.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Normal word order is not possible after a fronted 'Little' with this meaning.`,1:`Here 'did' follows the subject, so the required inversion is missing.`,3:`A lexical verb cannot be inverted with its subject; we need the auxiliary 'did'.`},options:[`she suspected`,`she did suspect`,`did she suspect`,`suspected she`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-022`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was only when the bill arrived ___ we realised how expensive the restaurant was.`,explanation:`In an 'It was ... that' cleft, the second part is introduced by 'that', even after a time expression: It was in 2010 that we met.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Repeating 'when' is a common error; the cleft structure needs 'that'.`,1:`'which' refers to a noun, but here we need the cleft link 'that'.`,2:`'so' expresses result and does not fit the cleft pattern.`},options:[`when`,`which`,`so`,`that`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-023`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`You may use the studio at weekends ___ you lock it when you leave.`,explanation:`'As long as' means 'on condition that': You can stay as long as you keep quiet.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'unless' means 'except if', which gives the opposite meaning.`,2:`'in case' means 'as a precaution', not a condition.`,3:`'even if' means the condition makes no difference, which does not fit here.`},options:[`as long as`,`unless`,`in case`,`even if`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-024`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Before signing anything, you should ___ legal advice.`,explanation:`'Seek advice' is a formal collocation meaning to ask for advice: Seek medical advice if symptoms continue.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'search advice' is wrong; 'search' needs 'for' and does not collocate with 'advice'.`,2:`'look advice' is ungrammatical; 'look' needs a preposition.`,3:`'find out' means to discover information, not to ask for advice.`},options:[`search`,`seek`,`look`,`find out`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-025`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a complaint letter: 'I would be grateful if you could ___ this matter as soon as possible.'`,explanation:`'Look into' (or 'investigate') is acceptable in formal letters; slang phrasal verbs are not: Please look into the delay.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'sort out' is common in speech but too informal for a formal complaint.`,2:`'fix up' is informal and means to repair or arrange.`,3:`'have a go at' is informal and can mean to criticise someone.`},options:[`sort out`,`look into`,`fix up`,`have a go at`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-026`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the risks, the climbers decided to continue towards the summit.`,explanation:`'Despite' can be followed by an -ing form in a participle-style clause: Despite feeling ill, he went to work.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Despite' is never followed by 'of'.`,1:`'Although' needs a full clause with a subject, or a participle without 'that'.`,3:`'In spite' must be followed by 'of'.`},options:[`Despite of knowing`,`Although knowing that`,`Despite knowing`,`In spite knowing`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-027`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`No sooner ___ the lease on the flat than the landlord announced he was selling the building.`,explanation:`'No sooner' is followed by inverted past perfect and then 'than': No sooner had I sat down than the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{0:`This keeps normal word order, but 'No sooner' at the start requires inversion.`,1:`With 'No sooner ... than' the standard form is the past perfect: had we signed.`,3:`The past simple without inversion breaks both rules of this structure.`},options:[`we had signed`,`did we sign`,`had we signed`,`we signed`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-028`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What the company should have done ___ the problem publicly.`,explanation:`After 'What + subject + do/did', we can use 'be' + bare infinitive: What she did was call the police.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'admitted' is a past form; after 'What ... done was' we use the base form.`,2:`The 'What' clause is singular and the continuous form is wrong here.`,3:`'to' must be followed by a base form, not 'admitted'.`},options:[`was admit`,`was admitted`,`were admitting`,`is to admitted`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-029`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If it hadn't been for the lifeguard, the boy ___.`,explanation:`'If it hadn't been for + noun' means 'without' and takes a third conditional result: If it hadn't been for you, I would have failed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would drown' refers to the present or future, but the event is past.`,1:`'will have drowned' is a future perfect, not an unreal past result.`,3:`The past perfect alone is not used in the result clause.`},options:[`would drown`,`will have drowned`,`would have drowned`,`had drowned`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-030`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The minister refused to ___ responsibility for the failed policy.`,explanation:`'Accept/take/assume responsibility' are standard collocations: She accepted full responsibility for the error.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'receive responsibility' is not natural; we 'accept' or 'take' it.`,1:`'bring responsibility' is not a collocation; we 'accept' or 'take' responsibility.`,2:`'get responsibility' is informal and not used for admitting blame.`},options:[`receive`,`bring`,`get`,`accept`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-031`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for an official notice: 'Passengers are ___ to keep their belongings with them at all times.'`,explanation:`Formal notices use passive verbs like 'are requested/required to': Visitors are requested to sign in.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'told off' means 'scolded' and does not fit a polite request.`,2:`'asked nicely' sounds childish and informal.`,3:`'bugged' is slang for 'annoyed'.`},options:[`requested`,`told off`,`asked nicely`,`bugged`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-032`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The woman ___ next to me on the plane turned out to be a famous chef.`,explanation:`A present participle can replace a relative clause with an active verb: the woman sitting next to me = the woman who was sitting next to me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'sit' is a base form and cannot describe the noun after it.`,2:`'who sitting' lacks the auxiliary 'was'.`,3:`'having sat' suggests a completed earlier action, not an ongoing one.`},options:[`sit`,`sitting`,`who sitting`,`having sat`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-033`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Rarely ___ a debut novel received such universal praise from critics.`,explanation:`After 'Rarely' at the start, use the auxiliary before the subject; here the subject is 'a debut novel', so 'has': Rarely has a film earned so much.`,wrongReasons:{1:`There is already a subject ('a debut novel'); adding 'it' creates two subjects.`,2:`The subject 'a debut novel' is singular, so 'have' does not agree.`,3:`'did' would need the base form 'receive', not the participle 'received'.`},options:[`has`,`it has`,`have`,`did`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-034`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The reason ___ he left the band was a disagreement over money.`,explanation:`'The reason why + clause' is a common cleft-like structure: The reason why I called was to apologise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'what' cannot follow 'the reason' as a relative word.`,2:`'because' cannot follow 'the reason' directly in this structure.`,3:`'for' alone does not introduce a clause here.`},options:[`what`,`why`,`because`,`for`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-035`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Take an umbrella ___ it rains later.`,explanation:`'In case' means 'as a precaution because something might happen': Take a map in case you get lost.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'unless' means 'if not' and gives the wrong meaning.`,2:`'provided' means 'on condition that', which does not make sense here.`,3:`'if only' expresses a wish or regret, not a precaution.`},options:[`in case`,`unless`,`provided`,`if only`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-036`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`There is a ___ chance that the flight will be delayed because of the snow.`,explanation:`'A strong chance' (also 'a good chance') collocates naturally: There's a strong chance of rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'heavy' collocates with rain or traffic, not with 'chance'.`,1:`'a large chance' is not idiomatic in English.`,3:`'wide' describes range or distance, not probability.`},options:[`heavy`,`large`,`strong`,`wide`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-037`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for an academic essay: 'There is ___ evidence to support this theory.'`,explanation:`Academic writing prefers precise single words such as 'insufficient' or 'limited': There is insufficient data.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'not enough of' is grammatically wrong here and too informal.`,1:`'hardly any old' is colloquial and ungrammatical.`,3:`'a bit less' is vague and conversational.`},options:[`not enough of`,`hardly any old`,`insufficient`,`a bit less`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-038`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not ___ what to say, he simply nodded and left the room.`,explanation:`Negative participle clauses put 'not' before the participle: Not knowing the way, we asked for directions.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'known' is passive and does not fit: he did not know.`,2:`'Not to know' expresses purpose or advice, not a reason.`,3:`'having known' suggests a completed earlier state, which sounds odd for a moment of uncertainty.`},options:[`knowing`,`known`,`to know`,`having known`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-039`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not until the final whistle ___ that the team had actually won the league.`,explanation:`A sentence starting with 'Not until...' inverts the main clause: Not until midnight did the guests leave.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a fronted 'Not until...' the main clause must be inverted.`,1:`A main verb cannot be placed before the subject; use 'did' + subject.`,2:`The auxiliary must come before the subject, not after it.`},options:[`the fans believed`,`believed the fans`,`the fans did believe`,`did the fans believe`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-040`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It's not the price ___ worries me; it's the poor quality.`,explanation:`Use 'that' (or 'which' for things) in an 'It's ... that' cleft: It's the noise that bothers me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'what' cannot follow the focused noun in an 'It's...' cleft.`,1:`'who' is for people, but 'the price' is a thing.`,3:`'where' refers to places, not to the price.`},options:[`what`,`who`,`that`,`where`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-041`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`But for her quick thinking, the meeting ___ a disaster.`,explanation:`'But for + noun' means 'if it had not been for' and takes would have + past participle for the past: But for the map, we would have got lost.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will be' is a real future form, not an unreal past result.`,1:`The past perfect alone cannot be the result clause.`,3:`'would being' is not a correct verb form.`},options:[`will be`,`had been`,`would have been`,`would being`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-042`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The two countries finally ___ an agreement after months of talks.`,explanation:`'Reach an agreement' is the standard collocation: The unions reached an agreement with management.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'arrive' needs 'at' and is less natural with 'agreement'.`,2:`'came an agreement' is ungrammatical; 'come' would need 'to'.`,3:`'do an agreement' is not used in English.`},options:[`reached`,`arrived`,`came`,`did`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-043`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a business letter: 'We ___ any inconvenience this may cause.'`,explanation:`'We apologise for any inconvenience' is a fixed formal phrase: We apologise for the delay.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'sorry-ish' is very informal and sounds insincere.`,1:`'feel bad over' is colloquial and uses the wrong preposition.`,3:`'say sorry to' needs a person after 'to', not 'inconvenience'.`},options:[`are sorry-ish about`,`feel bad over`,`apologise for`,`say sorry to`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-044`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`All the paintings ___ in the exhibition were donated by private collectors.`,explanation:`A past participle can replace a passive relative clause: the paintings displayed = the paintings which are displayed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'displaying' is active, but the paintings are displayed by someone.`,2:`'having displayed' is active and implies the paintings displayed something.`,3:`'to displaying' is ungrammatical here.`},options:[`displaying`,`displayed`,`having displayed`,`to displaying`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-045`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the warning been issued earlier, the village could have been evacuated in time.`,explanation:`In formal third conditionals, 'if' can be dropped and 'had' moved to the front: Had I known, I would have called.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'If' needs the auxiliary after the subject: 'If the warning had been issued'.`,2:`'Should' inversion refers to unlikely future events, not unreal past ones.`,3:`'Were' inversion is used for present unreal situations or with 'to', not with 'been issued'.`},options:[`Had`,`If`,`Should`,`Were`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-046`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What happened next ___ that the whole system crashed.`,explanation:`'What happened was (that) + clause' is a common cleft for telling stories: What happened was that we got lost.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'did' cannot link the 'What' clause to the clause that follows.`,1:`'had' does not connect the two parts of this cleft sentence.`,3:`'made' changes the meaning and is ungrammatical with 'that' here.`},options:[`did`,`had`,`was`,`made`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-047`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ you, I'd ask for a second opinion before the operation.`,explanation:`In second conditionals giving advice we use 'If I were you': If I were you, I'd apologise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'am' is used for real present conditions, not for advice with an imaginary situation.`,2:`'had been' refers to the past, but the advice is about now.`,3:`'would' is not normally used in the if-clause.`},options:[`am`,`were`,`had been`,`would be`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-048`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Her decision to resign came as a ___ surprise to her colleagues.`,explanation:`'Come as a complete surprise' is a common collocation: The news came as a complete surprise.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'a full surprise' is not idiomatic.`,2:`'a whole surprise' is not used; 'whole' does not intensify 'surprise'.`,3:`'entire' means 'all of something' and does not intensify 'surprise'.`},options:[`complete`,`full`,`whole`,`entire`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-049`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most appropriate opening for a formal letter when you don't know the recipient's name.`,explanation:`When you don't know the recipient's name in a formal letter, use 'Dear Sir or Madam': Dear Sir or Madam, I am writing to...`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Hi there' is friendly and informal.`,1:`'Hey everyone' is very informal and suits group chats.`,3:`'To whoever' sounds rude and is not a standard opening.`},options:[`Hi there,`,`Hey everyone,`,`Dear Sir or Madam,`,`To whoever,`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-050`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ by the noise, the baby started crying.`,explanation:`A past participle clause shows that the subject receives the action: Frightened by the dog, she ran away.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Frightening' would mean the baby frightened someone.`,2:`'Having frightened' is active and would mean the baby frightened something.`,3:`'Frighten' is a base form and cannot begin a participle clause.`},options:[`Frightening`,`Frightened`,`Having frightened`,`Frighten`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-051`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ you need any assistance during your stay, please contact reception.`,explanation:`'Should' at the start replaces 'if' in formal first conditionals: Should you have questions, call us.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Would' cannot replace 'if' at the start of a conditional clause.`,1:`'Had you need' is ungrammatical; 'had' inversion needs a past participle.`,2:`'Were you need' is ungrammatical; 'were' inversion needs 'to' + verb or an adjective/noun.`},options:[`Would`,`Had`,`Were`,`Should`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-052`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was in this very room ___ the treaty was signed in 1921.`,explanation:`When a place phrase is focused in an 'It was...' cleft, use 'that': It was in Paris that they met.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Adding 'it' creates an extra subject after 'where'.`,2:`'which' would need a noun to refer to, not a prepositional phrase.`,3:`'what' is not used to complete an 'It was...' cleft.`},options:[`where it`,`that`,`which`,`what`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-053`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Supposing the bank ___ your loan, what would you do?`,explanation:`'Supposing' can introduce a hypothetical condition with the past simple: Supposing you lost your job, how would you cope?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present tense suggests a real condition, but the result 'would you do' is hypothetical.`,1:`'had refuse' is not a correct form; the past perfect needs 'refused'.`,2:`'would' is not normally used in the condition clause.`},options:[`refuses`,`had refuse`,`would refuse`,`refused`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-054`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The tourism industry plays a ___ role in the island's economy.`,explanation:`'Play a key/vital/major role in' is a fixed collocation: Education plays a key role in development.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'heavy' does not collocate with 'role'; we say 'key' or 'major'.`,2:`'thick' describes physical width and does not collocate with 'role'.`,3:`'tall' describes height, not importance.`},options:[`heavy`,`key`,`thick`,`tall`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-055`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a policy document: 'Employees must ___ the company's safety regulations.'`,explanation:`'Comply with' is formal and used for rules and laws: All staff must comply with the regulations.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'stick to' is neutral-informal and less suitable for official documents.`,2:`'go along with' means to accept someone's idea, often reluctantly.`,3:`'play by' (the rules) is an informal idiom.`},options:[`stick to`,`comply with`,`go along with`,`play by`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-056`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ been warned several times, the driver was finally banned from the road.`,explanation:`'Having been + past participle' is a perfect passive participle clause: Having been told off, he apologised.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Being been' is ungrammatical.`,2:`'Had been' at the start would form an inverted conditional, which does not fit here.`,3:`'To have been warned' does not explain a reason in this way.`},options:[`Having`,`Being`,`Had`,`To have`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-057`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Hardly ___ the stage when the lights went out.`,explanation:`'Hardly' at the start takes inverted past perfect, followed by 'when': Hardly had we arrived when it began to rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a fronted 'Hardly' we need inversion, not normal word order.`,2:`With 'Hardly ... when' the standard tense is the past perfect, not the past simple.`,3:`There is no inversion here and the tense is also wrong for this structure.`},options:[`the actor had reached`,`had the actor reached`,`did the actor reach`,`the actor reached`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-058`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The thing ___ annoys me about the new office is the lack of natural light.`,explanation:`'The thing that + verb ... is' is a cleft-like structure: The thing that matters is your health.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'what' already means 'the thing that', so 'the thing what' is wrong.`,1:`'who' refers to people, not things.`,2:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},options:[`what`,`who`,`whose`,`that`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-059`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the river ___ its banks again, the whole valley will be flooded.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base form: If it rains, we will stay in.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'will' is not used in the if-clause of a first conditional.`,2:`'burst' without -s does not agree with 'the river' in the present.`,3:`'would' does not match the real-future result 'will be flooded'.`},options:[`bursts`,`will burst`,`burst`,`would burst`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-060`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`He was ___ asleep when the earthquake struck.`,explanation:`'Fast asleep' is a fixed collocation meaning sleeping deeply: The baby is fast asleep.`,wrongReasons:{0:`We say 'in a deep sleep' or 'sound asleep', not 'deep asleep'.`,1:`'hard asleep' is not used in English.`,3:`'strong asleep' is not an English collocation.`},options:[`deep`,`hard`,`fast`,`strong`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-061`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a news report: 'The suspect was ___ by police on Tuesday evening.'`,explanation:`Formal reports use 'apprehended' or 'arrested'; slang verbs are avoided: The thief was apprehended at the border.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'nabbed' is informal slang.`,1:`'collared' is informal British slang for arrested.`,2:`'picked off' means shot or eliminated one by one, which is a different meaning.`},options:[`nabbed`,`collared`,`picked off`,`apprehended`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-062`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`When ___ about the delay, the airline blamed the weather.`,explanation:`A past participle can follow 'when' with a passive meaning: When asked, he refused to comment.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'asking' would mean the airline asked someone.`,2:`'having asked' is active and changes the meaning.`,3:`'to ask' is not used after 'when' in this way.`},options:[`asking`,`asked`,`having asked`,`to ask`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-064`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What we need ___ a clear plan, not more meetings.`,explanation:`A 'What' clause is singular, so it takes 'is/was': What you need is rest.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The 'What' clause is singular, so 'are' does not agree.`,1:`'be' is the base form and cannot be the main verb of this clause.`,2:`'being' is not a finite verb and cannot complete the sentence.`},options:[`are`,`be`,`being`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-065`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Had I known about the strike, I ___ the train.`,explanation:`Inverted third conditional: Had + subject + past participle, would have + past participle: Had she called, I would have helped.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'wouldn't take' refers to the present, but the strike was in the past.`,2:`The past simple cannot be the result of an unreal past condition.`,3:`'won't take' is a real future form.`},options:[`wouldn't take`,`wouldn't have taken`,`didn't take`,`won't take`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-066`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The charity aims to ___ awareness of mental health issues among teenagers.`,explanation:`'Raise awareness (of)' is a fixed collocation: The campaign raised awareness of recycling.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'rise' is intransitive and cannot take an object like 'awareness'.`,1:`'grow awareness' is avoided in careful formal English.`,2:`'lift awareness' is not idiomatic.`},options:[`rise`,`grow`,`lift`,`raise`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-067`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for an email to a client: 'Could you ___ whether the meeting is still going ahead?'`,explanation:`In formal emails, use precise verbs like 'confirm' or 'advise': Could you confirm the date?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'let me know quick' is informal and uses an adjective instead of an adverb.`,1:`'give me a heads-up' is informal workplace slang.`,3:`'tell me straight' is blunt and conversational.`},options:[`let me know quick`,`give me a heads-up on`,`confirm`,`tell me straight`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-068`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ to the public in 2015, the park has become a popular spot for families.`,explanation:`A past participle clause can give background information with a passive meaning: Opened in 2015 = It was opened in 2015.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Opening' is active and suggests the park opened something.`,1:`'Having opening' is ungrammatical; it should be 'Having opened'.`,2:`'Being opening' combines two -ing forms and is ungrammatical.`},options:[`Opening`,`Having opening`,`Being opening`,`Opened`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-069`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`So complex ___ that even the experts needed weeks to understand it.`,explanation:`'So + adjective' at the start leads to inversion: So loud was the music that we left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a fronted 'So + adjective' the verb must come before the subject.`,1:`'did' cannot be used with an adjective; the verb here is 'be'.`,3:`This uses 'did' and normal word order, which are both wrong after 'So complex'.`},options:[`the software was`,`did the software`,`was the software`,`the software did`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-070`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was her grandmother ___ she owed her love of music.`,explanation:`In formal clefts, a preposition can move to the front of 'whom': It was Anna to whom he wrote.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'owe ... to' needs the preposition; without it the meaning is incomplete.`,1:`'who to' is wrong word order; formally it should be 'to whom'.`,2:`A preposition cannot go before 'that'.`},options:[`whom`,`who to`,`to that`,`to whom`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-071`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Even if you ___ me a fortune, I wouldn't sell my grandfather's watch.`,explanation:`'Even if' + past simple, would + base form expresses a hypothetical situation that would not change the result: Even if he begged, I wouldn't agree.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present form clashes with the hypothetical result 'wouldn't sell'.`,2:`'had offer' is ungrammatical; the participle is 'offered'.`,3:`'would' does not normally go in the if-clause.`},options:[`offered`,`offer`,`had offer`,`would offer`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-072`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The judge ___ a heavy fine on the company for polluting the river.`,explanation:`'Impose a fine/tax/ban on' is a formal collocation: The city imposed a ban on cars.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put up' means to raise or display, not to give a penalty.`,2:`'make a fine' is not used in English.`,3:`'force' needs 'someone to do something', not 'a fine on'.`},options:[`put up`,`imposed`,`made`,`forced`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-073`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a university letter: 'Applicants ___ to submit two references.'`,explanation:`Formal writing uses 'be required to' for obligations: Students are required to attend.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'have got' is informal and here also needs 'to'.`,2:`'gotta' is very informal spoken English.`,3:`'need to go' changes the meaning and is informal.`},options:[`have got`,`are required`,`gotta`,`need to go`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-074`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ his car keys, Martin had to walk home in the rain.`,explanation:`'Having + past participle' gives a reason that happened before the main action: Having missed the bus, she took a taxi.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Lost' alone would mean Martin was lost.`,1:`'Being lost' means Martin did not know where he was.`,2:`'Having been lost' is passive and would describe Martin, not the keys.`},options:[`Lost`,`Being lost`,`Having been lost`,`Having lost`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-075`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Nowhere ___ more clearly than in the city's crumbling public housing.`,explanation:`'Nowhere' at the start of a sentence requires inversion: Nowhere is the coffee better than here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Normal word order is wrong after a fronted 'Nowhere'.`,1:`'does' cannot combine with an adjective; with 'be', invert 'is' directly.`,3:`The adjective cannot come first here; we need verb + subject + adjective.`},options:[`the crisis is visible`,`does the crisis visible`,`is the crisis visible`,`visible is the crisis`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-076`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Where the plan went wrong ___ in underestimating the costs.`,explanation:`'Where + clause + was...' focuses on a place or point: Where we failed was in the marketing.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The 'Where' clause is singular, so 'were' does not agree.`,2:`'did' cannot link the two parts of this cleft.`,3:`'had' does not connect the 'Where' clause to the phrase that follows.`},options:[`was`,`were`,`did`,`had`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-077`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Unless the committee ___ the proposal by Friday, the project will be cancelled.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not' and is followed by a positive verb in the present for future conditions: Unless you hurry, you'll be late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Unless' already contains the negative, so 'doesn't' creates a double negative meaning.`,1:`'will' is not used after 'unless' in a first conditional.`,3:`The past tense does not match the real future result 'will be cancelled'.`},options:[`doesn't approve`,`will approve`,`approves`,`approved`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-078`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`After the accident, he suffered from ___ pain in his lower back.`,explanation:`'Chronic pain' is a medical collocation for pain that continues for a long time: She has chronic back pain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'durable' describes objects that last, not pain.`,2:`'elderly' describes old people, not pain.`,3:`'antique' describes old valuable objects, not medical conditions.`},options:[`durable`,`chronic`,`elderly`,`antique`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-079`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a cover letter: 'I have ___ experience in project management.'`,explanation:`Formal writing uses adjectives like 'extensive' or 'considerable' instead of informal quantifiers: She has extensive experience.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'loads of' is informal.`,1:`'tons of' is informal and exaggerated.`,3:`'a heap of' is colloquial.`},options:[`loads of`,`tons of`,`extensive`,`a heap of`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-080`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`There were dozens of fans ___ outside the stadium for autographs.`,explanation:`After 'There + be + noun', a present participle can describe what the people are doing: There were people standing in the rain.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'waited' is a past form and would wrongly suggest a passive meaning.`,2:`'to' must be followed by the base form, not 'waited'.`,3:`'have waited' is a finite verb and cannot follow the noun here.`},options:[`waiting`,`waited`,`to waited`,`have waited`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-081`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not once ___ for help, even when the project was falling apart.`,explanation:`'Not once' at the start is followed by auxiliary + subject: Not once did she complain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Normal word order is not possible after a fronted 'Not once'.`,1:`'did' must come before 'he' for inversion.`,2:`Main verbs are not inverted in modern English; use 'did he ask'.`},options:[`he asked`,`he did ask`,`asked he`,`did he ask`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-082`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It is the volunteers ___ deserve the credit for this success.`,explanation:`In an 'It is ... who' cleft, the verb agrees with the focused noun: It is the nurses who deserve thanks.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' refers to things, not people.`,2:`'what' cannot follow the focused noun in a cleft.`,3:`'whom' is an object form, but here the volunteers are the subject of 'deserve'.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`what`,`whom`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-083`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If you heat ice, it ___.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for general truths: if + present simple, present simple: If you mix red and blue, you get purple.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would melt' presents a scientific fact as imaginary.`,1:`The future perfect is not used for general truths.`,3:`The past tense does not express a general truth here.`},options:[`would melt`,`will have melted`,`melts`,`melted`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-084`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The government has pledged to ___ measures to reduce air pollution.`,explanation:`'Take measures/steps/action' is a standard collocation: The school took measures to improve safety.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'do measures' is not natural English.`,1:`'make measures' is a common error; we 'take' measures.`,3:`'give measures' is not a collocation.`},options:[`do`,`make`,`take`,`give`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-085`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for an apology from a company: 'We ___ that your order has been delayed.'`,explanation:`'We regret that...' is a formal way to express sorrow in business writing: We regret that the event is cancelled.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'gutted' is informal British slang for very disappointed.`,2:`'bummed' is informal American slang.`,3:`'cut up' is informal and means very upset.`},options:[`feel gutted`,`regret`,`are bummed`,`are cut up`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-086`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ carefully, the plants will flower again next spring.`,explanation:`A past participle clause can express a condition: Looked after carefully = If they are looked after carefully.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Looking after' would mean the plants look after something.`,1:`'Having looked after' is active and implies the plants did the caring.`,2:`'Look after' is an imperative, which does not fit the sentence.`},options:[`Looking after`,`Having looked after`,`Look after`,`Looked after`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-087`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only by cutting costs drastically ___ survive the recession.`,explanation:`'Only by + -ing' at the start causes inversion: Only by working together can we succeed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a fronted 'Only by...' the modal must come before the subject.`,2:`This keeps normal order and adds 'be', which makes 'survive' ungrammatical.`,3:`The subject must come right after the modal, and 'be' does not belong here.`},options:[`the firm could`,`could the firm`,`the firm can be`,`could be the firm`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-088`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What I'd like you to do ___ check the figures before Friday.`,explanation:`After 'What + subject + do', use 'is' + (to) infinitive: What I'd suggest is (to) wait.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'does' cannot link the 'What' clause to the action.`,1:`The 'What' clause is singular, so 'are' is wrong.`,2:`'it's' adds an extra subject; the 'What' clause is already the subject.`},options:[`does`,`are`,`it's`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-089`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If they had invested in better equipment years ago, they ___ so many breakdowns these days.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: past condition with a present result: If they had planned better, they wouldn't be struggling now.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'wouldn't have had' describes a past result, but 'these days' is present.`,1:`'won't have' is for a real future condition.`,2:`The past perfect alone is not used in the result clause.`},options:[`wouldn't have had`,`won't have`,`hadn't had`,`wouldn't be having`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-090`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Her research has had a ___ impact on how we treat the disease.`,explanation:`'A profound/significant impact' is a common formal collocation: The war had a profound impact on the region.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'deep-seated' collocates with fears or beliefs, not with 'impact'.`,1:`'tall' describes height and does not collocate with 'impact'.`,2:`'thick' describes physical size and cannot describe an impact.`},options:[`deep-seated`,`tall`,`thick`,`profound`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-091`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a scientific article: 'The results ___ that the treatment is effective.'`,explanation:`Scientific writing uses verbs like 'indicate' or 'suggest' to report findings: The data indicate a link.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'kind of show' is vague and conversational.`,2:`'tell us like' contains the filler 'like' used in speech.`,3:`'make it look' is informal and suggests a false appearance.`},options:[`kind of show`,`indicate`,`tell us like`,`make it look`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-092`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The documents ___ to the court yesterday contained new evidence.`,explanation:`A past participle can replace a passive relative clause: the documents submitted = the documents which were submitted.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'submitting' is active, but documents are submitted by people.`,1:`'having submitting' is not a correct form.`,2:`'submit' is a base form and cannot describe the noun here.`},options:[`submitting`,`having submitting`,`submit`,`submitted`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-093`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the government to raise taxes again, it would almost certainly lose the election.`,explanation:`'Were + subject + to-infinitive' is a formal way of saying 'if ... were to': Were he to resign, chaos would follow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Should the government to raise' is wrong; 'should' is followed by a bare infinitive.`,1:`'Had the government to raise' does not express a hypothetical condition.`,3:`'If the government to raise' lacks a verb; it needs 'were': 'If the government were to raise'.`},options:[`Should`,`Had`,`Were`,`If`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-094`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It wasn't until she moved abroad ___ she began to appreciate her hometown.`,explanation:`'It wasn't until ... that' is a fixed cleft pattern: It wasn't until 9pm that he arrived.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'than' is used for comparison, not in this cleft pattern.`,2:`'when' is a common error; the fixed pattern is 'not until ... that'.`,3:`'then' is an adverb and cannot introduce the clause here.`},options:[`than`,`that`,`when`,`then`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-095`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'll lend you the money on condition that you ___ me back by June.`,explanation:`'On condition that' is followed by the present simple for a future condition: I'll help on condition that you listen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used after 'on condition that' in this structure.`,2:`The past tense does not fit a real future condition.`,3:`'would' does not match the main clause 'I'll lend'.`},options:[`will pay`,`pay`,`paid`,`would pay`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-096`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`It's a ___ secret that the director is planning to retire.`,explanation:`'An open secret' is a fixed phrase for something supposed to be secret that everyone knows: It's an open secret they are dating.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'a public secret' is not the idiom.`,1:`'a known secret' is not used; it contradicts itself without being idiomatic.`,2:`'a clear secret' is not an English phrase.`},options:[`public`,`known`,`clear`,`open`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-097`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for an invitation: 'The pleasure of your company is ___ at the annual dinner.'`,explanation:`Formal invitations use set phrases such as 'The pleasure of your company is requested': Your presence is requested at...`,wrongReasons:{0:`'big time' is informal slang.`,2:`'loads' is informal and does not fit an invitation formula.`,3:`'fancied' is informal and means 'wanted' or 'found attractive'.`},options:[`wanted big time`,`requested`,`hoped for loads`,`fancied`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-098`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ in a small fishing village, she knew a lot about the sea.`,explanation:`'Having + past participle' explains a reason based on an earlier experience: Having lived in Spain, he speaks Spanish well.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Grown up' alone means 'adult', not 'having spent childhood'.`,2:`'Being grown up' means being an adult, which changes the meaning.`,3:`'To grow up' expresses purpose or future, not a past reason.`},options:[`Having grown up`,`Grown up`,`Being grown up`,`To grow up`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-099`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`In no way ___ responsible for the delay; the fault lay with the courier.`,explanation:`'In no way' at the start triggers inversion: In no way am I to blame.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Normal word order is wrong after a fronted 'In no way'.`,1:`'did' cannot be used with the adjective 'responsible'; the verb needed is 'be'.`,3:`This keeps normal order and uses an unsuitable continuous form.`},options:[`our team was`,`did our team`,`was our team`,`our team were being`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-100`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It's the system, not the staff, ___ to blame for the long queues.`,explanation:`In an 'It's ... that' cleft, the verb agrees with the focused noun; 'the system' is a singular thing: It's the rules that are unfair.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'the system' is singular, so 'are' does not agree.`,1:`'who' refers to people, but the focus here is 'the system'.`,3:`'what' cannot follow the focused noun in a cleft sentence.`},options:[`that are`,`who is`,`that is`,`what is`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-101`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I had been told about the change, I ___ here now waiting for nothing.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional with a continuous present result: If you had warned me, I wouldn't be standing here now.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'wouldn't have sat' refers to the past, but 'now' shows a present result.`,2:`'won't sit' is for a real future condition.`,3:`The past perfect alone cannot form the result clause.`},options:[`wouldn't have sat`,`wouldn't be sitting`,`won't sit`,`hadn't sat`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-102`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The rescue teams worked ___ to free the trapped miners.`,explanation:`'Around/round the clock' means all day and all night: Nurses work round the clock.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The idiom uses 'clock', not 'hour'.`,2:`The preposition in the idiom is 'around' or 'round', not 'over'.`,3:`'all the clocks' is not an idiom.`},options:[`around the hour`,`around the clock`,`over the clock`,`all the clocks`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-103`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a legal letter: 'Your account remains unpaid. ___, we will take legal action.'`,explanation:`'Failing + noun' is a formal way to say 'if this does not happen': Failing payment, the contract will end.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Or else' sounds threatening and informal.`,2:`'then yeah' adds spoken fillers unsuitable for a legal letter.`,3:`'like' is a spoken filler and makes the sentence informal.`},options:[`Failing payment`,`Or else`,`If not, then yeah`,`Otherwise, like`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-105`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Such ___ that the concert had to be moved to a bigger venue.`,explanation:`'Such + be + noun phrase + that' is a formal inverted pattern: Such was his anger that he left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Normal word order does not follow 'Such' in this structure.`,1:`'did' would need a main verb after the subject; this pattern uses 'be': Such was the demand.`,2:`This word order separates the noun phrase and makes the sentence ungrammatical.`},options:[`the demand for tickets was`,`did the demand for tickets`,`the demand was for tickets`,`was the demand for tickets`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-106`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What really matters to customers ___ reliable delivery and fair prices.`,explanation:`A 'What' clause is treated as singular even if a plural phrase follows: What matters is quality and price.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The continuous plural form does not agree with the singular 'What' clause.`,2:`'be' is the base form, not a finite verb.`,3:`'have' cannot link the 'What' clause to its complement.`},options:[`is`,`are being`,`be`,`have`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-107`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Should the alarm go off, staff ___ the building immediately.`,explanation:`Formal 'Should + subject + verb' introduces a possible future condition; the result can use a modal or imperative: Should it rain, the event will move indoors.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'must have left' expresses a deduction about the past, not an instruction.`,2:`The past perfect alone does not express an instruction for the future.`,3:`'would have left' describes an unreal past result.`},options:[`must leave`,`must have left`,`had left`,`would have left`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-108`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The committee will ___ a final decision at next week's meeting.`,explanation:`'Make a decision' is the standard collocation (also 'take a decision' in British English): We must make a decision soon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'do a decision' is a common learner error.`,1:`'have a decision' is not natural English.`,2:`'give a decision' is used for judges' rulings, not for committees choosing.`},options:[`do`,`have`,`give`,`make`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-109`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a report: 'Several factors ___ to the project's failure.'`,explanation:`'Contribute to' is a formal verb for causes and effects: Poor planning contributed to the delay.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'chipped in' is informal and means to help or give money.`,2:`'added up' means to make sense or to total numbers.`,3:`'played into the hands' needs 'of' and means helped an opponent.`},options:[`contributed`,`chipped in`,`added up`,`played into the hands`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-110`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the instructions, he assembled the bookcase in under an hour.`,explanation:`A present participle clause can show how something was done: Following the map, we found the cave.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Followed' is passive and would mean he was followed.`,1:`'Having been followed' is passive and means someone followed him.`,3:`'To be followed' is passive and expresses future or obligation.`},options:[`Followed`,`Having been followed`,`Following`,`To be followed`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-111`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Scarcely ___ the results when journalists started calling her.`,explanation:`'Scarcely' at the start takes inverted past perfect followed by 'when': Scarcely had he spoken when the door opened.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A fronted 'Scarcely' requires inversion, not normal word order.`,2:`The past simple with normal order breaks both rules of this structure.`,3:`With 'Scarcely ... when' the expected tense is the past perfect.`},options:[`she had announced`,`had she announced`,`she announced`,`did she announce`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-112`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was because of the fog ___ the flight was cancelled.`,explanation:`When a reason is focused in an 'It was...' cleft, use 'that': It was because of you that I stayed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'why' does not follow 'because of' in a cleft; use 'that'.`,1:`'so' shows result and breaks the cleft structure.`,3:`'which' would need a noun to refer to, not a reason phrase.`},options:[`why`,`so`,`that`,`which`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-113`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If only we ___ the tickets when they were still cheap!`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect expresses regret about the past: If only I had listened!`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple after 'if only' refers to the present, but 'when they were still cheap' is past.`,1:`'would book' expresses a wish for future change, not regret about the past.`,3:`The present perfect is not used after 'if only'.`},options:[`booked`,`would book`,`had booked`,`have booked`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-114`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`There has been a ___ increase in online fraud over the past year.`,explanation:`'A sharp increase/rise/fall' describes a sudden large change: Prices saw a sharp increase.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'pointed' collocates with remarks or questions, not with an increase.`,2:`'acute' collocates with pain or shortages, not with an increase in numbers.`,3:`'keen' collocates with interest or competition, not with an increase.`},options:[`sharp`,`pointed`,`acute`,`keen`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-115`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal ending for a letter that begins 'Dear Ms Patel'.`,explanation:`In British English, a formal letter with the recipient's name ends with 'Yours sincerely': Dear Mr Jones ... Yours sincerely.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Cheers' is a casual sign-off.`,1:`'See you soon' is friendly and informal.`,3:`'Lots of love' is for family and close friends.`},options:[`Cheers,`,`See you soon,`,`Yours sincerely,`,`Lots of love,`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-116`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ with the size of the task, the volunteers asked for more help.`,explanation:`'Faced with' is a common passive participle clause meaning 'confronted by': Faced with a choice, she hesitated.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Facing' is correct without 'with': we say 'Facing the task' or 'Faced with the task'.`,2:`'Having faced with' mixes active and passive forms incorrectly.`,3:`'Face' is a base form and cannot start this clause.`},options:[`Faced`,`Facing`,`Having faced with`,`Face`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-117`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only then ___ how much the old village had changed.`,explanation:`'Only then' at the start causes inversion: Only then did they understand the risk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Normal word order is wrong after a fronted 'Only then'.`,2:`'did' must come before 'I' to form inversion.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject; use 'did I notice'.`},options:[`I noticed`,`did I notice`,`I did notice`,`noticed I`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-118`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Something I've never understood ___ why people queue for hours for a new phone.`,explanation:`'Something/One thing + clause + is ...' is a cleft-like structure with singular 'is': One thing I love is the silence.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Something' is singular, so 'are' does not agree.`,1:`'it' adds a second subject to the sentence.`,2:`'what' alone does not provide the verb the sentence needs.`},options:[`are`,`it is`,`what`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-119`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Were the price to drop below ten dollars, investors ___ selling.`,explanation:`'Were + subject + to-infinitive' is a formal second conditional; the result uses would + base form: Were he to call, I would answer.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will start' is for real conditions, not the hypothetical 'Were ... to'.`,1:`The past perfect does not belong in the result clause.`,2:`The past simple alone does not express a hypothetical result.`},options:[`will start`,`had started`,`started`,`would start`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-120`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`She ___ a promise to visit her grandparents every Sunday.`,explanation:`'Make a promise' is the standard collocation; we also 'keep' or 'break' a promise: He made a promise to help.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'do a promise' is not English.`,2:`'say a promise' is not a collocation.`,3:`'tell' is used with stories or lies, not promises.`},options:[`made`,`did`,`said`,`told`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-121`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a press release: 'The company intends to ___ 200 new staff next year.'`,explanation:`Formal writing prefers single precise verbs like 'recruit' or 'employ': The hospital will recruit more nurses.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'take on loads of' contains the informal quantifier 'loads of'.`,1:`'bring on board some' is a business idiom that sounds informal here.`,2:`'get in' is informal and vague.`},options:[`take on loads of`,`bring on board some`,`get in`,`recruit`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-122`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Anyone ___ to attend the conference must register online by Friday.`,explanation:`A present participle can replace an active relative clause: anyone wishing = anyone who wishes.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'wished' is passive or past and does not fit: people wish to attend.`,2:`'having wish' is ungrammatical; it should be 'having wished'.`,3:`'who wishing' needs a finite verb: 'who wishes'.`},options:[`wishing`,`wished`,`having wish`,`who wishing`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-123`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`At no time ___ that the investment carried any risk.`,explanation:`'At no time' at the start triggers inversion of the auxiliary: At no time was the public informed.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Normal word order is not possible after a fronted 'At no time'.`,2:`'did' must be followed by a base form, not 'told'.`,3:`This is an active form with no auxiliary and no inversion; the meaning needs the passive.`},options:[`were clients told`,`clients were told`,`did clients told`,`clients told`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-124`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It is you, not your brother, ___ responsible for the damage.`,explanation:`In an 'It is you who...' cleft, the verb agrees with the focused pronoun 'you': It is you who are late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The verb must agree with 'you', so 'is' is wrong.`,1:`'that is' also fails to agree with 'you'.`,2:`'which' cannot refer to a person.`},options:[`who is`,`that is`,`which are`,`who are`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-125`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Without your support, I ___ the marathon last spring.`,explanation:`'Without + noun' can replace an if-clause; for the past use would have + past participle: Without the map, we would have got lost.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would never finish' refers to the present or future, but 'last spring' is past.`,1:`The past simple states a fact and does not express an unreal result.`,3:`The past perfect alone cannot be the result of a conditional.`},options:[`would never finish`,`never finished`,`would never have finished`,`had never finished`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-126`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The film ___ a lasting impression on everyone who saw it.`,explanation:`'Leave/make an impression on someone' is a fixed collocation: Her speech left a strong impression on me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put an impression' is not idiomatic.`,1:`'gave up' means to stop doing something.`,3:`'take an impression' means to copy a shape, such as of teeth.`},options:[`put`,`gave up`,`left`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-127`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for a notice in a hotel: 'Guests are kindly asked to ___ from smoking in the rooms.'`,explanation:`'Refrain from + -ing' is a formal way to ask people not to do something: Please refrain from talking.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'stop off' means to visit somewhere briefly.`,2:`'keep off' is used with grass or topics, and is less formal.`,3:`'lay off' is informal and means to stop doing or to dismiss workers.`},options:[`refrain`,`stop off`,`keep off`,`lay off`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-128`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ by the success of her first novel, she started writing a second one.`,explanation:`A past participle clause can show the cause of an action: Encouraged by the results, they expanded the project.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Encouraging' would mean she encouraged someone.`,2:`'Having encouraged' is active and changes the meaning.`,3:`'To encourage' expresses purpose, not cause.`},options:[`Encouraged`,`Encouraging`,`Having encouraged`,`To encourage`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-129`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not only ___ the museum's opening hours, but it has also cut ticket prices.`,explanation:`After a fronted 'Not only', the auxiliary comes before the subject: Not only has she won, but she has also set a record.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'Not only' at the start the auxiliary must come before the subject.`,2:`The subject has to go between the auxiliary and the participle.`,3:`Normal word order is wrong after a fronted 'Not only'.`},options:[`the council has extended`,`has the council extended`,`has extended the council`,`the council extended`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-130`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What sets this hotel apart ___ its attention to detail.`,explanation:`A 'What' clause takes a singular verb: What sets her apart is her patience.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The 'What' clause is singular, so 'are' does not agree.`,1:`'does' cannot link the subject clause to its complement.`,3:`'it is' adds an unnecessary extra subject.`},options:[`are`,`does`,`is`,`it is`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-131`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If she ___ more careful, she wouldn't keep losing her keys.`,explanation:`Second conditional for present unreal situations: if + past simple ('were'), would + base form: If he were taller, he would play basketball.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'had been' refers to the past, but 'keep losing' shows a present habit.`,2:`'is' is used for real conditions, not unreal ones.`,3:`'would' is not normally used in the if-clause.`},options:[`were`,`had been`,`is`,`would be`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-132`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`collocations`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The prime minister's speech was ___ received by the audience.`,explanation:`'Warmly received/welcomed' means received with enthusiasm: The proposal was warmly received.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'hotly' collocates with 'debated' or 'contested', not 'received'.`,2:`'heatedly' describes angry arguments, not a positive reception.`,3:`'sunnily' is not used to describe reception.`},options:[`hotly`,`warmly`,`heatedly`,`sunnily`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-133`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`register`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Choose the most formal option for an academic paper: 'This study ___ the effects of noise on concentration.'`,explanation:`Academic writing uses verbs such as 'examines', 'investigates' or 'explores': This paper investigates sleep patterns.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'checks out' is an informal phrasal verb.`,1:`'has a look at' is conversational.`,3:`'goes over quick' is informal and uses an adjective instead of an adverb.`},options:[`checks out`,`has a look at`,`examines`,`goes over quick`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-134`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Once ___, the decision cannot be reversed.`,explanation:`After conjunctions like 'once' or 'when', a past participle can be used with a passive meaning: Once opened, keep the bottle in the fridge.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'making' would mean the decision makes something.`,1:`'having make' is ungrammatical; the participle is 'made'.`,3:`'to make' is not used after 'once' in this way.`},options:[`making`,`having make`,`made`,`to make`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-136`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The board recommended that the chief executive ___ immediately.`,explanation:`After verbs like recommend, suggest and insist + that, formal English uses the base form (subjunctive): They insisted that he leave.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The -s form is not used in the subjunctive after 'recommend that'.`,2:`The past form is not used after 'recommended that' in formal English.`,3:`'will' is not used in a that-clause after 'recommend'.`},options:[`resign`,`resigns`,`resigned`,`will resign`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-137`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`After months of arguing, the two partners finally decided to bury the ___ and work together again.`,explanation:`'Bury the hatchet' means to stop arguing and become friends again: After the match, the rivals buried the hatchet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Axe' is similar in meaning, but the fixed idiom uses 'hatchet'.`,1:`'Knife' does not form an idiom with 'bury'.`,2:`'Bury the sword' is not an English idiom.`},options:[`axe`,`knife`,`sword`,`hatchet`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-138`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The negotiations ___ when neither side was willing to compromise on salaries.`,explanation:`'Break down' (of talks or a system) means to fail: The peace talks broke down after a week.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Break out' means to start suddenly, like a war or a fire.`,1:`'Break up' is used for relationships or crowds ending, not talks failing.`,3:`'Break in' means to enter a building by force.`},options:[`broke out`,`broke up`,`broke down`,`broke in`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-139`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the heavy traffic, we arrived at the airport in good time.`,explanation:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a clause: Despite the rain, we went out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Although' must be followed by a full clause with a verb.`,2:`'However' links two sentences and cannot introduce a noun phrase like this.`,3:`'Even though' needs a subject and a verb after it.`},options:[`Although`,`Despite`,`However`,`Even though`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-140`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The results of the experiment were ___ different from what the team had predicted.`,explanation:`An adverb modifies an adjective: considerably different, remarkably good.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Considerable' is an adjective and cannot modify another adjective.`,2:`'Consider' is a verb.`,3:`'Considerate' is an adjective meaning kind to others.`},options:[`considerably`,`considerable`,`consider`,`considerate`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-141`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The suspect is believed ___ the country two days before the robbery was discovered.`,explanation:`Use a perfect infinitive after a passive reporting verb for an earlier action: He is believed to have left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to leave' refers to the present or future, but the leaving happened earlier.`,1:`'to be leaving' describes an action in progress now, not a completed past one.`,2:`'is believed' needs a to-infinitive, not a participle.`},options:[`to leave`,`to be leaving`,`having left`,`to have left`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-142`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It is essential that every passenger ___ a valid ticket before boarding.`,explanation:`After 'It is essential/vital/important that', formal English uses the base form: It is vital that she be informed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has' is the normal present form, but the subjunctive needs 'have'.`,1:`'had' is past and does not fit this present requirement.`,3:`A that-clause needs a finite verb, not an -ing form.`},options:[`has`,`had`,`have`,`having`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-143`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I didn't mean to tell her about the party; I just let the cat out of the ___.`,explanation:`'Let the cat out of the bag' means to reveal a secret by accident: Tom let the cat out of the bag about the wedding.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The idiom uses 'bag', not 'box'.`,2:`'Out of the house' is literal and not part of this idiom.`,3:`'Basket' is not used in this idiom.`},options:[`box`,`bag`,`house`,`basket`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-144`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`We'll have to ___ the trip to the coast until the weather improves.`,explanation:`'Put off' means to postpone: They put off the wedding until spring.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Put out' means to extinguish a fire or a cigarette.`,2:`'Put up' means to raise or to give someone a place to stay.`,3:`'Put away' means to tidy something into its place.`},options:[`put off`,`put out`,`put up`,`put away`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-145`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The hotel was expensive; ___, the service was disappointing.`,explanation:`'Moreover' adds a further point, often a stronger one, in the same direction: The plan is costly; moreover, it is risky.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Whereas' compares two contrasting facts and does not follow a semicolon like this.`,1:`'Nevertheless' introduces a contrast, but both points here are negative.`,2:`'Otherwise' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`whereas`,`nevertheless`,`otherwise`,`moreover`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-146`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`I've ___ finished the report; I just need to add the conclusion.`,explanation:`'Nearly' is an adverb meaning almost: I've nearly finished.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Nearby' means close in distance.`,1:`'Near' is about physical distance, not 'almost'.`,3:`'Nearest' is a superlative adjective.`},options:[`nearby`,`near`,`nearly`,`nearest`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-148`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If only I ___ to my grandmother's advice when I was younger!`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect expresses regret about the past: If only I had studied harder.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'would listen' is used for annoying habits or wishes for change, not past regret.`,2:`'listened' expresses regret about the present, not about the past.`,3:`The present perfect cannot follow 'if only' for regret.`},options:[`had listened`,`would listen`,`listened`,`have listened`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-149`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The new tax rules are just the tip of the ___; far bigger changes are coming.`,explanation:`'The tip of the iceberg' is a small, visible part of a much larger problem: These complaints are just the tip of the iceberg.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'The tip of the mountain' is not an idiom; we would say 'the top'.`,1:`'Island' does not carry the idea of a hidden larger part.`,2:`'Volcano' does not form this idiom.`},options:[`mountain`,`island`,`volcano`,`iceberg`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-150`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Researchers are trying to ___ why the bees are disappearing so quickly.`,explanation:`'Find out' means to discover information: Can you find out when the train leaves?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Make up' means to invent a story or to become friends again.`,1:`'Figure in' means to include something in a calculation.`,3:`'Turn out' means to happen in a particular way, and it does not take this object.`},options:[`make up`,`figure in`,`find out`,`turn out`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-151`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Take an umbrella ___ it rains later in the afternoon.`,explanation:`'In case' means as a precaution against something that might happen: Take a map in case you get lost.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Unless' means 'if not', which gives the opposite meaning.`,2:`'So that' expresses purpose, but you don't want it to rain.`,3:`'As long as' sets a condition, which does not fit a precaution.`},options:[`unless`,`in case`,`so that`,`as long as`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-152`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`She works very ___ and is always the last to leave the office.`,explanation:`'Hard' is both an adjective and an adverb: She works hard. 'Hardly' means almost not.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Hardly' means almost not, which gives the opposite meaning.`,2:`'Hardy' is an adjective meaning strong and able to survive.`,3:`'Harder than' needs something to compare with.`},options:[`hard`,`hardly`,`hardy`,`harder than`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-153`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`All the paintings in the east wing ___ while the roof is being repaired.`,explanation:`Present continuous passive (am/is/are being + past participle) describes something done to the subject now: The files are being checked.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'are storing' is active; the paintings do not store anything.`,1:`'have stored' is active present perfect and makes the paintings the doers.`,2:`'store' is active present simple and has the wrong meaning.`},options:[`are storing`,`have stored`,`store`,`are being stored`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-155`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`With the deadline only two days away, the whole team is working against the ___.`,explanation:`'Work against the clock' means to work fast because there is little time: Engineers worked against the clock to fix the bridge.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Against time' exists in 'a race against time', but 'working against the time' is incorrect.`,2:`'Watch' is not used in this idiom.`,3:`'Against the hour' is not an English idiom.`},options:[`time`,`clock`,`watch`,`hour`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-156`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Despite strong opposition, the council decided to ___ the plan to build a new stadium.`,explanation:`'Go ahead with' means to start doing something planned: They went ahead with the launch despite the risks.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Come up against' means to face a problem, not to proceed.`,2:`'Get round to' means to finally do something after a delay.`,3:`'Fall back on' means to use something as a reserve when other things fail.`},options:[`go ahead with`,`come up against`,`get round to`,`fall back on`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-157`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`You can borrow my car ___ you return it with a full tank.`,explanation:`'Provided (that)' introduces a condition, like 'only if': You can go provided that you finish your homework.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Whereas' shows a contrast, not a condition.`,1:`'In case' expresses a precaution, not a condition for permission.`,2:`'Even if' means the result happens anyway, which is not the meaning here.`},options:[`whereas`,`in case`,`even if`,`provided that`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-158`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`We ___ ever go to the cinema now that we have young children.`,explanation:`'Hardly ever' means almost never: We hardly ever eat out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Almost ever' is not correct; we say 'almost never'.`,1:`'Nearly ever' is not used in English; we say 'nearly always'.`,3:`'Even ever' is not an English expression.`},options:[`almost`,`nearly`,`hardly`,`even`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-159`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The documents must ___ to the tax office no later than 31 January.`,explanation:`Modal + be + past participle forms the passive: The form must be signed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must submit' is active; documents cannot submit themselves.`,2:`'must have submitted' is a past deduction, not an obligation.`,3:`After a modal we need the base form 'be', not 'being'.`},options:[`submit`,`be submitted`,`have submitted`,`being submitted`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-160`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'd rather you ___ anyone about the surprise party.`,explanation:`'I'd rather' + another subject + past simple refers to the present or future: I'd rather you stayed.`,wrongReasons:{1:`A present form is not used after 'I'd rather' with a different subject.`,2:`'will' is not used after 'I'd rather you'.`,3:`'not tell' is missing the auxiliary 'did'.`},options:[`didn't tell`,`don't tell`,`won't tell`,`not tell`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-161`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`You hit the nail on the ___ when you said the real problem was poor communication.`,explanation:`'Hit the nail on the head' means to describe exactly what is causing a situation: She hit the nail on the head with that comment.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'End' does not form this idiom.`,1:`The idiom uses 'head', not 'top'.`,2:`'Point' may seem logical, but it is not part of the idiom.`},options:[`end`,`top`,`point`,`head`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-162`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I've decided to ___ sugar for a month to see if I feel healthier.`,explanation:`'Give up' means to stop doing or using something: She gave up smoking last year.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Give away' means to give something for free or reveal a secret.`,1:`'Give in' means to stop resisting or to surrender.`,3:`'Give off' means to produce a smell, heat or light.`},options:[`give away`,`give in`,`give up`,`give off`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-163`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Some critics loved the film, ___ others found it slow and confusing.`,explanation:`'Whereas' contrasts two different facts in one sentence: I like tea, whereas my wife prefers coffee.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Despite' cannot be followed by a full clause like 'others found'.`,2:`'Therefore' shows a result, not a contrast.`,3:`'In spite' needs 'of' and a noun, not a clause.`},options:[`despite`,`whereas`,`therefore`,`in spite`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-164`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The plane landed ___ despite the strong winds.`,explanation:`Use an adverb to describe how an action is done: The plane landed safely.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Safe' is an adjective and describes a noun, not how the plane landed.`,2:`'Safety' is a noun.`,3:`'Saved' is a past participle with a different meaning.`},options:[`safely`,`safe`,`safety`,`saved`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-165`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`It ___ that the merger will lead to hundreds of job losses.`,explanation:`Impersonal passive: It + is + reporting verb (past participle) + that-clause: It is thought that prices will rise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has feared' is active and needs a real subject.`,1:`'It fears' is active and suggests 'it' is a person who fears.`,2:`'fear' is a state verb and is not normally used in the continuous here.`},options:[`has feared`,`fears`,`is fearing`,`is feared`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-166`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`She talks about the project as if she ___ in charge of it, but she isn't.`,explanation:`'As if' + past simple (or 'were') shows that something is not true: He acts as if he were the boss.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'is' suggests she really is in charge, but the sentence says she isn't.`,1:`The present perfect does not show the unreal meaning.`,3:`'will be' refers to the future and is not used for an unreal comparison.`},options:[`is`,`has been`,`were`,`will be`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-167`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Don't worry about the interview; for someone with your experience it'll be a piece of ___.`,explanation:`'A piece of cake' means something very easy: The exam was a piece of cake.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'A piece of bread' is literal, not an idiom.`,2:`'Easy as pie' exists, but not 'a piece of pie'.`,3:`'A piece of chocolate' has only a literal meaning.`},options:[`bread`,`cake`,`pie`,`chocolate`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-168`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The rumours about the singer's retirement ___ to be completely false.`,explanation:`'Turn out' + to-infinitive means to be discovered to be: The stranger turned out to be my cousin.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Turn up' means to arrive or appear.`,2:`'Turn down' means to refuse an offer or reduce volume.`,3:`'Turn over' means to flip something or give control to someone.`},options:[`turned out`,`turned up`,`turned down`,`turned over`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-169`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The flight was cancelled ___ a technical fault in one of the engines.`,explanation:`'Owing to' is followed by a noun phrase and gives a reason: The match was cancelled owing to rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Since' giving a reason must be followed by a clause, not a noun phrase.`,1:`'Because' needs a clause; with a noun we need 'because of'.`,2:`'So' introduces a result, not a cause.`},options:[`since`,`because`,`so`,`owing to`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-170`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`He was ___ injured in the accident and spent three months in hospital.`,explanation:`Adverbs modify past participles used as adjectives: seriously injured, badly damaged.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Seriousness' is a noun.`,1:`'Serious' is an adjective and cannot modify 'injured'.`,3:`'More serious' is a comparative adjective and needs a comparison.`},options:[`seriousness`,`serious`,`seriously`,`more serious`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-171`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`By the time the firefighters arrived, most of the warehouse ___.`,explanation:`Past perfect passive (had been + past participle) shows an action finished before another past moment: The food had been eaten.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'destroyed' is active here and needs an object.`,2:`'was destroying' is active continuous; the warehouse did not destroy anything.`,3:`Present perfect does not fit a sequence of past events.`},options:[`destroyed`,`had been destroyed`,`was destroying`,`has been destroyed`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-172`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`___ it not been for your quick thinking, we would have missed the last train.`,explanation:`'Had it not been for' is the inverted form of 'If it hadn't been for', for past situations: Had it not been for the map, we'd have got lost.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Were it not for' has no 'been' and refers to the present, not the past.`,2:`'Should it not' is used for unlikely future conditions, not past ones.`,3:`'Did it not' is not a conditional inversion.`},options:[`Had`,`Were`,`Should`,`Did`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-173`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`He's been feeling a bit under the ___ since he came back from his trip.`,explanation:`'Under the weather' means slightly ill: I'm feeling a bit under the weather today.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Climate' is never used in this idiom.`,1:`'Under the sky' is literal and does not mean ill.`,2:`'Under the rain' is not idiomatic English.`},options:[`climate`,`sky`,`rain`,`weather`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-174`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Our supplies were beginning to ___, so we headed back to the village.`,explanation:`'Run out' means to be used up completely: Time is running out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Run into' means to meet by chance or hit something.`,1:`'Run over' means to hit with a vehicle or exceed a time limit.`,3:`'Run up' means to build up a debt or a bill.`},options:[`run into`,`run over`,`run out`,`run up`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-175`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The evidence was weak. ___, the jury found him guilty.`,explanation:`'Nonetheless' introduces a contrast with what was just said: The task was hard. Nonetheless, she finished it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Consequently' shows a logical result, but a guilty verdict is not a result of weak evidence.`,2:`'Furthermore' adds a similar point, not a contrast.`,3:`'Thus' introduces a result, which does not fit here.`},options:[`Consequently`,`Nonetheless`,`Furthermore`,`Thus`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-176`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The two reports were written ___ by different authors.`,explanation:`An adverb describes how something was done: The two teams worked independently.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Independent' is an adjective and cannot describe the verb.`,2:`'Independence' is a noun.`,3:`'Dependent' is an adjective with the opposite meaning.`},options:[`independently`,`independent`,`independence`,`dependent`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-177`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`She had her passport ___ at the airport, so she couldn't board the plane.`,explanation:`Have + object + past participle can describe something bad that happened to someone: He had his car stolen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'stole' is the past simple, not the past participle.`,1:`'stealing' would mean the passport was doing the stealing.`,2:`'have something to steal' has a completely different meaning.`},options:[`stole`,`stealing`,`to steal`,`stolen`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-178`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish my neighbours ___ their music so loud every night.`,explanation:`'Wish' + would expresses annoyance at someone's repeated behaviour that we want to change: I wish he would stop shouting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is never used after 'wish'; we need the past form 'would'.`,1:`A present form cannot follow 'wish'.`,3:`'hadn't played' refers to a single past event, not a nightly habit.`},options:[`won't play`,`don't play`,`wouldn't play`,`hadn't played`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-179`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The minister's resignation came out of the ___; nobody had expected it.`,explanation:`'Out of the blue' means suddenly and unexpectedly: She called me out of the blue after ten years.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Out of the dark' is not an idiom for something unexpected.`,2:`'Out of thin air' exists, but 'out of the air' is not the idiom here.`,3:`'Out of the sky' is literal, as with a falling object.`},options:[`dark`,`blue`,`air`,`sky`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-180`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The professor ___ several important points that I hadn't considered.`,explanation:`'Bring up' means to mention a subject: She brought up the question of costs.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Bring about' means to cause something to happen.`,2:`'Bring down' means to make a government fall or reduce prices.`,3:`'Bring off' means to succeed in doing something difficult.`},options:[`brought up`,`brought about`,`brought down`,`brought off`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-181`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ he had all the qualifications, he wasn't offered the job.`,explanation:`'Although' is followed by a full clause and introduces a contrast: Although it was late, we kept working.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'However' does not join two clauses in this position.`,1:`'Despite' cannot be followed by a full clause; we would need 'despite having'.`,2:`'In spite of' needs a noun or -ing form, not a clause.`},options:[`However`,`Despite`,`In spite of`,`Although`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-182`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`I ___ disagree with the decision to close the library.`,explanation:`'Strongly' collocates with verbs of opinion: I strongly believe, I strongly object.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Strength' is a noun.`,1:`'Strong' is an adjective and cannot modify the verb 'disagree'.`,3:`'Stronger' is a comparative adjective.`},options:[`strength`,`strong`,`strongly`,`stronger`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-183`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The candidates are said ___ for several hours before the results were announced.`,explanation:`A perfect continuous infinitive after a passive reporting verb describes an earlier action in progress: They are said to have been working all night.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to wait' does not show that the waiting happened earlier and lasted.`,2:`'wait' is intransitive here, so it has no passive form.`,3:`'are said' needs a to-infinitive, not an -ing form.`},options:[`to wait`,`to have been waiting`,`to be waited`,`waiting`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-184`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The lawyer demanded that her client ___ access to all the evidence.`,explanation:`The subjunctive passive is 'be' + past participle after demand/insist that: They demanded that he be released.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'is given' is the normal indicative form, not the subjunctive.`,2:`'was given' is past indicative and not used after 'demanded that' in formal style.`,3:`'gives' is active and makes the client the giver.`},options:[`be given`,`is given`,`was given`,`gives`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-185`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Buying that cheap printer was a false economy; the ink costs an arm and a ___.`,explanation:`'Cost an arm and a leg' means to be very expensive: That car cost him an arm and a leg.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The idiom pairs 'arm' with 'leg', not 'foot'.`,1:`'An arm and a hand' is not an idiom.`,2:`'Head' does not complete this idiom.`},options:[`foot`,`hand`,`head`,`leg`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-187`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Sales have fallen sharply this year. ___, the company plans to cut costs.`,explanation:`'As a result' introduces a consequence of the previous sentence: It rained all day. As a result, the match was cancelled.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Even so' introduces a surprising contrast, not a consequence.`,2:`'On the other hand' presents an opposing point.`,3:`'In contrast' compares two different things, not cause and effect.`},options:[`Even so`,`As a result`,`On the other hand`,`In contrast`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-188`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The instructions were ___ worded that nobody understood them.`,explanation:`'So' + adverb + that-clause expresses a result: He spoke so quickly that I missed it.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Such' is used before a noun phrase, not an adverb.`,2:`'Too' is followed by a to-infinitive, not a that-clause.`,3:`'Very' cannot introduce a that-clause of result.`},options:[`so badly`,`such badly`,`too badly`,`very badly`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-189`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The ancient manuscript ___ to have been written by a single monk.`,explanation:`Personal passive with reporting verbs: subject + is thought/said/believed + to-infinitive: He is thought to be rich.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'thinks' is active; a manuscript cannot think.`,1:`'has thought' is active and makes the manuscript the thinker.`,2:`The continuous active form gives the wrong meaning.`},options:[`thinks`,`has thought`,`is thinking`,`is thought`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-190`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Suppose you ___ the lottery tomorrow, what would you do first?`,explanation:`'Suppose' + past simple introduces an imaginary present or future situation: Suppose you lost your job?`,wrongReasons:{0:`With 'would' in the result, the hypothetical needs a past form.`,1:`'will' is not used after 'suppose' in this unreal meaning.`,3:`'had won' refers to the past, but the sentence says 'tomorrow'.`},options:[`win`,`will win`,`won`,`had won`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-191`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Our plans for a picnic went down the ___ when it started to pour.`,explanation:`'Go down the drain' means to be wasted or to fail completely: All our hard work went down the drain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Down the river' is literal and has no idiomatic meaning here.`,2:`'Go downhill' means to get worse gradually, but 'down the hill' is literal.`,3:`'Down the street' only describes a place.`},options:[`river`,`drain`,`hill`,`street`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-192`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The company had to ___ two hundred employees after sales collapsed.`,explanation:`'Lay off' means to dismiss workers because there is no work: The plant laid off 500 staff.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Lay down' means to establish rules or put something down.`,2:`'Lay out' means to arrange or spend money.`,3:`'Lay aside' means to save or ignore something for now.`},options:[`lay off`,`lay down`,`lay out`,`lay aside`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-193`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`We need to leave now ___ we'll miss the beginning of the concert.`,explanation:`'Or else' introduces what will happen if something isn't done: Hurry, or else you'll be late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'As though' compares something to an unreal situation.`,1:`'So that' expresses purpose, but nobody wants to miss the concert.`,2:`'In order to' must be followed by a base verb, not a clause.`},options:[`as though`,`so that`,`in order to`,`or else`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-194`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Prices have risen ___ over the last decade, and many families are struggling.`,explanation:`Adverbs like dramatically, sharply and steadily describe changes: Sales rose sharply.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Drama' is a noun.`,1:`'Dramatic' is an adjective; we would need 'a dramatic rise'.`,3:`'Dramatised' means turned into a play or film.`},options:[`drama`,`dramatic`,`dramatically`,`dramatised`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-195`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Applicants will be informed of the decision once their files ___.`,explanation:`After time words like 'once' or 'when', use a present tense, not 'will', even for the future; here passive present perfect: once the work has been done.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used after 'once' in a future time clause.`,2:`'have reviewed' is active; files cannot review anything.`,3:`'are reviewing' is active and makes the files the doers.`},options:[`will be reviewed`,`have been reviewed`,`have reviewed`,`are reviewing`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-196`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Far be it from me ___ you how to raise your children.`,explanation:`The fixed expression 'Far be it from me to' + infinitive means 'I don't want to criticise, but': Far be it from me to complain.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The fixed phrase takes a to-infinitive, not an -ing form.`,2:`'that I tell' does not fit this fixed expression.`,3:`A bare infinitive cannot follow 'from me' here.`},options:[`to tell`,`telling`,`that I tell`,`tell`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-197`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I'm not going to beat about the ___: your work this month has been unacceptable.`,explanation:`'Beat about the bush' means to avoid saying something directly: Stop beating about the bush and tell me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The idiom uses 'bush', not 'tree'.`,1:`'Field' does not appear in this idiom.`,2:`'Beat about the garden' is not an English idiom.`},options:[`tree`,`field`,`garden`,`bush`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-198`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I can't ___ his rudeness any longer; I'm going to complain.`,explanation:`'Put up with' means to tolerate something unpleasant: I can't put up with the noise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Catch up with' means to reach the same level as someone.`,1:`'Come up with' means to think of an idea or a plan.`,3:`'Keep up with' means to stay at the same speed or level.`},options:[`catch up with`,`come up with`,`put up with`,`keep up with`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-199`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ his age, my grandfather still swims in the sea every morning.`,explanation:`'Notwithstanding' is a formal word meaning 'despite' and takes a noun: Notwithstanding the cost, the plan went ahead.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Although' needs a clause, for example 'Although he is old'.`,2:`'Whereas' contrasts two clauses and cannot take a noun alone.`,3:`'Even' alone cannot introduce a noun phrase of contrast.`},options:[`Although`,`Notwithstanding`,`Whereas`,`Even`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-200`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The old cathedral was ___ destroyed by the earthquake; not a single wall remained standing.`,explanation:`An adverb modifies a past participle in a passive: The town was completely flooded.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Complete' is an adjective and cannot modify the verb 'destroyed'.`,2:`'Completion' is a noun.`,3:`'Completed' is a past participle and cannot modify another participle.`},options:[`completely`,`complete`,`completion`,`completed`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-201`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The match was called off because the pitch ___ unplayable by heavy rain.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect passive for an earlier action done to the subject: The road had been blocked by snow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'had rendered' is active; the pitch did not render anything unplayable.`,1:`Present perfect does not fit a past narrative.`,2:`'was rendering' is active and has the wrong meaning.`},options:[`had rendered`,`has rendered`,`was rendering`,`had been rendered`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-203`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`When the factory closed, hundreds of workers were left out in the ___.`,explanation:`'Left out in the cold' means ignored or excluded from a group or benefit: Smaller firms were left out in the cold.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Out in the rain' is literal, not idiomatic.`,2:`'In the air' means likely to happen soon, which is a different idiom.`,3:`'Out in the street' is about being homeless, not excluded.`},options:[`rain`,`cold`,`air`,`street`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-204`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The thieves were ___ by a shop assistant who recognised them from the news.`,explanation:`'Turn in' someone means to report them to the police: His own brother turned him in.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Turn off' means to stop a machine or to make someone lose interest.`,2:`'Turn away' means to refuse someone entry.`,3:`'Turn back' means to return the way you came.`},options:[`turned in`,`turned off`,`turned away`,`turned back`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-205`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`She trained every day for a year ___ she could compete in the national championships.`,explanation:`'So that' expresses purpose and is often followed by can/could: He saved money so that he could travel.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'As if' describes how something seems, not a purpose.`,1:`'In case' expresses a precaution, not a goal.`,2:`'Unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`as if`,`in case`,`unless`,`so that`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-206`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The film was ___ boring that half the audience left before the end.`,explanation:`'So' + adjective + that-clause expresses a result: It was so cold that the lake froze.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Too' is followed by a to-infinitive, not a that-clause.`,1:`'Such' is used with a noun: such a boring film.`,3:`'Enough' comes after the adjective and is followed by a to-infinitive.`},options:[`too`,`such`,`so`,`enough`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-207`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`There is no point in complaining; the decision has already ___.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive: has/have + been + past participle: The order has been sent.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has made' is active and needs an object.`,2:`'has been making' is active continuous; a decision does not make anything.`,3:`'has being made' is not a possible verb form.`},options:[`made`,`been made`,`been making`,`being made`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-208`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Whether he ___ guilty or innocent, he has the right to a fair trial.`,explanation:`In formal English, 'whether ... or' can take the subjunctive 'be': Whether it be true or false.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'being' is not a finite verb and cannot follow 'whether he'.`,2:`A to-infinitive cannot follow 'whether he' here.`,3:`'been' needs an auxiliary such as 'has'.`},options:[`be`,`being`,`to be`,`been`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-209`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Stop sitting on the ___ and tell us which candidate you support.`,explanation:`'Sit on the fence' means to avoid choosing a side: The senator is still sitting on the fence.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'On the wall' is not part of this idiom.`,1:`'Bench' has no idiomatic meaning in this phrase.`,2:`'Sitting on the chair' is literal.`},options:[`wall`,`bench`,`chair`,`fence`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-210`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Could you ___ the main arguments of the report in a few sentences?`,explanation:`'Sum up' means to give a short summary: To sum up, the plan is too expensive.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Make out' means to see or hear with difficulty.`,1:`'Add up' means to calculate a total or to make sense.`,3:`'Hold up' means to delay or to rob.`},options:[`make out`,`add up`,`sum up`,`hold up`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-211`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the new law was passed, crime in the city has fallen by a third.`,explanation:`'Since' + a past event is used with the present perfect to show a starting point: Since he moved here, he has made many friends.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'For' is used with a period of time, not with a clause.`,2:`'During' is followed by a noun, not a clause.`,3:`'While' describes something happening at the same time, which does not fit the present perfect here.`},options:[`For`,`Since`,`During`,`While`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-212`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Have you finished your homework ___?`,explanation:`'Yet' is used at the end of questions and negatives with the present perfect: Have you eaten yet?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Ago' is used with the past simple and a time period, not alone with the present perfect.`,2:`'Still' is used for continuing situations, not completion.`,3:`'Just' goes before the past participle, not at the end.`},options:[`yet`,`ago`,`still`,`just`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-213`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The singer insisted on ___ by her first name throughout the interview.`,explanation:`After a preposition, use a passive gerund (being + past participle) when the subject receives the action: She objected to being ignored.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'addressing' is active: she would be addressing someone else.`,1:`The base form cannot follow a preposition.`,2:`A preposition is followed by an -ing form, not a to-infinitive.`},options:[`addressing`,`address`,`to be addressed`,`being addressed`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-214`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If she ___ the job offer last year, she would be living in Canada now.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional uses past perfect in the if-clause and 'would' + verb for a present result: If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'accepted' refers to an unreal present, but the offer was 'last year'.`,1:`'would' is not used in the if-clause.`,3:`The present form gives a real condition, which clashes with 'would'.`},options:[`accepted`,`would accept`,`had accepted`,`accepts`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-215`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`My brother and I don't see eye to ___ on politics, so we avoid the subject.`,explanation:`'See eye to eye' means to agree with someone: We rarely see eye to eye on money.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Face to face' is a different idiom meaning in person.`,2:`'Eye to mind' is not an expression in English.`,3:`'Eye to head' does not exist as an idiom.`},options:[`face`,`eye`,`mind`,`head`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-216`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Fewer young people are ___ farming as a career these days.`,explanation:`'Take up' means to start a hobby, job or activity: He took up painting when he retired.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Take in' means to understand or to deceive someone.`,2:`'Take over' means to gain control of something.`,3:`'Take off' means to remove clothes or for a plane to leave the ground.`},options:[`taking up`,`taking in`,`taking over`,`taking off`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-217`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the reasons for the delay, the project must be finished by June.`,explanation:`'Whatever' + noun means 'it doesn't matter which': Whatever the weather, we'll go.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Whichever way' refers to manner or direction, not to reasons.`,1:`'However' + adjective or adverb is possible, but not + noun.`,2:`'Whenever' refers to time, not to reasons.`},options:[`Whichever way`,`However`,`Whenever`,`Whatever`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-218`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The new software is ___ easy to use, even for beginners.`,explanation:`An adverb intensifies an adjective: remarkably easy, surprisingly cheap.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Remark' is a noun or verb.`,1:`'Remarkable' is an adjective and cannot modify 'easy'.`,3:`'Remarked' is a past form of the verb.`},options:[`remark`,`remarkable`,`remarkably`,`remarked`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-219`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Visitors ___ not to feed the animals under any circumstances.`,explanation:`Present simple passive is common for rules and notices: Passengers are reminded to keep their bags with them.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'ask' is active and would mean visitors ask someone else.`,2:`'are asking' is active continuous and reverses the meaning.`,3:`'have asked' is active and makes the visitors the speakers.`},options:[`ask`,`are asked`,`are asking`,`have asked`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-220`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`God ___ the Queen!`,explanation:`Some fixed expressions keep the old subjunctive base form: Long live the king; God save the Queen.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The -s form is not used in this fixed subjunctive expression.`,2:`The past form changes it into a statement about the past.`,3:`The continuous form does not exist in this fixed expression.`},options:[`save`,`saves`,`saved`,`is saving`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-221`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The company is in hot ___ after customers' data was leaked online.`,explanation:`'In hot water' means in trouble: He got into hot water for missing the meeting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Hot oil' has no idiomatic meaning here.`,1:`'In hot fire' is not an idiom; 'under fire' means criticised.`,2:`'In the soup' is old-fashioned, and 'hot soup' is not the idiom.`},options:[`oil`,`fire`,`soup`,`water`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-222`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The police are ___ the cause of the explosion at the chemical plant.`,explanation:`'Look into' means to investigate: The manager promised to look into my complaint.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Look forward to' means to wait for something with pleasure.`,1:`'Look up to' means to admire and respect someone.`,3:`'Look down on' means to think you are better than someone.`},options:[`looking forward to`,`looking up to`,`looking into`,`looking down on`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-223`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`He apologised; ___, I'm still angry about what he said.`,explanation:`'All the same' means 'despite this': It's an old car; all the same, I love it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Therefore' shows a result, but being angry is not a result of an apology.`,2:`'In addition' adds a similar point, not a contrast.`,3:`'Accordingly' means 'as a result' and does not show contrast.`},options:[`therefore`,`all the same`,`in addition`,`accordingly`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-224`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`He arrived ___ late that the ceremony had already finished.`,explanation:`'So' + adverb + that-clause expresses a result: She ran so fast that she won.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Too' is followed by a to-infinitive, not a that-clause.`,2:`'Very' cannot introduce a result clause.`,3:`'Such' is used before nouns, not adverbs.`},options:[`so`,`too`,`very`,`such`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-225`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The road through the valley ___ for months, so drivers had to take a long detour.`,explanation:`Past continuous passive (was/were being + past participle) describes an action in progress on the subject in the past: The house was being painted.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'had resurfaced' is active and also loses the idea of duration.`,1:`'was resurfacing' is active; a road cannot resurface itself.`,2:`'resurfaced' is active past simple and needs a doer.`},options:[`had resurfaced`,`was resurfacing`,`resurfaced`,`was being resurfaced`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-226`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It's about time you ___ responsibility for your own mistakes.`,explanation:`'It's about time' + subject + past simple criticises something that should already have happened: It's about time he apologised.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The base form is not used after 'it's about time you'.`,1:`An -ing form cannot follow 'you' here without an auxiliary.`,3:`'will' does not follow 'it's about time'.`},options:[`take`,`taking`,`took`,`will take`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-227`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Learning the basics of coding is one thing, but building an app is a whole different ___.`,explanation:`'A different kettle of fish' means a completely different situation: Teaching adults is a different kettle of fish.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'A can of worms' is a situation full of new problems, used with 'open', not 'a whole different'.`,2:`'Not my cup of tea' means something you don't like.`,3:`'A piece of cake' means easy, which does not fit the contrast.`},options:[`can of worms`,`kettle of fish`,`cup of tea`,`piece of cake`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-228`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`When the main speaker fell ill, her assistant ___ for her at the conference.`,explanation:`'Stand in for' means to replace someone temporarily: I stood in for my boss at the meeting.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Stand off' is not followed by 'for'; a 'stand-off' is a situation where neither side acts.`,2:`'Stand out' means to be easy to notice.`,3:`'Stand by' means to support someone or be ready to act, and is not used with 'for'.`},options:[`stood in`,`stood off`,`stood out`,`stood by`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-229`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The museum is closed on Mondays, ___ we'll have to visit it on Tuesday.`,explanation:`'So' introduces a result in the second clause: It was cold, so I wore a coat.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because' gives a reason, which reverses the logic.`,1:`'But' introduces a contrast, not a result.`,2:`'Although' introduces a contrast, which does not fit here.`},options:[`because`,`but`,`although`,`so`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-230`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The village is ___ accessible by boat during the winter months.`,explanation:`'Only' as an adverb limits a phrase: The island is only reachable by ferry.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Lonely' is an adjective describing sad feelings.`,1:`'Alone' means without other people and cannot modify 'accessible'.`,3:`'Single' is an adjective meaning one.`},options:[`lonely`,`alone`,`only`,`single`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-231`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Neither of the two reports ___ by the committee so far.`,explanation:`After 'neither of' use a singular verb in formal English; here present perfect passive: Neither of the plans has been approved.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'have discussed' is active and also plural.`,2:`'has discussed' is active; reports cannot discuss anything.`,3:`'is discussing' is active continuous with the wrong meaning.`},options:[`have discussed`,`has been discussed`,`has discussed`,`is discussing`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-232`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The committee proposed that the meeting ___ until all members could attend.`,explanation:`After 'propose that', formal English uses the base form, here passive: We propose that the rule be changed.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'is postponed' is indicative, not the formal subjunctive.`,2:`'postpones' is active; a meeting cannot postpone anything.`,3:`'will postpone' is active and uses 'will' in a mandative clause.`},options:[`be postponed`,`is postponed`,`postpones`,`will postpone`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-233`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The scientist's theory was ahead of its ___, so few colleagues took it seriously.`,explanation:`'Ahead of its time' means too modern to be understood or accepted then: Her designs were ahead of their time.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Ahead of its age' is not the fixed expression.`,1:`'Period' does not form this idiom.`,2:`'Ahead of its days' is not idiomatic.`},options:[`age`,`period`,`days`,`time`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-234`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The government has promised to ___ on tax evasion by large corporations.`,explanation:`'Crack down on' means to deal with something illegal more strictly: Police are cracking down on speeding.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Break off' means to end a relationship or stop talking suddenly.`,1:`'Crack up' means to laugh a lot or to have a breakdown.`,3:`'Cut back on' means to reduce spending, not to act strictly against crime.`},options:[`break off`,`crack up`,`crack down`,`cut back`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-235`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the risks involved, very few investors were interested in the project.`,explanation:`'Given' + noun means 'considering': Given his experience, he should get the job.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Unless' needs a clause and introduces a condition.`,2:`'Although' needs a clause and expresses contrast.`,3:`'Whereas' contrasts two clauses and cannot take a noun phrase.`},options:[`Unless`,`Given`,`Although`,`Whereas`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-236`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The manager spoke ___ to the staff about the need for changes.`,explanation:`Use an adverb after a verb to describe how something is done: She spoke frankly.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Frank' is an adjective and cannot describe the verb 'spoke'.`,2:`'Frankness' is a noun.`,3:`'Franker' is a comparative adjective.`},options:[`frankly`,`frank`,`frankness`,`franker`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-237`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The old factory is going ___ into a block of luxury flats.`,explanation:`'Going to' + be + past participle is the passive future with 'going to': The park is going to be closed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to convert' is active; the factory is not converting anything.`,1:`'going be' is missing 'to' and is active.`,2:`'going' needs 'to' + verb, not a bare participle.`},options:[`to convert`,`be converting`,`converted`,`to be converted`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-238`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Would you rather I ___ you a lift to the station?`,explanation:`'Would rather' + another subject + past simple refers to the present or future: Would you rather I left?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The base form is not used after 'would you rather I'.`,1:`'would' is not repeated after 'would rather I'.`,3:`'had given' refers to the past, but the offer is about now.`},options:[`give`,`would give`,`gave`,`had given`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-239`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Getting a meeting with the director is like getting blood out of a ___.`,explanation:`'Like getting blood out of a stone' means very difficult to get something from someone: Getting a refund was like getting blood out of a stone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The idiom uses 'stone', not 'rock'.`,2:`'Brick' is never used in this idiom.`,3:`'Out of a wall' is not the fixed form.`},options:[`rock`,`stone`,`brick`,`wall`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-240`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The fire alarm ___ in the middle of the night and woke everyone up.`,explanation:`'Go off' (of an alarm or bomb) means to ring or explode: My alarm goes off at six.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Go out' means to stop burning or to leave home for fun.`,2:`'Go on' means to continue or to happen.`,3:`'Go over' means to check or review something.`},options:[`went off`,`went out`,`went on`,`went over`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-241`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The festival will take place ___ the weather is good or bad.`,explanation:`'Whether ... or' means it doesn't matter which of two possibilities happens: We'll go whether you like it or not.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Either' needs 'or' but cannot introduce a clause like this.`,1:`'If ... or' cannot introduce two alternatives in this way.`,2:`'Unless' means 'if not' and does not fit with 'or'.`},options:[`either`,`if`,`unless`,`whether`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-242`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Mark is ___ the most talented player in the team.`,explanation:`'By far' strengthens a superlative: She is by far the best student.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Much more' is used with comparatives, not superlatives.`,1:`'Very' cannot modify 'the most' + adjective in this way.`,3:`'Too' means more than needed and cannot modify a superlative.`},options:[`much more`,`very`,`by far`,`too`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-243`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The painting ___ to be a fake, so the museum quietly removed it from display.`,explanation:`Personal passive: subject + was found/shown/proved + to-infinitive: The claim was shown to be false.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'found' is active; the painting did not find anything.`,2:`'was finding' is active continuous and gives no sense here.`,3:`'had found' is active past perfect, again with the wrong doer.`},options:[`found`,`was found`,`was finding`,`had found`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-245`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I can't make ___ or tail of these instructions; they're completely confusing.`,explanation:`'Can't make head or tail of something' means you cannot understand it at all: I can't make head or tail of this map.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Front or tail' does not exist as an idiom.`,1:`'Top or tail' is used for preparing vegetables, not for understanding.`,2:`'Face or tail' is not an idiom.`},options:[`front`,`top`,`face`,`head`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-246`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Unless we ___ costs, we will have to close two of our branches.`,explanation:`'Cut down on' means to reduce the amount of something: I'm trying to cut down on coffee.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Cut off' means to disconnect or isolate.`,1:`'Cut in on' means to interrupt a conversation or a dance.`,3:`'Cut out' means to remove completely or to stop working, as an engine.`},options:[`cut off`,`cut in on`,`cut down on`,`cut out`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-247`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The scheme proved popular; ___, it was extended to three other cities.`,explanation:`'Consequently' introduces a result: The road was icy; consequently, there were many accidents.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Nevertheless' introduces a contrast, but extending a popular scheme is a natural result.`,2:`'On the contrary' contradicts the previous statement.`,3:`'Meanwhile' refers to something happening at the same time.`},options:[`nevertheless`,`consequently`,`on the contrary`,`meanwhile`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-248`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The patient's condition has improved ___, so she can go home tomorrow.`,explanation:`Use an adverb after a verb to say how much something changed: Her health has improved greatly.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Significant' is an adjective and cannot describe the verb.`,2:`'Significance' is a noun.`,3:`'Signify' is a verb meaning to mean or show.`},options:[`significantly`,`significant`,`significance`,`signify`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-249`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The athlete denied ___ any money to lose the race.`,explanation:`'Deny' is followed by an -ing form; use 'having been' + past participle for an earlier passive action: He denied having been paid.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'offering' is active: it would mean he offered money to someone.`,1:`'deny' is not followed by a to-infinitive.`,2:`'deny' does not take a to-infinitive, and this form is also active.`},options:[`offering`,`to be offered`,`to have offered`,`having been offered`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-250`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Were the company ___ its prices, many loyal customers would leave.`,explanation:`'Were + subject + to-infinitive' is a formal inverted conditional for unlikely situations: Were he to resign, there would be chaos.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The base form cannot follow 'Were the company'.`,1:`A past form cannot follow 'Were the company'; this inverted structure needs 'to' + base verb.`,3:`This inverted structure needs a to-infinitive, not an -ing form.`},options:[`raise`,`raised`,`to raise`,`raising`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-251`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Since the scandal, the senator has been keeping a low ___ and avoiding journalists.`,explanation:`'Keep a low profile' means to avoid attracting attention: After the trial, she kept a low profile.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Keep a low level' is not an idiom.`,2:`'Keep a low face' does not exist; 'keep a straight face' is different.`,3:`'Keep a low voice' only means speaking quietly.`},options:[`level`,`profile`,`face`,`voice`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-252`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The children's tricks didn't fool their teacher; she ___ them immediately.`,explanation:`'See through' someone means to recognise their trick or lie: I saw through his excuse at once.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'See off' means to say goodbye to someone at a station or airport.`,2:`'See to' means to deal with or take care of something.`,3:`'See out' means to accompany someone to the door or last until the end.`},options:[`saw through`,`saw off`,`saw to`,`saw out`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-253`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the book is well written, its argument is not very convincing.`,explanation:`'While' can mean 'although' at the start of a sentence: While I understand your point, I disagree.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because' gives a cause, but the two clauses contrast.`,1:`'Since' gives a reason or a starting time, not a contrast.`,2:`'As' gives a reason, which does not fit the contrast.`},options:[`Because`,`Since`,`As`,`While`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-254`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The two brothers look ___ alike; most people can't tell them apart.`,explanation:`An adverb can intensify 'alike': They look remarkably alike.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Strike' is a noun or verb.`,1:`'Striking' is an adjective and cannot modify 'alike'.`,3:`'Struck' is the past form of the verb 'strike'.`},options:[`strike`,`striking`,`strikingly`,`struck`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-255`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Not a single complaint ___ since the new manager took over.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect passive with 'since' for a period up to now: No changes have been made since May.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has received' is active; a complaint cannot receive anything.`,2:`Past continuous does not fit a period that continues up to now.`,3:`Present simple does not fit with 'since'.`},options:[`has received`,`has been received`,`was receiving`,`is received`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-256`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If he weren't so stubborn, he ___ the argument yesterday.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional with a present cause and a past result uses 'would have' + past participle: If I were braver, I would have spoken up.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'wouldn't lose' refers to the present, but the argument was yesterday.`,2:`The past simple is not used in the result clause.`,3:`The past perfect belongs in the if-clause, not the result.`},options:[`wouldn't have lost`,`wouldn't lose`,`didn't lose`,`hadn't lost`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-257`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The final decision is up in the ___ until the investors meet next week.`,explanation:`'Up in the air' means not decided yet: Our holiday plans are still up in the air.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Up in the sky' is literal.`,1:`'Up in the wind' is not an idiom.`,2:`'Head in the clouds' means daydreaming, which is a different idiom.`},options:[`sky`,`wind`,`clouds`,`air`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-258`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`After years of hard work, the small start-up finally ___ and became a major brand.`,explanation:`'Take off' can mean to suddenly become successful: Her career took off after the film.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Take after' means to resemble an older relative.`,1:`'Take back' means to return something or withdraw a statement.`,3:`'Take apart' means to separate something into pieces.`},options:[`took after`,`took back`,`took off`,`took apart`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-259`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`You won't get a refund ___ you have the original receipt.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'except if': You can't enter unless you have a ticket.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'If' reverses the meaning: no refund if you have the receipt.`,2:`'Provided' reverses the meaning in the same way as 'if'.`,3:`'As long as' also means 'only if', which gives the wrong meaning with 'won't'.`},options:[`if`,`unless`,`provided`,`as long as`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-260`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adverbs`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`I ___ remember locking the door, but it was open when I got home.`,explanation:`'Distinctly' collocates with 'remember' to mean clearly: I distinctly remember him saying that.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Distinct' is an adjective and cannot modify the verb.`,2:`'Distinction' is a noun.`,3:`'Distinctive' is an adjective meaning easy to recognise.`},options:[`distinctly`,`distinct`,`distinction`,`distinctive`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-261`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The children were made ___ the classroom before they were allowed to go home.`,explanation:`In the active, 'make' takes a bare infinitive, but in the passive it takes a to-infinitive: They were made to wait.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The bare infinitive is used only in the active: they made them tidy.`,1:`'were made' is not followed by an -ing form.`,2:`'tidied' would wrongly suggest the children themselves were tidied.`},options:[`tidy`,`tidying`,`tidied`,`to tidy`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-262`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Heaven ___ that anything should happen to the children while we're away!`,explanation:`'Heaven forbid' is a fixed subjunctive expression meaning 'I really hope not': Heaven forbid that it rains on the wedding day.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past form does not fit this fixed wish.`,1:`The fixed expression keeps the base form, without -s.`,3:`An -ing form cannot be the main verb here.`},options:[`forbade`,`forbids`,`forbid`,`forbidding`],correct:2},{id:`c1-mc-263`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`idioms`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The new intern really knows the ___; she's been here a week and already understands everything.`,explanation:`'Know the ropes' means to understand how something is done: It takes a month to learn the ropes here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Pull strings' is a different idiom about using influence.`,2:`'Know the lines' refers only to an actor's script.`,3:`'Wires' does not form this idiom.`},options:[`strings`,`ropes`,`lines`,`wires`],correct:1},{id:`c1-mc-264`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Please ___ the form carefully and return it to reception.`,explanation:`'Fill in' means to complete a form with information: Fill in your name and address.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Fill up' means to make a container completely full.`,2:`'Fill out' exists, but 'fill out on' is not a phrasal verb.`,3:`'Fill over' is not a phrasal verb in English.`},options:[`fill in`,`fill up`,`fill out on`,`fill over`],correct:0},{id:`c1-mc-265`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The design is simple, ___ it is extremely effective.`,explanation:`'Yet' introduces a surprising contrast: The car is small, yet it is very comfortable.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Besides' adds another point rather than contrasting.`,1:`'Despite' cannot be followed by a clause with a subject and verb.`,2:`'Hence' means 'for this reason', which does not show contrast.`},options:[`besides`,`despite`,`hence`,`yet`],correct:3},{id:`c1-mc-266`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The news came as a bolt from the ___, since no one had expected the company to close.`,explanation:`"A bolt from the blue" means a complete surprise.`,options:[`blue`,`cloud`,`storm`,`sky`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Bolt from the sky" is not the standard form.`,1:`"Bolt from the cloud" is not the idiom.`,2:`"Bolt from the storm" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-267`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The little girl clung ___ her mother's hand as they crossed the road.`,explanation:`"Cling to" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`at`,`to`,`by`,`on`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Cling at" is not standard.`,3:`"Cling on" is less natural here.`,2:`"Cling by" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-268`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She ___ her degree for only a few months when she realised she had chosen the wrong subject.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration up to a past moment.`,options:[`pursued`,`had been pursuing`,`was pursuing`,`has been pursuing`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before the realisation.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit past events.`,0:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-269`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There was ___ little enthusiasm for the proposal that it was quietly withdrawn.`,explanation:`"So little ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`too`,`very`,`such`,`so`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Such little enthusiasm" is awkward.`,1:`"Very little ... that" needs "so".`,0:`"Too" does not introduce a result with "that".`}},{id:`c1-mc-270`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After years of hard work, she finally achieved her ___ of becoming a doctor.`,explanation:`An "ambition" is a strong desire to achieve.`,options:[`ambition`,`ambitious`,`ambiguity`,`amateur`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Ambitious" is an adjective.`,2:`"Ambiguity" means unclear meaning.`,3:`An "amateur" is a nonprofessional.`}},{id:`c1-mc-271`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`He has two sons, neither of ___ shows any interest in the family business.`,explanation:`"Neither of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`who`,`which`,`whom`,`them`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Which" refers to things.`,0:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,3:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-272`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I'm sure she ___ the phone ringing; she was in the garden.`,explanation:`"Can't have" + participle expresses a negative deduction.`,options:[`couldn't hear`,`can't have heard`,`needn't have heard`,`mustn't have heard`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Mustn't have" is not used for deductions.`,0:`This does not express a deduction about the past.`,2:`"Needn't have" says something was unnecessary.`}},{id:`c1-mc-273`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Whatever the outcome, we ___ be grateful for the opportunity to take part.`,explanation:`"Shall" in a first-person statement expresses firm intention.`,options:[`can`,`should`,`shall`,`might`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Should" would suggest obligation rather than determination.`,3:`"Might" would weaken the commitment.`,0:`"Can" does not express firm intention.`}},{id:`c1-mc-274`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She had a ___ feeling that something was about to go wrong.`,explanation:`"Nagging" means persistently worrying.`,options:[`nagged`,`nagging`,`nags`,`nag`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Nagged" is a past tense.`,3:`"Nag" is a verb or noun.`,2:`"Nags" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-275`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The patient's condition is ___ and doctors cannot yet predict how he will respond to treatment.`,explanation:`"Unpredictable" means impossible to forecast.`,options:[`unpredicted`,`unpredict`,`unpredictable`,`unpredictably`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Unpredicted" means not foreseen.`,3:`"Unpredictably" is an adverb.`,1:`This word does not exist.`}},{id:`c1-mc-276`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`As soon as the minister ___ the room, the journalists began shouting questions.`,explanation:`The simple past reports events in sequence.`,options:[`was entering`,`had entered`,`entered`,`has entered`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A simple sequence of events uses the simple past.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,0:`The continuous does not report a completed action.`}},{id:`c1-mc-277`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The twins are so ___ that even their parents confuse them.`,explanation:`"Alike" means very similar.`,options:[`like`,`alike`,`likely`,`liked`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Like" needs an object.`,3:`"Liked" is a participle.`,2:`"Likely" means probable.`}},{id:`c1-mc-278`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The committee's findings ___ serious doubts about the safety of the product.`,explanation:`"Cast doubt on" is the fixed collocation.`,options:[`cast`,`gave`,`threw`,`put`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Threw doubts" is not the collocation.`,1:`"Gave doubts" is not the collocation.`,3:`"Put doubts" is not the collocation.`}},{id:`c1-mc-279`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The region has been ___ by a series of devastating floods in recent years.`,explanation:`"Ravaged" means severely damaged.`,options:[`ravished`,`rampaged`,`ravenous`,`ravaged`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Ravenous" means extremely hungry.`,0:`"Ravished" has a different meaning.`,1:`"Rampaged" means behaved wildly.`}},{id:`c1-mc-280`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The report highlights the need for greater ___ in the way public money is spent.`,explanation:`"Transparency" means openness about decisions.`,options:[`transaction`,`transparence`,`transference`,`transparency`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Transference" is a psychological or legal term.`,1:`This form is rare and nonstandard.`,0:`"Transaction" means a deal.`}},{id:`c1-mc-281`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The delegates, ___ were tired after the long journey, were shown to their rooms.`,explanation:`"Most of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`most of whom`,`most of which`,`most of them`,`most of who`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Them" cannot join the clauses.`,3:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-mc-282`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She said she ___ him since the day he left the company.`,explanation:`The present perfect shifts to the past perfect in reported speech.`,options:[`had not seen`,`would not see`,`did not see`,`has not seen`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`Reported speech shifts the present perfect back.`,2:`"Since" calls for a perfect form.`,1:`"Would" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-283`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The speaker ___ his argument with several striking examples.`,explanation:`"Illustrated" means showed clearly.`,options:[`illustrious`,`illuminated`,`illusory`,`illustrated`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Illuminated" means lit up.`,0:`"Illustrious" means famous.`,2:`"Illusory" means unreal.`}},{id:`c1-mc-284`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The moment ___ the news broke, the shares plummeted.`,explanation:`"The moment that" is a standard time conjunction.`,options:[`that`,`when that`,`which`,`what`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Which" would need a preposition.`,1:`"When that" is redundant.`,3:`"What" cannot follow a noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-285`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I have ___ doubt that the committee will approve the plan.`,explanation:`"No doubt" means not any doubt.`,options:[`no`,`not`,`any`,`none`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Not doubt" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"None doubt" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"I have any doubt" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-286`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The report, ___ authors were all independent experts, criticised the government's approach.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`which`,`whom`,`who`,`whose`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Which authors" does not show possession.`,1:`"Whom authors" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Who authors" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-287`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The island can be reached by a ___ ferry that runs only twice a week in winter.`,explanation:`"Irregular" means not at fixed intervals.`,options:[`irregularly`,`irregular`,`regular`,`irregularity`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`An adjective is needed.`,3:`"Irregularity" is a noun.`,2:`"Regular" means the opposite.`}},{id:`c1-mc-288`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I'm afraid I have ___ choice but to accept their terms.`,explanation:`"No choice but" is a fixed expression.`,options:[`none`,`not`,`any`,`no`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Not choice" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"None choice" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Have any choice but" does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-289`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`He was in two ___ about whether to accept the job in another city.`,explanation:`"In two minds" means undecided.`,options:[`thoughts`,`ways`,`minds`,`heads`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"In two thoughts" is not the idiom.`,1:`"In two ways" has a different meaning.`,3:`"In two heads" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-290`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The new law will affect ___ one of us, regardless of age or income.`,explanation:`"Every one of us" emphasises that no individual is excluded.`,options:[`every`,`whole`,`each of`,`all`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"All one of us" is not grammatical.`,2:`"Each of one" duplicates the quantifier.`,1:`"Whole one of us" is not grammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-291`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The new law will mainly affect ___ elderly and the disabled.`,explanation:`"The" + adjective names a group of people.`,options:[`no article`,`some`,`an`,`the`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"An elderly" is not used as a group noun.`,1:`"Some elderly and the disabled" is unbalanced.`,0:`A group noun needs the definite article.`}},{id:`c1-mc-292`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The jury found the evidence ___ and acquitted the defendant.`,explanation:`"Inconclusive" means not decisive.`,options:[`inconclude`,`inconclusively`,`inconclusive`,`inclusion`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`An adjective is needed after "found ... ".`,0:`This word does not exist.`,3:`"Inclusion" is a noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-293`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The new rules apply to ___ employees, regardless of their position.`,explanation:`"All" goes with plural nouns.`,options:[`each`,`every`,`whole`,`all`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Every employees" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Each employees" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Whole employees" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-294`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She ___ for the company for over thirty years when she was suddenly made redundant.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`was working`,`has been working`,`worked`,`had been working`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before the event.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-295`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`It was ___ a remarkable performance that the audience gave a standing ovation.`,explanation:`"Such a + adjective + noun" is followed by a result clause.`,options:[`very`,`such`,`quite so`,`so`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"So a remarkable performance" is not the standard order.`,2:`"Quite so" does not fit here.`,0:`"Very" cannot combine with "a remarkable performance" like this.`}},{id:`c1-mc-296`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There was ___ of the early enthusiasm left by the time the project entered its third year.`,explanation:`"Little" with an uncountable noun means almost none.`,options:[`few`,`little`,`a few`,`many`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Enthusiasm" is uncountable, so "few" cannot be used.`,2:`"A few" goes with countable nouns.`,3:`"Many" goes with countable nouns.`}},{id:`c1-mc-297`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`It took me ages to ___ the courage to ask for a pay rise.`,explanation:`"Summon up courage" means to gather it.`,options:[`summons`,`summary`,`summit`,`summon`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`A "summit" is a mountain top.`,1:`A "summary" is a short account.`,0:`"Summons" is a legal order.`}},{id:`c1-mc-298`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`They live in a sprawling house on the ___ of the city, surrounded by woods.`,explanation:`The "outskirts" are the outer parts of a town.`,options:[`outcomes`,`outlines`,`outlets`,`outskirts`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Outlines" are shapes or summaries.`,2:`"Outlets" are exits or shops.`,0:`"Outcomes" are results.`}},{id:`c1-mc-299`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The village has managed to ___ its traditional character despite the arrival of mass tourism.`,explanation:`"Retain" means to keep.`,options:[`retire`,`retain`,`restrain`,`retort`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Restrain" means to hold back.`,3:`"Retort" means to reply sharply.`,0:`"Retire" means to stop working.`}},{id:`c1-mc-300`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The mountain village has ___ views of the valley and the snow-capped peaks.`,explanation:`"Breathtaking" means astonishingly beautiful.`,options:[`breathing`,`breathy`,`breathless`,`breathtaking`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Breathless" means out of breath.`,0:`"Breathing" is a participle.`,1:`"Breathy" describes a voice.`}},{id:`c1-mc-301`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The committee was set up to ___ allegations of misconduct by police officers.`,explanation:`"Investigate" means to examine.`,options:[`investment`,`investigator`,`investigated`,`investigate`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`After "to" use the base form.`,0:`"Investment" is a noun.`,1:`An "investigator" is a person.`}},{id:`c1-mc-302`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`If the committee ___ the proposal, the company would have gone bankrupt within months.`,explanation:`The if-clause uses the past perfect.`,options:[`would not approve`,`has not approved`,`did not approve`,`had not approved`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,0:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-303`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Staff were asked to ___ their working practices to the new digital system.`,explanation:`"Adapt to" means to adjust to.`,options:[`adjoin`,`adapt`,`adopt`,`adept`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Adopt" means to take up.`,3:`"Adept" is an adjective.`,0:`"Adjoin" means to be next to.`}},{id:`c1-mc-304`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She has an extraordinary ___ for spotting talent before anyone else notices it.`,explanation:`"An eye for" means a talent for noticing.`,options:[`ear`,`hand`,`eye`,`nose`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"An ear for" is used with music or languages.`,3:`"A nose for" is used with scandal or trouble.`,1:`"A hand for" is not the usual collocation.`}},{id:`c1-mc-305`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Not for a moment ___ that the project would fail.`,explanation:`"Not for a moment" requires inversion with "did".`,options:[`she did imagine`,`imagined she`,`did she imagine`,`she imagined`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Not for a moment" at the start requires inversion.`,0:`The word order is not inverted.`,1:`The auxiliary is missing.`}},{id:`c1-mc-306`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`It was ___ fascinating exhibition that we stayed until the gallery closed.`,explanation:`"Such a + adjective + noun + that" shows a result.`,options:[`very`,`such a`,`so a`,`quite`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"So a" is not the standard order.`,3:`"Quite fascinating exhibition" lacks an article.`,0:`"Very" cannot introduce a result.`}},{id:`c1-mc-307`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The accident ___ have been avoided if the driver had been paying attention.`,explanation:`"Could have" expresses a missed possibility.`,options:[`may`,`would`,`could`,`might to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"May have been avoided" does not fit the unreal condition.`,1:`"Would have been avoided" lacks the sense of possibility.`,3:`"Might to" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-308`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The patient ___ for several hours before the doctor finally arrived.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`had been waiting`,`was waiting`,`waited`,`has been waiting`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before arrival.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-309`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This is the first time that I ___ such a magnificent view.`,explanation:`"The first time" takes the present perfect.`,options:[`have seen`,`had seen`,`saw`,`see`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"The first time" takes the present perfect.`,1:`The past perfect needs a past reference.`,3:`A present simple does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-310`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`He ___ to have left the building by now; his car is no longer in the car park.`,explanation:`"Ought to have" expresses a reasonable expectation or deduction.`,options:[`should to`,`must`,`would`,`ought`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Should" is not followed by "to".`,1:`"Must to have" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Would to have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-311`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`They stayed in a small hotel ___ the sea, with views of the harbour.`,explanation:`"Overlooking" means having a view of.`,options:[`overlooking`,`overseeing`,`oversleeping`,`overtaking`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Overseeing" means supervising.`,2:`"Oversleeping" means sleeping too long.`,3:`"Overtaking" means passing.`}},{id:`c1-mc-312`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`You ___ me by now if you had really wanted to speak to me.`,explanation:`The third conditional result uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`would have called`,`would call`,`will have called`,`had called`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`This refers to the present.`,2:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`,3:`The result clause needs a modal.`}},{id:`c1-mc-313`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The merger is expected to create significant ___, as duplicated departments are combined.`,explanation:`"Synergies" are benefits from combining.`,options:[`syntheses`,`synonyms`,`syndromes`,`synergies`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Synonyms" are words with similar meanings.`,2:`"Syndromes" are medical conditions.`,0:`"Syntheses" are combinations of ideas.`}},{id:`c1-mc-314`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The company has ___ employees than it had five years ago.`,explanation:`"Fewer" goes with countable plural nouns.`,options:[`lesser`,`little`,`less`,`fewer`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Employees" is countable.`,1:`"Little" cannot form a comparison.`,0:`"Lesser" does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-315`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The government has been ___ in its refusal to negotiate with the strikers.`,explanation:`"Adamant" means refusing to change one's mind.`,options:[`amenable`,`alleged`,`adamant`,`ambiguous`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Amenable" means willing to accept.`,3:`"Ambiguous" means unclear.`,1:`"Alleged" means claimed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-316`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The village has remained largely ___ by tourism.`,explanation:`"Unspoilt" means not damaged.`,options:[`unspoilt`,`unspent`,`unspotted`,`unspoken`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Unspoken" means not said.`,1:`"Unspent" means not used.`,2:`"Unspotted" means not seen.`}},{id:`c1-mc-317`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company has been ___ pressure to reduce its carbon footprint.`,explanation:`"Under pressure" is the fixed collocation.`,options:[`on`,`under`,`in`,`at`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"On pressure" is not standard.`,2:`"In pressure" is not standard.`,3:`"At pressure" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-318`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`If you ___ be so kind as to wait a moment, the director will see you shortly.`,explanation:`"Would be so kind as to" is a formal polite request.`,options:[`should`,`will to`,`might`,`would`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Will to" is not a grammatical combination.`,0:`"Should be so kind" does not make a polite request.`,2:`"Might be so kind" reads as speculation, not a request.`}},{id:`c1-mc-319`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ have been more than twenty when she wrote her first novel.`,explanation:`"Can't have" expresses a negative deduction.`,options:[`shouldn't`,`can't`,`mustn't`,`needn't`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Needn't have been" says it was unnecessary.`,2:`"Mustn't have" is not used for deductions.`,0:`"Shouldn't have" expresses criticism.`}},{id:`c1-mc-320`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The prisoner is believed ___ escaped through a window during the night.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive refers to an earlier time.`,options:[`has`,`to have`,`to has`,`having`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"To" takes the base form.`,3:`A participle does not follow "believed".`,0:`A finite verb does not follow "believed".`}},{id:`c1-mc-321`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ of the managers has the authority to approve expenditure on this scale.`,explanation:`"None of" refers to three or more.`,options:[`None`,`No`,`Any`,`Neither`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Neither" is used for two.`,2:`"Any of the managers has" lacks a negative sense.`,1:`"No of" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-322`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After the argument, an awkward ___ hung in the air and nobody knew what to say.`,explanation:`"Silence" is the noun.`,options:[`silent`,`silenced`,`silently`,`silence`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Silent" is an adjective.`,2:`"Silently" is an adverb.`,1:`"Silenced" is a participle.`}},{id:`c1-mc-323`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The museum's collection ___ some of the finest examples of medieval manuscripts in Europe.`,explanation:`The present simple states a fact about the collection.`,options:[`includes`,`included`,`including`,`include`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Collection" is singular.`,2:`An auxiliary is missing.`,1:`The present is the time of reference.`}},{id:`c1-mc-324`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I find it hard to ___ with people who are constantly negative about everything.`,explanation:`"Empathise with" means to understand another's feelings.`,options:[`embark`,`empathise`,`empower`,`emphasise`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Emphasise" means to stress.`,2:`"Empower" means to give authority.`,0:`"Embark" means to begin a journey.`}},{id:`c1-mc-325`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two reports differ ___ their assessment of the risks involved.`,explanation:`"Differ in" introduces the respect in which things are different.`,options:[`at`,`by`,`on`,`in`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Differ on" would introduce the topic of disagreement.`,0:`"Differ at" is not standard.`,1:`"Differ by" introduces an amount.`}},{id:`c1-mc-326`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The film received ___ critical acclaim and several awards.`,explanation:`"Considerable" means large in amount.`,options:[`considerably`,`considerable`,`considerate`,`consideration`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Considerate" means thoughtful.`,0:`An adverb cannot modify a noun.`,3:`"Consideration" is a noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-327`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She is renowned ___ her ability to explain complex ideas simply.`,explanation:`"Renowned for" introduces the reason for fame.`,options:[`for`,`by`,`with`,`of`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Renowned of" is not standard.`,1:`"Renowned by" is not standard.`,2:`"Renowned with" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-328`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Next summer, the bridge ___ for a full century.`,explanation:`The future perfect gives a total up to a future point.`,options:[`will stand`,`has stood`,`stands`,`will have stood`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`A present form does not give a future total.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a future point.`,0:`The future simple does not show the total duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-329`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She could not remember ___ she had left the documents, which made her increasingly anxious.`,explanation:`"Where" introduces an embedded question about place.`,options:[`which`,`what`,`where`,`when`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Which" needs an antecedent.`,1:`"What" does not fit a question about place.`,3:`"When" asks about time.`}},{id:`c1-mc-330`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She has a remarkable aptitude ___ languages and picks up new ones with ease.`,explanation:`"Aptitude for" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`to`,`in`,`for`,`of`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Aptitude in" is rare.`,3:`"Aptitude of" is not standard.`,0:`"Aptitude to" is less common.`}},{id:`c1-mc-331`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`They spent a week ___ the coast in a converted fishing boat.`,explanation:`"Cruise along" means to travel at a leisurely pace.`,options:[`cruising between`,`cruising along`,`cruising through`,`cruising across`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Across the coast" is not natural.`,2:`"Through the coast" is not natural.`,0:`"Between the coast" is not natural.`}},{id:`c1-mc-332`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Scientists have yet to ___ the mystery of how the pyramids were built.`,explanation:`"Solve a mystery" is a standard collocation.`,options:[`solve`,`dissolve`,`absolve`,`resolve`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Resolve" often means to decide.`,1:`"Dissolve" means to melt.`,2:`"Absolve" means to forgive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-333`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I would have accepted the job if they ___ me more money.`,explanation:`The third conditional uses the past perfect.`,options:[`would offer`,`have offered`,`offered`,`had offered`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,0:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`The past perfect is needed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-334`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The professor gave ___ lecture on the origins of language that held the audience spellbound.`,explanation:`"A" introduces a singular countable noun.`,options:[`fascinating`,`an fascinating`,`the fascinating`,`a fascinating`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The lecture has not been mentioned before.`,0:`A singular noun needs an article.`,1:`"Fascinating" begins with a consonant sound.`}},{id:`c1-mc-335`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Visitors are strongly advised to ___ their valuables in the hotel safe.`,explanation:`"Deposit" means to place for safekeeping.`,options:[`deport`,`depose`,`deposit`,`depress`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Depose" means to remove from power.`,0:`"Deport" means to expel.`,3:`"Depress" means to make sad.`}},{id:`c1-mc-336`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`By ___ large, the survey confirmed what we had already suspected.`,explanation:`"By and large" means generally.`,options:[`far`,`a`,`the`,`and`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"By the large" is not the idiom.`,1:`"By a large" is not the idiom.`,0:`"By far large" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-337`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`His ___ remarks about her appearance left her feeling deeply uncomfortable.`,explanation:`"Tactless" means lacking sensitivity.`,options:[`tactical`,`tactless`,`tactile`,`tactful`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Tactful" means the opposite.`,2:`"Tactile" relates to touch.`,0:`"Tactical" relates to strategy.`}},{id:`c1-mc-338`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I was going to ring you, but I ___ it completely.`,explanation:`The simple past reports the completed lapse.`,options:[`had forgot`,`have forgotten`,`forgot`,`was forgetting`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The past time reference needs the simple past.`,3:`The continuous does not fit a completed lapse.`,0:`The form "forgot" is not a participle.`}},{id:`c1-mc-339`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`There was an awkward ___ in the conversation when she mentioned his former wife.`,explanation:`A "pause" is a brief silence.`,options:[`pose`,`pause`,`pulse`,`paws`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A "pose" is a posture.`,3:`"Paws" are animal feet.`,2:`A "pulse" is a heartbeat.`}},{id:`c1-mc-340`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Several residents took exception ___ the council's decision to close the library.`,explanation:`"Take exception to" means to object to.`,options:[`to`,`with`,`at`,`for`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Take exception at" is not standard.`,1:`"Take exception with" is not standard.`,3:`"Take exception for" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-341`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Unless she ___ an apology, I shall not speak to her again.`,explanation:`"Unless" takes an affirmative present form.`,options:[`doesn't offer`,`offers`,`will offer`,`offered`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Unless" already has a negative meaning.`,2:`A future form is not used after "unless".`,3:`A past form does not refer to the future.`}},{id:`c1-mc-342`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two parties were unable to reach agreement ___ the terms of the settlement.`,explanation:`"Agreement on" introduces the matter agreed.`,options:[`for`,`on`,`at`,`by`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Agreement at" is not standard.`,3:`"Agreement by" is not standard.`,0:`"Agreement for" is not standard here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-343`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Anyone ___ wishes to attend should register in advance.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject.`,options:[`who`,`whose`,`which`,`whom`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Whom" is an object form.`,2:`"Which" refers to things.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-344`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The village, nestled in a remote valley, remains largely ___ by mass tourism.`,explanation:`"Unspoilt" means not damaged by development.`,options:[`unsettled`,`unspoken`,`unsound`,`unspoilt`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Unspoken" means not said aloud.`,2:`"Unsound" means unreliable.`,0:`"Unsettled" means disturbed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-345`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Many graduates find that their qualifications do not ___ them for the realities of the workplace.`,explanation:`"Equip someone for" means to prepare them.`,options:[`equal`,`equip`,`equate`,`equivalent`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Equal" means to be the same as.`,2:`"Equate" means to consider as equivalent.`,3:`"Equivalent" is an adjective.`}},{id:`c1-mc-346`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The minister was forced to resign ___ the pressure of public opinion.`,explanation:`"Under pressure" is the fixed collocation.`,options:[`in`,`under`,`at`,`with`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"In the pressure" is not standard.`,2:`"At the pressure" is not standard.`,3:`"With the pressure" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-347`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She showed ___ little interest in the proposal that it was dropped.`,explanation:`"So little ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`too`,`such`,`so`,`very`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Such little interest" is awkward.`,3:`"Very" cannot introduce a result.`,0:`"Too" does not introduce a result with "that".`}},{id:`c1-mc-348`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`They blamed ___ for the failure of the negotiations, each accusing the other of bad faith.`,explanation:`"Each other" expresses a mutual action.`,options:[`one`,`others`,`each other`,`themselves`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Themselves" would mean each blamed himself.`,0:`"One" does not fit here.`,1:`"Others" is not a reciprocal pronoun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-349`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`If I ___ you, I would think very carefully before accepting the offer.`,explanation:`"If I were you" is a fixed advice structure.`,options:[`am`,`was`,`would be`,`were`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The second conditional needs a past form.`,1:`"Were" is the standard form in this advice structure.`,2:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-350`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ you could come to the meeting, so I did not book a room for you.`,explanation:`The simple past reports a past assumption.`,options:[`was assuming`,`have assumed`,`assumed`,`had assumed`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The simple past reports a conclusion reached at the time.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a past misunderstanding.`,3:`The past perfect needs a later past reference.`}},{id:`c1-mc-351`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The thief is believed ___ the country already.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive shows an earlier time.`,options:[`to leave`,`leaving`,`to have left`,`having left`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The perfect infinitive refers to an earlier time.`,1:`A participle does not follow "believed".`,3:`"Believed" takes an infinitive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-352`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I am afraid I cannot ___ with such rudeness any longer.`,explanation:`"Put up with" means to tolerate.`,options:[`put out`,`put up`,`put on`,`put in`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Put on" means to wear.`,0:`"Put out" means to extinguish.`,3:`"Put in" means to insert.`}},{id:`c1-mc-353`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The firm's decision to relocate abroad ___ a storm of protest from the workforce.`,explanation:`"Provoke" means to cause a strong reaction.`,options:[`provided`,`provoked`,`proved`,`prohibited`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Proved" means demonstrated.`,0:`"Provided" means supplied.`,3:`"Prohibited" means banned.`}},{id:`c1-mc-354`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the end of the decade, the population of the city ___ by almost fifty per cent.`,explanation:`"By the end of" calls for the future perfect.`,options:[`will have grown`,`has grown`,`grows`,`will grow`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The future perfect shows completion before a future time.`,2:`A present tense does not fit.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a future time.`}},{id:`c1-mc-355`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`One should always do ___ best to treat others with respect.`,explanation:`"One" is followed by "one's" in formal usage.`,options:[`one's`,`their`,`its`,`his`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"His" excludes women in formal usage.`,1:`"Their" breaks the formal pattern.`,2:`"Its" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-mc-356`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`What I find most irritating is ___ he never admits he is wrong.`,explanation:`"That" introduces the noun clause after "is".`,options:[`what`,`that`,`which`,`how`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"What" would repeat the cleft word.`,2:`"Which" needs an antecedent.`,3:`"How" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-357`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The children were ___ to find that the circus had left town.`,explanation:`"Dismayed" means upset and disappointed.`,options:[`dismayed`,`dismissed`,`dismantled`,`disarmed`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Dismissed" means sent away.`,2:`"Dismantled" means taken apart.`,3:`"Disarmed" means deprived of weapons.`}},{id:`c1-mc-358`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`No sooner had we sat down to dinner ___ the telephone started ringing.`,explanation:`"No sooner ... than" is a fixed correlative pair.`,options:[`as`,`that`,`than`,`when`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"No sooner" is followed by "than".`,1:`"That" does not complete the comparison.`,0:`"As" does not follow "no sooner".`}},{id:`c1-mc-359`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She was ___ with grief when she heard that her old teacher had died.`,explanation:`"Overcome with" means overwhelmed by a feeling.`,options:[`overcome`,`overtaken`,`overheard`,`overlooked`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Overtaken" means passed by.`,3:`"Overlooked" means failed to notice.`,2:`"Overheard" means listened to by chance.`}},{id:`c1-mc-360`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`There is little ___ we can do to change the situation now.`,explanation:`"That" is the usual relative pronoun after "little".`,options:[`which`,`that`,`whose`,`what`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot follow "little" here.`,0:`"Which" is awkward after "little".`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-361`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The two sisters, both of ___ are doctors, run a clinic together.`,explanation:`"Both of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`which`,`whom`,`who`,`them`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,0:`"Which" refers to things.`,3:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-362`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Hardly ___ of the staff attended the training session.`,explanation:`"Hardly any" means almost none.`,options:[`some`,`no`,`any`,`all`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Hardly no" is a double negative.`,0:`"Hardly some" is not idiomatic.`,3:`"Hardly all" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-363`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`He ___ well be right, but I still think we should check.`,explanation:`"May well" expresses a strong possibility.`,options:[`shall`,`ought`,`may`,`would`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Would well" is not idiomatic.`,0:`"Shall well" is not idiomatic.`,1:`"Ought well be" lacks "to".`}},{id:`c1-mc-364`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The drought has had a devastating ___ on agriculture in the region.`,explanation:`"Impact" means effect.`,options:[`impart`,`impasse`,`impact`,`impulse`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Impart" means to give information.`,3:`An "impulse" is a sudden urge.`,1:`An "impasse" is a deadlock.`}},{id:`c1-mc-365`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Not ___ the candidates were equally qualified, but all were given a fair hearing.`,explanation:`"Not all the candidates" is a partial negation.`,options:[`every`,`all`,`each`,`whole`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Not every the candidates" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Not each the candidates" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Whole" cannot be used this way.`}},{id:`c1-mc-366`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two brothers have been estranged ___ many years over a dispute about their father's will.`,explanation:`"For" is used with a length of time.`,options:[`for`,`during`,`since`,`from`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Since" needs a starting point.`,1:`"During" does not take a length of time.`,3:`"From" does not combine with the present perfect.`}},{id:`c1-mc-367`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Teachers complained that the new curriculum was overloaded and left no room for ___.`,explanation:`"Creativity" is the noun for creative ability.`,options:[`creativity`,`creatively`,`creation`,`creative`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Creative" is an adjective.`,2:`"Creation" is the act of creating.`,1:`"Creatively" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-368`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Visitors are strongly advised to ___ travel insurance before setting off on an expedition.`,explanation:`"Take out" insurance means to buy a policy.`,options:[`take on`,`take in`,`take out`,`take off`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Take in" means to absorb.`,0:`"Take on" means to accept a task.`,3:`"Take off" means to remove.`}},{id:`c1-mc-369`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two cultures differ ___ one another in many fundamental respects.`,explanation:`"Differ from" compares two things.`,options:[`with`,`against`,`to`,`from`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Differ to" is uncommon.`,0:`"Differ with" means to disagree.`,1:`"Differ against" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-370`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She was left feeling thoroughly ___ after the interview, convinced that she had made a terrible impression.`,explanation:`"Deflated" means suddenly lacking confidence.`,options:[`deflected`,`deflated`,`defeated`,`deflowered`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Deflected" means turned aside.`,3:`This word has a different meaning.`,2:`"Defeated" means beaten in a contest.`}},{id:`c1-mc-371`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The scientist's theory was met with a good deal of ___.`,explanation:`"Scepticism" is the noun.`,options:[`scepticism`,`sceptical`,`sceptic`,`sceptically`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Sceptical" is an adjective.`,2:`A "sceptic" is a person.`,3:`"Sceptically" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-372`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`We are doing everything in our power to ensure that ___ money is wasted.`,explanation:`"No" is the determiner meaning not any.`,options:[`none`,`any`,`not`,`no`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"None money" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Not money" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Any money is wasted" would not mean the same.`}},{id:`c1-mc-373`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`They were ___ with envy when they saw the size of her house.`,explanation:`"Green with envy" is the fixed idiom.`,options:[`blue`,`grey`,`red`,`green`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Blue" suggests sadness.`,2:`"Red" suggests anger or embarrassment.`,1:`"Grey" suggests dullness.`}},{id:`c1-mc-374`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`It is the first time that the city ___ such a violent storm in living memory.`,explanation:`"The first time" takes the present perfect.`,options:[`experienced`,`has experienced`,`experiences`,`had experienced`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"The first time" takes the present perfect.`,3:`The past perfect needs a past reference.`,2:`A present simple does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-375`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ not use your phone during the examination; anyone caught will be disqualified.`,explanation:`"May not" can express a formal prohibition.`,options:[`might`,`may`,`should`,`would`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Might not" is weaker than a prohibition.`,3:`"Would not use" does not give a rule.`,2:`"Should not" is advice.`}},{id:`c1-mc-376`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He made a ___ effort to appear calm, but his hands were shaking.`,explanation:`"Conscious" means deliberate here.`,options:[`conscious`,`conscientious`,`conscience`,`consciously`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A "conscience" is a moral sense.`,1:`"Conscientious" means careful.`,3:`"Consciously" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-377`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Although the plan looked flawless on paper, it ___ never have worked in practice.`,explanation:`"Could never have worked" says it was impossible for it to work.`,options:[`ought`,`could`,`should`,`might`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Should never have worked" would mean it was wrong that it worked.`,0:`"Ought never have" lacks the infinitive marker in this structure.`,3:`"Might never have worked" would leave the possibility open.`}},{id:`c1-mc-378`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Campaigners have called for a ___ on the sale of single-use plastics.`,explanation:`A "ban" is an official prohibition.`,options:[`band`,`ban`,`bar`,`bane`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A "band" is a group or strip.`,2:`"Bar" is a place or a rod.`,3:`"Bane" is a source of trouble.`}},{id:`c1-mc-379`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`After a long and exhausting journey, we finally ___ at our destination just before midnight.`,explanation:`"Arrive at" is used with a destination.`,options:[`reached at`,`landed`,`came`,`arrived`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Landed" is used for planes and ships.`,0:`"Reached at" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Came" needs a preposition.`}},{id:`c1-mc-380`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Many travellers prefer to ___ a car rather than rely on infrequent public transport in rural areas.`,explanation:`"Hire" means to rent for a short time.`,options:[`hire`,`lend`,`borrow`,`employ`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Lend" means to give temporarily.`,2:`"Borrow" means to take from a person.`,3:`"Employ" is for people.`}},{id:`c1-mc-381`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The consultant was brought in to ___ the efficiency of the whole department.`,explanation:`"Assess" means to evaluate.`,options:[`access`,`assess`,`assets`,`assist`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Access" means to reach.`,2:`"Assets" are possessions.`,3:`"Assist" means to help.`}},{id:`c1-mc-382`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ evidence presented at the trial was circumstantial, and the jury remained unconvinced.`,explanation:`"The evidence" refers to specific evidence introduced at the trial.`,options:[`The`,`An`,`Some of`,`A`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Evidence" is uncountable and cannot take "an".`,2:`"Some of" needs a following determiner.`,3:`"Evidence" is uncountable and cannot take "a".`}},{id:`c1-mc-383`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The children were told to ___ their best behaviour in front of the guests.`,explanation:`"Be on one's best behaviour" is a fixed phrase.`,options:[`be on`,`put on`,`keep on`,`have on`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Have on" means to wear.`,1:`"Put on" means to display falsely.`,2:`"Keep on" means to continue.`}},{id:`c1-mc-384`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`He is a ___ individual who has never followed the crowd.`,explanation:`A "maverick" is an independent-minded person.`,options:[`martyr`,`mercenary`,`meander`,`maverick`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Meander" means to wander.`,1:`A "mercenary" fights for money.`,0:`A "martyr" suffers for a cause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-385`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`There ___ be no doubt that the economy is entering a period of serious uncertainty.`,explanation:`"There can be no doubt" is a fixed emphatic expression.`,options:[`can`,`would`,`may`,`shall`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"There would be no doubt" is conditional.`,3:`"Shall" is not used like this.`,2:`"May be no doubt" weakens the statement.`}},{id:`c1-mc-386`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`___ she said took everyone by surprise.`,explanation:`"What" means "the thing that".`,options:[`Which`,`That`,`What`,`Who`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"That she said" lacks a head.`,0:`"Which she said" lacks an antecedent.`,3:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`c1-mc-387`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He was dismissed ___ the grounds of gross misconduct.`,explanation:`"On the grounds of" introduces the reason.`,options:[`on`,`by`,`for`,`from`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"For the grounds" is not standard.`,1:`"By the grounds" is not standard.`,3:`"From the grounds" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-388`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Having been rerouted twice, the passengers eventually arrived at their destination hours behind ___.`,explanation:`"Behind schedule" means later than planned.`,options:[`time`,`schedule`,`plan`,`timetable`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Behind timetable" is possible but "behind schedule" is the fixed phrase.`,2:`"Behind plan" is not standard.`,0:`"Behind time" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-389`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The prime minister ___ to announce an election, but she has since changed her mind.`,explanation:`"Was due to" refers to a scheduled event that did not happen.`,options:[`is due`,`had due`,`has been due`,`was due`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Had due" is ungrammatical.`,0:`The present does not show that the plan has changed.`,2:`"Has been due" is not used with a changed plan.`}},{id:`c1-mc-390`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The speaker was ___ a master of his subject that nobody dared to question him.`,explanation:`"Such a" precedes a noun phrase.`,options:[`so`,`such`,`too`,`very`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"So a master" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Very a master" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Too a master" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-391`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`They ___ a heated argument over who should pay the bill.`,explanation:`"Have an argument" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`did`,`had`,`made`,`took`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Make an argument" is less natural.`,0:`"Do an argument" is not standard.`,3:`"Take an argument" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-392`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The firm was fined ___ breaching environmental regulations.`,explanation:`"Fined for" introduces the offence.`,options:[`with`,`of`,`for`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Fined of" is not standard.`,0:`"Fined with" is not standard.`,3:`"Fined at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-393`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The scheme is intended to provide assistance ___ families on low incomes.`,explanation:`"Assistance to" introduces the recipients.`,options:[`to`,`at`,`for`,`on`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Assistance for" is possible but "to" is usual here.`,1:`"Assistance at" is not standard.`,3:`"Assistance on" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-394`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The figures ___ since the last quarter and now show a steady recovery.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous links change to now.`,options:[`are rising`,`rose`,`have been rising`,`rise`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The present simple does not show duration.`,0:`The present continuous does not show duration since a point.`,1:`The simple past does not link to the present.`}},{id:`c1-mc-395`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The charity works to ___ the plight of refugees fleeing conflict.`,explanation:`"Alleviate" means to reduce suffering.`,options:[`allocate`,`alienate`,`alleviated`,`alleviate`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Alienate" means to make hostile.`,0:`"Allocate" means to assign.`,2:`After "to" use the base form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-396`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The theatre ___ for renovation since January and is not expected to reopen until next spring.`,explanation:`The present perfect passive links a past start to now.`,options:[`has been closed`,`is closing`,`closed`,`had been closed`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The present continuous does not link to a past starting point.`,2:`The simple past does not link to now.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit a situation continuing now.`}},{id:`c1-mc-397`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Tourists are drawn to the region by its ___ scenery and unspoilt beaches.`,explanation:`"Breathtaking" means stunningly beautiful.`,options:[`breathing`,`breathless`,`breathed`,`breathtaking`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Breathless" means out of breath.`,0:`"Breathing" is a participle.`,2:`"Breathed" is a past tense.`}},{id:`c1-mc-398`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The professor's ___ lecture challenged many of the assumptions on which the discipline is based.`,explanation:`"Provocative" means designed to stimulate debate.`,options:[`provocation`,`provocative`,`provoked`,`provoking`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Provoking" does not describe a lecture in this sense.`,0:`"Provocation" is a noun.`,2:`"Provoked" is a participle.`}},{id:`c1-mc-399`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company is seeking to diversify ___ new markets overseas.`,explanation:`"Diversify into" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`by`,`at`,`to`,`into`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Diversify to" is not standard.`,1:`"Diversify at" is not standard.`,0:`"Diversify by" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-400`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The old man was a ___ figure in the village, respected by everyone.`,explanation:`"Revered" means deeply respected.`,options:[`revived`,`revered`,`reviewed`,`reversed`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Revived" means brought back.`,3:`"Reversed" means turned round.`,2:`"Reviewed" means examined.`}},{id:`c1-mc-401`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The children ___ be exhausted after such a long journey, so let them sleep.`,explanation:`"Must" expresses a strong deduction about the present.`,options:[`must`,`ought`,`would`,`may to`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Ought be" lacks "to".`,3:`"May to" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Would be" does not express a strong deduction.`}},{id:`c1-mc-402`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`___ you require further assistance, please do not hesitate to contact us.`,explanation:`"Should" + subject is a formal conditional.`,options:[`Might`,`Should`,`Would`,`Could`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Would you require" does not form the formal conditional.`,3:`"Could you require" does not form the formal conditional.`,0:`"Might you require" is archaic.`}},{id:`c1-mc-403`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`He is said to ___ the first person to cross the desert on foot.`,explanation:`"To have been" refers to an earlier event in a reporting structure.`,options:[`have been`,`being`,`had been`,`be being`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`A continuous form changes the meaning.`,2:`A finite form is not used after "to".`,1:`After "said to" an infinitive is needed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-404`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The discovery that the drug could reduce the risk of heart disease was ___ by chance during a trial for an unrelated condition.`,explanation:`"Make a discovery" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`taken`,`made`,`done`,`given`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Do a discovery" is not a collocation.`,0:`"Take a discovery" is not a collocation.`,3:`"Give a discovery" is not a collocation.`}},{id:`c1-mc-405`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`There's no knowing how the markets ___ react to the announcement.`,explanation:`"There's no knowing" is followed by a wh-clause.`,options:[`will`,`shall to`,`would to`,`to`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`After "how" a finite verb is needed.`,2:`"Would to" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Shall to" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-406`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`He was promoted ___ the heads of several more experienced colleagues.`,explanation:`"Over the heads of" means ignoring others in rank.`,options:[`above`,`across`,`over`,`along`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Above the heads" has a different sense.`,1:`"Across the heads" is not standard.`,3:`"Along the heads" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-407`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The novel, ___ was published anonymously, was an instant bestseller.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`that`,`whose`,`which`,`what`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,0:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-408`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She has little patience ___ people who arrive late for meetings.`,explanation:`"Patience with" introduces the person tolerated.`,options:[`to`,`at`,`with`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Patience to" is not standard.`,3:`"Patience for" is possible but "with" is the usual collocation.`,1:`"Patience at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-409`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The books ___ I have borrowed from the library are all about astronomy.`,explanation:`"That" is a relative pronoun for things.`,options:[`what`,`who`,`whose`,`that`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"What" cannot follow a noun.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`,1:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`c1-mc-410`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`They have been at ___ over the inheritance ever since their mother's funeral.`,explanation:`"At odds" means in disagreement.`,options:[`ends`,`odds`,`war`,`sea`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"At ends" is not the idiom.`,2:`"At war over" is possible but "at odds" is the idiom for disagreement.`,3:`"At sea" means confused or on a ship.`}},{id:`c1-mc-411`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The firm's ___ has grown steadily, reaching ten million pounds last year.`,explanation:`"Turnover" is total sales.`,options:[`turnover`,`turnout`,`changeover`,`takeover`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Turnout" means attendance.`,3:`A "takeover" is the buying of a company.`,2:`"Changeover" means a switch.`}},{id:`c1-mc-412`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She seemed ___ to the noise and carried on reading as though nothing were happening.`,explanation:`"Oblivious to" means unaware of.`,options:[`obliged`,`oblivious`,`oblique`,`obvious`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Obvious" means easy to see.`,2:`"Oblique" means slanting.`,0:`"Obliged" means bound by duty.`}},{id:`c1-mc-413`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The proposal met ___ widespread approval among the staff, who had long wanted such a change.`,explanation:`"Meet with approval" means to receive it.`,options:[`with`,`at`,`in`,`by`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Met by approval" suggests the approval was a person.`,2:`"Met in approval" is not standard.`,1:`"Met at approval" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-414`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`There has been a sharp ___ in the number of students choosing to study foreign languages.`,explanation:`"A decline in" means a fall.`,options:[`declining`,`decline of`,`decline`,`declination`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A noun phrase needs "in" here.`,3:`This is a rare technical word.`,0:`"Declining" is a participle.`}},{id:`c1-mc-415`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`They ___ well have been unaware of the risks when they signed the contract.`,explanation:`"May well have" expresses a strong possibility.`,options:[`would`,`ought`,`shall`,`may`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Would well have been" is not idiomatic.`,2:`"Shall well have been" is not idiomatic.`,1:`"Ought well have been" lacks "to".`}},{id:`c1-mc-416`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Her sudden departure took everyone completely by ___; nobody had suspected she was unhappy.`,explanation:`"Take someone by surprise" means to astonish them.`,options:[`shock`,`storm`,`force`,`surprise`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Take by storm" has a different meaning.`,0:`"Take by shock" is not the idiom.`,2:`"Take by force" means to seize.`}},{id:`c1-mc-417`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He has made ___ contributions to the field that his name is known throughout the world.`,explanation:`"So many ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`such many`,`too many`,`so many`,`very many`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Such many" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Too many" does not introduce a result with "that".`,3:`"Very many ... that" needs "so".`}},{id:`c1-mc-418`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`They set off at ___ to avoid the worst of the midday heat.`,explanation:`"At dawn" means at first light.`,options:[`dawn`,`sunshine`,`morning`,`daylight`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Set off at daylight" is not the fixed phrase.`,1:`"Set off at sunshine" is not the fixed phrase.`,2:`"At morning" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-419`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The minister is thought ___ the country secretly last night.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive refers to an earlier event.`,options:[`to be leaving`,`to have left`,`to leave`,`leaving`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The simple infinitive does not show an earlier event.`,0:`The continuous infinitive suggests an ongoing action.`,3:`After "thought" an infinitive is needed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-420`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`He took the news in his ___ and carried on with his work as if nothing had happened.`,explanation:`"Take in one's stride" means to deal with calmly.`,options:[`walk`,`step`,`stride`,`pace`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"In his step" is not the idiom.`,0:`"In his walk" is not the idiom.`,3:`"In his pace" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-421`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I thought that you ___ the report by now.`,explanation:`"Would have finished" is the past of "will have finished".`,options:[`would have finished`,`have finished`,`finished`,`will have finished`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`Reported thought shifts "will" to "would".`,1:`The present perfect does not fit the past thought.`,2:`The simple past does not express the expected completion.`}},{id:`c1-mc-422`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The film ___ by the time we got to the cinema.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,options:[`already started`,`had already started`,`was already starting`,`has already started`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`The sentence is in the past.`,0:`The simple past does not show an earlier action.`,2:`The continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-mc-423`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He was dismissed ___ misconduct after an internal investigation.`,explanation:`"Dismissed for" introduces the reason.`,options:[`with`,`of`,`by`,`for`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Dismissed of" is not standard.`,2:`"Dismissed by" introduces an agent.`,0:`"Dismissed with" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-424`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The ancient ruins are ___ for their well-preserved mosaics.`,explanation:`"Renowned for" means famous for.`,options:[`renewed`,`removed`,`renowned`,`rendered`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Renewed" means made new.`,3:`"Rendered" means made or given.`,1:`"Removed" means taken away.`}},{id:`c1-mc-425`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Is there anyone ___ can explain what has happened?`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject.`,options:[`whose`,`whom`,`which`,`who`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Whom" is an object form.`,2:`"Which" refers to things.`,0:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-426`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I ___ rather have stayed at home than spent the whole evening at that dreadful party.`,explanation:`"Would rather have" expresses a past preference.`,options:[`might`,`would`,`should`,`had`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Had rather" is dated.`,2:`"Should rather" is rare.`,0:`"Might rather" is not used.`}},{id:`c1-mc-427`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The government is under pressure to ___ the regulations governing online privacy.`,explanation:`"Tighten" means to make rules stricter.`,options:[`tighten`,`tightened`,`tighten up`,`tight`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Tight" is an adjective.`,2:`"Tighten" is the verb used directly with an object here.`,1:`After "to" use the base form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-428`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The old couple have been ___ for sixty years, and still hold hands when they walk.`,explanation:`"Devoted" means loving and loyal.`,options:[`devoted`,`devoured`,`deflated`,`devout`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Devout" means deeply religious.`,1:`"Devoured" means eaten greedily.`,2:`"Deflated" means let down.`}},{id:`c1-mc-429`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`He is rather ___ when it comes to money and never offers to pay his share.`,explanation:`"Tight-fisted" means unwilling to spend money.`,options:[`tight-fisted`,`tight-knit`,`tight-rope`,`tight-lipped`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Tight-lipped" means refusing to speak.`,1:`"Tight-knit" describes a close group.`,2:`This compound does not exist.`}},{id:`c1-mc-430`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`It was not until I read the final chapter ___ I understood the author's intention.`,explanation:`"It was not until ... that" is a cleft structure.`,options:[`which`,`that`,`what`,`when`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"It was not until ... when" is informal.`,0:`"Which" is ungrammatical here.`,2:`"What" is ungrammatical here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-431`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Early detection of the disease dramatically ___ the chances of successful treatment.`,explanation:`The present simple states a general fact.`,options:[`improvements`,`improved`,`improving`,`improves`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Improvements" is a plural noun.`,2:`An auxiliary is missing.`,1:`The sentence states a general truth.`}},{id:`c1-mc-432`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By this time next year, the new system ___ in operation for six months.`,explanation:`"By this time next year" calls for the future perfect.`,options:[`would be`,`has been`,`will be`,`will have been`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The future perfect shows duration up to a future point.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a future time.`,0:`"Would" does not fit a real future.`}},{id:`c1-mc-433`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Poverty is often ___ by a lack of access to education and healthcare.`,explanation:`"Exacerbated" means made worse.`,options:[`exasperated`,`exaggerated`,`exacerbated`,`exacted`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Exasperated" means extremely irritated.`,1:`"Exaggerated" means overstated.`,3:`"Exacted" means demanded.`}},{id:`c1-mc-434`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`If only I ___ to her advice at the time; I wouldn't be in this mess now.`,explanation:`"If only" + past perfect expresses a regret about the past.`,options:[`had listened`,`would listen`,`listen`,`listened`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`A mixed regret about the past needs the past perfect.`,2:`A present tense does not fit.`,1:`"Would" does not express past regret.`}},{id:`c1-mc-435`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The inspector found several ___ in the company's accounts that required explanation.`,explanation:`"Discrepancies" are inconsistencies.`,options:[`discoveries`,`disagreements`,`disruptions`,`discrepancies`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Disruptions" are interruptions.`,1:`"Disagreements" are arguments.`,0:`"Discoveries" are findings.`}},{id:`c1-mc-436`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The scheme was designed to ___ the gap between the richest and poorest regions.`,explanation:`"Narrow the gap" means to reduce it.`,options:[`narrows`,`narrow`,`narrate`,`narrowly`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Narrate" means to tell a story.`,3:`"Narrowly" is an adverb.`,0:`After "to" use the base form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-437`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The proposal has been met with ___ from several quarters, and it may have to be revised.`,explanation:`"Criticism" is the noun.`,options:[`critical`,`criticism`,`criticise`,`critic`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A "critic" is a person.`,0:`"Critical" is an adjective.`,2:`"Criticise" is a verb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-438`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Hardly ___ the contract been signed when the first problems emerged.`,explanation:`"Hardly had ... when" is the standard correlative pair.`,options:[`was`,`did`,`had`,`has`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The past perfect is needed.`,1:`"Did the contract been" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Was the contract been" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-439`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The guesthouse was ___ and welcoming, with a log fire in the lounge.`,explanation:`"Cosy" means warm and comfortable.`,options:[`cosy`,`costly`,`cosmic`,`cozy`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`This is the American spelling.`,2:`"Cosmic" relates to the universe.`,1:`"Costly" means expensive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-440`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We are indebted ___ our colleagues in the laboratory for their painstaking work.`,explanation:`"Indebted to" introduces the person owed.`,options:[`to`,`with`,`at`,`for`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Indebted for" would introduce the thing owed.`,1:`"Indebted with" is not standard.`,2:`"Indebted at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-441`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She has ___ knowledge of the subject than any of her colleagues.`,explanation:`"More ... than" forms a comparative.`,options:[`most`,`more`,`much`,`many`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Much knowledge than" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Most than" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Knowledge" is uncountable.`}},{id:`c1-mc-442`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The inspector took no notice ___ the complaints of the residents.`,explanation:`"Take notice of" is the fixed phrase.`,options:[`to`,`at`,`for`,`of`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Take notice to" is not standard.`,2:`"Take notice for" is not standard.`,1:`"Take notice at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-443`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She tried to ___ her annoyance, but her irritation was obvious to everyone in the room.`,explanation:`"Conceal" means to hide.`,options:[`conceal`,`concern`,`concede`,`conceive`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Conceive" means to imagine or become pregnant.`,2:`"Concede" means to admit.`,1:`"Concern" means to worry.`}},{id:`c1-mc-444`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After weeks of ___ negotiations, the two families finally agreed on the terms of the sale.`,explanation:`"Protracted" means lasting longer than expected.`,options:[`protruded`,`protected`,`protracted`,`protested`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Protected" means kept safe.`,0:`"Protruded" means stuck out.`,3:`"Protested" means objected.`}},{id:`c1-mc-445`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I'm afraid I'm rather out of ___ with modern music; I prefer the classics.`,explanation:`"Out of touch with" means no longer familiar with.`,options:[`touch`,`step`,`reach`,`line`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Out of reach" means unattainable.`,1:`"Out of step" has a different meaning.`,3:`"Out of line" means behaving unacceptably.`}},{id:`c1-mc-446`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`After the scandal, the chairman was forced to ___ down from his position.`,explanation:`"Step down" means to resign.`,options:[`walk`,`pull`,`cut`,`step`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Pull down" means to demolish.`,0:`"Walk down" is literal.`,2:`"Cut down" means to fell.`}},{id:`c1-mc-447`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The theory is still ___, as there is no firm evidence to support it.`,explanation:`"Speculative" means based on guesswork.`,options:[`speculation`,`spectacular`,`speculate`,`speculative`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Speculate" is a verb.`,0:`"Speculation" is a noun.`,1:`"Spectacular" means impressive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-448`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The tenants, ___ had complained about the heating for months, were finally offered compensation.`,explanation:`"All of whom" joins the clauses.`,options:[`all of them`,`all of who`,`all of whom`,`all of which`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Them" cannot join the clauses.`,1:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,3:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-mc-449`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The tour guide ___ out that the building had been rebuilt after a fire in 1666.`,explanation:`"Pointed out" is the past simple.`,options:[`point`,`points`,`pointed`,`pointing`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`An auxiliary is missing.`,0:`A past form is needed.`,1:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-450`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The committee is composed ___ representatives of all the major parties.`,explanation:`"Composed of" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`of`,`with`,`from`,`by`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Composed by" refers to music.`,2:`"Composed from" is not standard.`,1:`"Composed with" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-451`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The consultant's report was ___ of the management's handling of the crisis.`,explanation:`"Critical of" means expressing disapproval.`,options:[`criticism`,`critical`,`critically`,`critic`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A "critic" is a person.`,0:`"Criticism" is a noun.`,2:`"Critically" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-452`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She is said to ___ the most gifted pianist of her generation, although she never sought fame.`,explanation:`"To have been" refers to an earlier state in reported opinions.`,options:[`had been`,`being`,`have been`,`be being`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`After "said to" an infinitive is needed.`,3:`A continuous passive form changes the meaning.`,0:`A finite perfect form is not used after "to".`}},{id:`c1-mc-453`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Whatever the reason, she ___ not to have spoken to him in that tone.`,explanation:`"Ought not to have" criticises a past action.`,options:[`ought`,`must`,`would`,`needs`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Must not to have" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Would not to have" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Needs not to have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-454`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ number of students have expressed concern about the new assessment procedures.`,explanation:`"A number of" means several and takes a plural verb.`,options:[`Each`,`A`,`Some of`,`The`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"The number of" takes a singular verb.`,2:`"Some of number" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Each number" does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-455`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`It's high time the government ___ serious steps to curb inflation.`,explanation:`"It's high time" + past simple states that action is overdue.`,options:[`has taken`,`took`,`takes`,`would take`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`After "it's high time" a past form is required.`,3:`"Would take" is not used after "it's high time".`,0:`The present perfect does not fit the structure.`}},{id:`c1-mc-456`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Many rural communities feel ___ by the government, which they accuse of ignoring their needs.`,explanation:`"Neglected" describes how the communities feel.`,options:[`negligent`,`neglectful`,`negligible`,`neglected`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Negligent" describes a person who fails in a duty.`,1:`"Neglectful" describes the person who ignores.`,2:`"Negligible" means very small.`}},{id:`c1-mc-457`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The directors ___ to be informed immediately of any change in the company's financial position.`,explanation:`"Be to" expresses an obligation or arrangement.`,options:[`will`,`do`,`have`,`are`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Have to be informed" changes the structure.`,1:`"Do to be informed" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Will to be informed" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-458`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The manager was criticised for her ___ handling of the dispute.`,explanation:`"Clumsy" means awkward.`,options:[`clumsily`,`clumsiness`,`clutched`,`clumsy`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`An adjective is needed.`,1:`"Clumsiness" is a noun.`,2:`"Clutched" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-459`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`At the time of the earthquake, most residents ___ asleep.`,explanation:`The past simple of "be" describes the situation at the time.`,options:[`were`,`have been`,`are`,`had been`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The past perfect would imply an earlier state.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit past events.`,2:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-460`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The hotel offers ___ views over the bay from every room.`,explanation:`"Panoramic" means wide and unbroken.`,options:[`pandemic`,`panic`,`pantomime`,`panoramic`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Panic" is sudden fear.`,0:`A "pandemic" is a widespread disease.`,2:`A "pantomime" is a stage show.`}},{id:`c1-mc-461`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Antibiotics are ineffective ___ viral infections such as the common cold.`,explanation:`"Ineffective against" introduces the thing opposed.`,options:[`at`,`against`,`with`,`on`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Ineffective at" needs a gerund.`,2:`"Ineffective with" is not standard.`,3:`"Ineffective on" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-462`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She gave a ___ smile that did not quite reach her eyes.`,explanation:`"Strained" means forced or unnatural.`,options:[`strengthened`,`strange`,`strained`,`strait`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Strange" means unusual.`,3:`"Strait" is a narrow sea passage.`,0:`"Strengthened" means made stronger.`}},{id:`c1-mc-463`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company is currently ___ negotiations with a potential buyer.`,explanation:`"In negotiations" is the fixed collocation.`,options:[`on`,`at`,`with`,`in`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"On negotiations" is not standard.`,1:`"At negotiations" is not standard.`,2:`"With negotiations" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-464`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`It took her a long time to come to ___ with the loss of her job.`,explanation:`"Come to terms with" means to accept.`,options:[`ends`,`grips`,`peace`,`terms`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Come to grips with" has a different structure.`,0:`"Come to ends" is not the idiom.`,2:`"Come to peace" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-465`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`He has two brothers; ___ of them lives in London.`,explanation:`"Neither of them lives" refers to two.`,options:[`all`,`neither`,`none`,`both`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"None" is used for more than two.`,3:`"Both of them lives" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"All" refers to more than two.`}},{id:`c1-mc-466`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The rise in prices was largely due ___ the shortage of raw materials.`,explanation:`"Due to" introduces the cause.`,options:[`of`,`by`,`to`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Due of" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Due for" has another meaning.`,1:`"Due by" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-467`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Despite the setbacks, she remained ___ and refused to let anything dampen her spirits.`,explanation:`"Resilient" means able to recover quickly from difficulties.`,options:[`resigned`,`resistant`,`reluctant`,`resilient`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Resistant" means able to withstand something specific.`,2:`"Reluctant" means unwilling.`,0:`"Resigned" means accepting something unpleasant.`}},{id:`c1-mc-468`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The report calls for a radical ___ of the education system.`,explanation:`An "overhaul" is a thorough revision.`,options:[`overhaul`,`overhead`,`overhear`,`overlook`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Overhead" means above.`,3:`"Overlook" means to fail to notice.`,2:`"Overhear" means to hear by chance.`}},{id:`c1-mc-469`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The surgeon performed a ___ operation that lasted fourteen hours.`,explanation:`"Gruelling" means exhausting.`,options:[`grooming`,`growling`,`grueling`,`gruelling`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`This is the American spelling.`,0:`"Grooming" means preparing.`,1:`"Growling" is a sound.`}},{id:`c1-mc-470`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He apologised for the ___ he had caused by arriving late.`,explanation:`"Inconvenience" is the noun.`,options:[`inconvenience`,`inconveniently`,`incontinence`,`inconvenient`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Inconvenient" is an adjective.`,1:`"Inconveniently" is an adverb.`,2:`"Incontinence" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-471`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the time the new bridge opens, work on it ___ for nearly six years.`,explanation:`The future perfect continuous shows duration up to a future moment.`,options:[`has gone on`,`will go on`,`goes on`,`will have been going on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The future perfect continuous shows duration.`,2:`A present tense does not fit.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit a future time.`}},{id:`c1-mc-472`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The candidates, ___ had been shortlisted, were invited for interview.`,explanation:`"All of whom" joins the clauses.`,options:[`all of whom`,`all of which`,`all of who`,`all of them`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Them" cannot join the clauses.`,2:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-mc-473`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I haven't the faintest ___ what she meant by that remark.`,explanation:`"Haven't the faintest idea" is a fixed idiom.`,options:[`ideas`,`thought`,`idea`,`notion about`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"The faintest idea" is singular.`,3:`"Notion about" does not fit the idiom.`,1:`"Thought" does not fit the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-474`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Much as I'd ___ to help, I'm afraid my schedule makes it impossible.`,explanation:`"Much as I'd like to" is a concessive structure.`,options:[`likely`,`liking`,`like`,`liked`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`After "I'd" use the base form.`,3:`"liked" does not work here, because after "I'd" use the base form.`,0:`"Likely" is an adjective.`}},{id:`c1-mc-475`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two cultures differ ___ a number of important respects.`,explanation:`"Differ in" introduces the respect of difference.`,options:[`in`,`at`,`by`,`on`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Differ on" introduces a disagreement.`,1:`"Differ at" is not standard.`,2:`"Differ by" introduces an amount.`}},{id:`c1-mc-476`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The proposal met ___ fierce opposition from the unions.`,explanation:`"Meet with opposition" means to encounter it.`,options:[`by`,`with`,`to`,`in`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Met by" is possible but "met with" suggests encountering a reaction.`,2:`"Met to" is not standard.`,3:`"Met in" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-477`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The company ___ losses for three consecutive years before it was rescued by the government.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`has been making`,`was making`,`made`,`had been making`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect continuous does not fit a past rescue.`,1:`The past continuous does not show duration before the rescue.`,2:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-478`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She spoke with ___ conviction that even her critics were impressed.`,explanation:`"Such + noun + that" shows a result.`,options:[`so`,`too`,`very`,`such`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"So conviction" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Too conviction" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Very conviction" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-479`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ about it all day and still cannot decide what to do.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous links duration to the present.`,options:[`thought`,`am thinking`,`have been thinking`,`think`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The present simple does not show duration.`,1:`The present continuous does not show duration up to now.`,0:`The simple past does not connect to the present.`}},{id:`c1-mc-480`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Staff were ___ to attend the training day, although it was not compulsory.`,explanation:`"Encouraged to" is a passive pattern.`,options:[`encouraging`,`encouragement`,`encouraged`,`encourage`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A passive past participle is needed.`,0:`A past participle is needed.`,1:`"Encouragement" is a noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-481`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ never have succeeded without the generous support of our sponsors.`,explanation:`"Could never have" says it was impossible without support.`,options:[`would to`,`could`,`should`,`must`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Would to" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Should never have succeeded" suggests it was wrong.`,3:`"Must never have succeeded" is a deduction.`}},{id:`c1-mc-482`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She has a remarkable talent ___ languages.`,explanation:`"A talent for" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`in`,`at`,`to`,`for`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Talent at" is less common.`,2:`"Talent to" is followed by a verb.`,0:`"Talent in" is less idiomatic.`}},{id:`c1-mc-483`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Whoever ___ the manager's office must first report to reception.`,explanation:`"Whoever" takes a singular present verb.`,options:[`enter`,`entering`,`enters`,`entered`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Whoever" takes a singular verb.`,1:`A finite verb is needed.`,3:`The sentence states a rule.`}},{id:`c1-mc-484`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The epidemic was brought under ___ only after strict restrictions had been imposed.`,explanation:`"Bring under control" is a fixed phrase.`,options:[`control`,`controlled`,`controls`,`controlling`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Under controls" is not the idiom.`,1:`"Under controlled" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Under controlling" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-485`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`His sarcastic comment ___ a nerve with several members of the audience.`,explanation:`"Strike a nerve" means to upset someone.`,options:[`striked`,`stroke`,`struck`,`stricken`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Stroke" is a noun here.`,0:`This past form does not exist.`,3:`"Stricken" is a participle with another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-486`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Scarcely ___ she finished speaking when the audience burst into applause.`,explanation:`"Scarcely had ... when" is the standard correlative pair.`,options:[`did`,`was`,`has`,`had`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The past perfect is needed.`,0:`"Scarcely did she finished" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Was she finished" is passive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-487`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Had it not been for her quick thinking, the situation ___ out of control.`,explanation:`The inverted third conditional uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`would have spiralled`,`would spiral`,`had spiralled`,`will have spiralled`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`This refers to the present, not the past.`,2:`The result clause needs a modal.`,3:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-488`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`He ___ be as rich as everyone says, or he would not live in such a modest house.`,explanation:`"Can't" expresses a negative deduction.`,options:[`can't`,`needn't`,`shouldn't`,`mustn't`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Mustn't" is a prohibition.`,1:`"Needn't" says it is unnecessary.`,2:`"Shouldn't" is advice.`}},{id:`c1-mc-489`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I'll lend you the book as soon as I ___ it.`,explanation:`The present perfect shows completion after a time conjunction.`,options:[`will finish`,`have finished`,`am finishing`,`finished`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A future form is not used after "as soon as".`,2:`The continuous does not show completion.`,3:`A past form does not refer to the future.`}},{id:`c1-mc-490`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The new manager has a reputation for being ___ but fair.`,explanation:`"Strict" means demanding obedience.`,options:[`stricken`,`strictly`,`strict`,`strictness`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`An adjective is needed.`,3:`"Strictness" is a noun.`,0:`"Stricken" means affected by trouble.`}},{id:`c1-mc-491`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The management, ___ of whom had been with the firm for decades, resisted any change.`,explanation:`"Most of whom" is the standard relative construction.`,options:[`mostly`,`most`,`the most`,`almost`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"The most of whom" is not idiomatic.`,3:`"Almost of whom" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Mostly of whom" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-492`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She was passed ___ for promotion despite her outstanding record.`,explanation:`"Passed over" means ignored for promotion.`,options:[`up`,`over`,`by`,`on`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Passed on" means handed on.`,0:`"Passed up" means declined an opportunity.`,2:`"Passed by" means went past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-493`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The firm's reputation ___ on its ability to deliver on time and within budget.`,explanation:`"Rests on" means depends on.`,options:[`rested`,`rest`,`resting`,`rests`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Rest" does not agree with a singular subject.`,2:`An auxiliary is missing.`,0:`The sentence describes a general truth.`}},{id:`c1-mc-494`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ not be aware of it, but the system has been down for the past three hours.`,explanation:`"May not" expresses possibility.`,options:[`may`,`shall`,`ought`,`would`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Shall not be aware" is not natural.`,2:`"Ought not be aware" lacks "to".`,3:`"Would not be aware" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-495`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She told me that she ___ the manuscript by the end of the week.`,explanation:`"Will have finished" becomes "would have finished" in reported speech.`,options:[`finished`,`has finished`,`would have finished`,`will have finished`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`Reported speech shifts "will" to "would".`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`,0:`The future perfect shifts back.`}},{id:`c1-mc-496`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`It is the third time this month that the train ___ late.`,explanation:`"It is the third time" takes the present perfect.`,options:[`has been`,`is`,`was`,`had been`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"The third time" takes the present perfect.`,1:`A present simple does not fit "this month".`,3:`The past perfect needs a past reference.`}},{id:`c1-mc-497`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The tour group was ushered ___ the cathedral by an enthusiastic guide.`,explanation:`"Around" suggests a tour of the interior.`,options:[`across`,`over`,`behind`,`around`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Across the cathedral" suggests a crossing.`,1:`"Over the cathedral" suggests above.`,2:`"Behind the cathedral" suggests position.`}},{id:`c1-mc-498`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It was the manager herself ___ made the final decision.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject of the cleft clause.`,options:[`whose`,`which`,`who`,`whom`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The relative acts as the subject.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`,0:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-499`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The report identifies three factors, the most significant of ___ is the lack of investment.`,explanation:`"The most significant of which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`whom`,`that`,`which`,`them`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Whom" refers to people.`,1:`"That" cannot follow a preposition.`,3:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-500`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She holds a degree ___ economics from a leading university.`,explanation:`"A degree in" a subject is the standard collocation.`,options:[`on`,`of`,`at`,`in`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Degree of economics" is unusual.`,0:`"Degree on" is not standard.`,2:`"Degree at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-501`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I would rather you ___ smoke in the house.`,explanation:`A past form expresses a present preference.`,options:[`won't`,`haven't`,`don't`,`didn't`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`After "would rather" + person use a past form.`,0:`"Won't" is not used here.`,1:`"Haven't" is not used here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-502`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Looking back, I realise that I ___ the warning signs for months before the crisis occurred.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`ignored`,`was ignoring`,`have been ignoring`,`had been ignoring`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The present perfect continuous does not fit a past crisis.`,1:`The past continuous does not show duration before the crisis.`,0:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-503`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The cruise offers an unrivalled opportunity to ___ the Norwegian fjords from the comfort of your cabin.`,explanation:`"Take in" means to view fully.`,options:[`take off`,`take up`,`take in`,`take on`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Take on" means to accept.`,1:`"Take up" means to start.`,0:`"Take off" means to remove.`}},{id:`c1-mc-504`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The firm's reputation rests ___ its commitment to quality rather than on advertising.`,explanation:`"Rest on" means to depend on.`,options:[`with`,`in`,`on`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Rests in" is not the standard collocation.`,3:`"Rests at" is not standard.`,0:`"Rests with" suggests responsibility.`}},{id:`c1-mc-505`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two parties disagreed ___ how the funds should be allocated.`,explanation:`"Disagree on" introduces the point of contention.`,options:[`at`,`for`,`about`,`on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Disagree about" is possible, but "on" is the more usual with questions of principle.`,0:`"Disagree at" is not standard.`,1:`"Disagree for" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-506`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She has just won ___ award for her research into climate change.`,explanation:`"An" precedes a vowel sound.`,options:[`an`,`the`,`a`,`some`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Award" begins with a vowel sound.`,1:`The award is not yet known.`,3:`"Some award" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-507`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She is not one to ___ the boat, and always avoids confrontation.`,explanation:`"Rock the boat" means to cause trouble.`,options:[`row`,`steer`,`sink`,`rock`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Row the boat" is literal.`,2:`"Sink the boat" is not the idiom.`,1:`"Steer the boat" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-508`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The prime minister has been ___ for failing to keep his promises.`,explanation:`"Castigated" means severely criticised.`,options:[`cast`,`castrated`,`cascaded`,`castigated`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Cascaded" means poured down.`,0:`"Cast" is a verb with different meanings.`,1:`"Castrated" is a physical term.`}},{id:`c1-mc-509`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The more the minister tried to explain, the less convinced people ___.`,explanation:`The past simple matches the correlative structure.`,options:[`became`,`had become`,`have become`,`were becoming`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect would imply an earlier completed change.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit past events.`,3:`The continuous weakens the parallel with "tried".`}},{id:`c1-mc-510`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`They were inseparable as children, but they ___ apart as they grew older.`,explanation:`"Drift apart" means to become less close.`,options:[`drift`,`drifting`,`drafted`,`drifted`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Drafted" means conscripted.`,1:`An auxiliary is missing.`,0:`A past form is needed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-511`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`After a long ___, the committee agreed to raise the membership fee.`,explanation:`"Deliberation" means careful discussion.`,options:[`deliberation`,`delegation`,`deliberate`,`deliverance`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Deliverance" means rescue.`,2:`"Deliberate" is an adjective or verb.`,1:`"Delegation" is a group of representatives.`}},{id:`c1-mc-512`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The painting, which had been missing for fifty years, was ___ in an attic by a family clearing out their grandmother's house.`,explanation:`"Discovered" means found.`,options:[`explored`,`uncovered`,`discovered`,`invented`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Uncovered" suggests removing a cover.`,3:`You invent new things, not find old ones.`,0:`"Explored" refers to places.`}},{id:`c1-mc-513`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`We need to ___ more resources to the project if it is to be completed on time.`,explanation:`"Allocate" means to assign.`,options:[`allude`,`allegiance`,`allocate`,`alleviate`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Allegiance" is loyalty.`,3:`"Alleviate" means to reduce suffering.`,0:`"Allude" means to refer to indirectly.`}},{id:`c1-mc-514`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The patient, ___ condition had deteriorated, was transferred to intensive care.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`who`,`whose`,`whom`,`which`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Who condition" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Whom condition" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Which condition" does not show possession for a person.`}},{id:`c1-mc-515`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`It was only when I read the letter again ___ I understood what she had meant.`,explanation:`"It was only when ... that" is a cleft structure.`,options:[`that`,`when`,`what`,`which`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"When" duplicates the earlier "when".`,3:`"Which" is ungrammatical here.`,2:`"What" is ungrammatical here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-516`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The organisers have asked ___ who wishes to attend to register in advance.`,explanation:`"Anyone who" means any person who.`,options:[`whichever`,`anyone`,`no one`,`someone`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Someone who wishes" does not express any person.`,2:`This reverses the meaning.`,0:`"Whichever" needs a noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-517`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Hardly ___ of the delegates had read the report before the meeting began.`,explanation:`"Hardly any" means very few.`,options:[`some`,`much`,`any`,`no`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Hardly no" is a double negative.`,0:`"Hardly some" is not idiomatic.`,1:`"Much" cannot go with the countable "delegates".`}},{id:`c1-mc-518`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I resent ___ being spoken to in that tone of voice.`,explanation:`A possessive before a gerund is formal usage.`,options:[`his`,`him's`,`he`,`himself`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A possessive or object is needed before the gerund.`,1:`"Him's" is nonstandard.`,3:`"Himself" is reflexive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-519`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The tour operator went ___ of business, leaving hundreds of holidaymakers stranded abroad.`,explanation:`"Go out of business" means to close permanently.`,options:[`bust`,`down`,`off`,`out`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Off of business" is nonstandard.`,0:`"Bust of business" is not the idiom.`,1:`"Down of business" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-520`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The cruise ship ___ at the port for only six hours, which gave passengers little time to explore.`,explanation:`"Docked" means moored at a port.`,options:[`dock`,`docked up`,`docked`,`docking`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Docked up" is not standard.`,0:`A past form is needed.`,3:`An auxiliary is missing.`}},{id:`c1-mc-521`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After the ___ of the first week, the students began to settle into a routine.`,explanation:`"Upheaval" means disruption and chaos.`,options:[`uptake`,`upbringing`,`upheaval`,`upkeep`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Upkeep" means maintenance.`,1:`"Upbringing" means childhood training.`,0:`"Uptake" means acceptance.`}},{id:`c1-mc-522`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Provided that the weather ___ fine, the event will take place in the park.`,explanation:`A present form follows "provided that" for the future.`,options:[`stayed`,`would stay`,`will stay`,`stays`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`A future form is not used after "provided that".`,1:`"Would" is not used here.`,0:`A past form does not refer to the future.`}},{id:`c1-mc-523`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Few employers are prepared to ___ staff who lack even basic digital skills.`,explanation:`"Take on" means to hire.`,options:[`take up`,`take in`,`take over`,`take on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Take in" means to absorb.`,0:`"Take up" means to start an activity.`,2:`"Take over" means to assume control.`}},{id:`c1-mc-524`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The archaeologists ___ at the site for three seasons before they found the first inscription.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`dug`,`were digging`,`have been digging`,`had been digging`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before the discovery.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a past discovery.`,0:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-525`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`It is advisable to take out insurance ___ theft before travelling.`,explanation:`"Insurance against" introduces the risk covered.`,options:[`of`,`on`,`against`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Insurance for theft" is acceptable but "against" is the standard collocation.`,0:`"Insurance of theft" is not standard.`,1:`"Insurance on theft" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-526`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She had no intention of showing ___ the letter.`,explanation:`"Anyone" is used in negative contexts.`,options:[`someone`,`no one`,`everyone`,`anyone`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"No intention of showing everyone" changes the meaning.`,0:`"Someone" is not used in negatives.`,1:`"No intention of showing no one" is a double negative.`}},{id:`c1-mc-527`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The soup was ___ salty that I could not finish it.`,explanation:`"Far too" suggests an excess.`,options:[`very`,`far too`,`enough`,`so much`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Very ... that" does not form a result clause.`,3:`"So much salty" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Enough" follows the adjective.`}},{id:`c1-mc-528`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`No sooner ___ the meeting started than the fire alarm went off.`,explanation:`"No sooner had" + subject + participle is the standard inversion.`,options:[`has`,`did`,`was`,`had`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect is needed in the inverted clause.`,1:`"No sooner did" is followed by the base form.`,2:`"Was the meeting started" is passive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-529`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The study found a strong ___ between poor diet and mental illness.`,explanation:`A "correlation" is a connection between two things.`,options:[`collaboration`,`correspondence`,`correlation`,`correction`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Correspondence" means letters or similarity.`,3:`"Correction" means a change to fix an error.`,0:`"Collaboration" means working together.`}},{id:`c1-mc-530`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ more she thought about it, the less convinced she became.`,explanation:`"The more ... the less" is a double comparative.`,options:[`Some`,`The`,`A`,`Much`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"A more" does not form the correlative.`,3:`"Much more" does not combine with "the less".`,0:`"Some more" does not form the correlative.`}},{id:`c1-mc-531`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There has been a marked increase in ___ number of complaints this year.`,explanation:`"The number of" refers to the total figure.`,options:[`a`,`the`,`some`,`no article`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"A number of" has a different meaning.`,2:`"Some number of" is not idiomatic.`,3:`The noun needs a determiner.`}},{id:`c1-mc-532`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ you were going to apply for the job; nobody told me you had changed your mind.`,explanation:`The past simple reports a conclusion reached at the time.`,options:[`was assuming`,`had assumed`,`assumed`,`have assumed`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The present perfect does not fit a past misunderstanding.`,0:`The continuous does not report a conclusion reached.`,1:`The past perfect needs a later past reference.`}},{id:`c1-mc-533`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The film is set ___ the backdrop of the Spanish Civil War.`,explanation:`"Set against the backdrop of" is the fixed phrase.`,options:[`on`,`in`,`against`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"On the backdrop" is not standard.`,3:`"At the backdrop" is not standard.`,1:`"In the backdrop" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-534`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ have told me earlier that the deadline had been brought forward; now it is far too late to rearrange anything.`,explanation:`"Could at least have" voices a mild reproach about a missed chance.`,options:[`needn't have`,`mustn't have`,`could at least`,`can't have`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Needn't have" would say that telling me was unnecessary, which contradicts the complaint.`,1:`"Mustn't have" is not used for criticism of a past omission.`,3:`"Can't have" is a negative deduction, not a reproach.`}},{id:`c1-mc-535`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The council has received ___ complaints about the noise from the building site.`,explanation:`"Numerous" means many.`,options:[`numerical`,`numerous`,`numerously`,`numeral`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Numeral" is a symbol for a number.`,0:`"Numerical" relates to numbers.`,2:`"Numerously" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-536`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The reason ___ she resigned was never made public.`,explanation:`"The reason why" is a standard structure.`,options:[`for which why`,`why`,`that why`,`what`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Why" and "for which" duplicate each other.`,3:`"What" cannot follow "reason".`,2:`"That why" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-537`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two companies have agreed to merge ___ the condition that jobs are protected.`,explanation:`"On the condition that" is the fixed phrase.`,options:[`in`,`with`,`on`,`under`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"In the condition" is not standard.`,3:`"Under the condition" is possible but "on" is the fixed phrase.`,1:`"With the condition" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-538`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The more I thought about the offer, the less attractive it ___.`,explanation:`The correlative structure uses the simple past.`,options:[`has seemed`,`seemed`,`seems`,`was seeming`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The sentence is in the past.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`"Seem" is a stative verb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-539`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ not be aware of the problem, but it has been affecting us for months.`,explanation:`"May not" expresses possibility.`,options:[`shall`,`ought`,`may`,`would`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Shall not be aware" is not natural.`,3:`"Would not be aware" changes the meaning.`,1:`"Ought not be aware" lacks "to".`}},{id:`c1-mc-540`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The cruise ship set ___ from Southampton at noon, bound for the Caribbean.`,explanation:`"Set sail" is the idiom for a ship starting a voyage.`,options:[`forth`,`out`,`sail`,`off`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Set out" is not used for ships leaving port in this phrase.`,3:`"Set off" is used for starting journeys in general.`,0:`"Set forth" is archaic.`}},{id:`c1-mc-541`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`They decided to ___ the main tourist routes and explore the lesser-known villages instead.`,explanation:`"Steer clear of" means to avoid.`,options:[`steer clear of`,`steer away`,`steer round of`,`steer out`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Steer away" needs "from".`,2:`This is not a fixed phrase.`,3:`"Steer out" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-542`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The decision to merge was taken ___ the advice of the company's lawyers.`,explanation:`"Against the advice of" means ignoring it.`,options:[`on`,`to`,`against`,`by`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"On the advice" would mean following it.`,1:`"To the advice" is not standard.`,3:`"By the advice" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-543`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The island is accessible only by a narrow causeway that is ___ at high tide.`,explanation:`"Submerged" means covered by water.`,options:[`subsided`,`submerged`,`substituted`,`subdued`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Subdued" means quiet or toned down.`,0:`"Subsided" means became less.`,2:`"Substituted" means replaced.`}},{id:`c1-mc-544`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The consultancy was brought in to ___ the efficiency of the company's supply chain.`,explanation:`"Evaluate" means to assess.`,options:[`evade`,`evaluate`,`evacuate`,`evoke`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Evacuate" means to leave a place.`,0:`"Evade" means to avoid.`,3:`"Evoke" means to call to mind.`}},{id:`c1-mc-545`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ have told me earlier; now it is too late to change the arrangements.`,explanation:`"Should have" expresses criticism of a past omission.`,options:[`might to`,`can`,`should`,`would to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Might to" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Would to" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Can have told" does not express criticism.`}},{id:`c1-mc-546`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I'd like to thank everyone ___ helped to make the event a success.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject.`,options:[`that's`,`which`,`whom`,`who`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Whom" is an object form.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`,0:`"That's" means "that is".`}},{id:`c1-mc-547`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ people believe that technology will solve every environmental problem, but few experts agree.`,explanation:`"Some" is used with plural countable nouns.`,options:[`Any`,`Much`,`Some`,`Every`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Any people believe" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Every people" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Much people" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-548`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She was offered a ___ position in the finance department, which she accepted with enthusiasm.`,explanation:`"Senior" means of higher rank.`,options:[`senior`,`seniorly`,`seniority`,`seniors`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Seniority" is a noun.`,3:`"Seniors" is a plural noun.`,1:`This word does not exist.`}},{id:`c1-mc-549`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The company has ___ a number of staff in order to cut costs.`,explanation:`"Lay off" means to dismiss workers.`,options:[`laid on`,`laid out`,`laid up`,`laid off`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Laid up" means ill in bed.`,1:`"Laid out" means arranged.`,0:`"Laid on" means provided.`}},{id:`c1-mc-550`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Everyone ___ to leave when the announcement was made that the delayed flight would depart shortly.`,explanation:`The past continuous shows an action in progress at a past moment.`,options:[`prepares`,`prepared`,`has prepared`,`was preparing`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The past continuous shows an action in progress.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit.`,0:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-551`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Don't ___ your chickens before they hatch; the deal has not been signed yet.`,explanation:`"Don't count your chickens" warns against premature confidence.`,options:[`count`,`feed`,`hatch`,`sell`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Hatch" cannot be repeated.`,1:`"Feed your chickens" is literal.`,3:`"Sell your chickens" is not the proverb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-552`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After the argument, they gave each other the cold ___ for days.`,explanation:`"Give someone the cold shoulder" means to ignore them.`,options:[`shoulder`,`shoulders`,`shudder`,`sholder`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The idiom uses the singular.`,2:`A "shudder" is a shiver.`,3:`This spelling is wrong.`}},{id:`c1-mc-553`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I'd rather you ___ the matter confidential until the announcement is made.`,explanation:`A past form expresses a present preference.`,options:[`kept`,`have kept`,`will keep`,`keep`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`After "would rather" + person use a past form.`,2:`"Will" is not used here.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-mc-554`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The government has come under fire for failing to ___ the root causes of rising inequality.`,explanation:`"Address" a problem means to deal with it.`,options:[`adjourn`,`adopt`,`adhere`,`address`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Adjourn" means to postpone a meeting.`,2:`"Adhere" means to stick or follow.`,1:`"Adopt" means to take up.`}},{id:`c1-mc-555`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The new manager is very ___ about the quality of everything the department produces.`,explanation:`"Particular" means demanding about details.`,options:[`particulars`,`particular`,`particularly`,`particle`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A "particle" is a tiny piece.`,2:`"Particularly" is an adverb.`,0:`"Particulars" are details.`}},{id:`c1-mc-556`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He was dismissed ___ grounds of persistent absenteeism.`,explanation:`"On grounds of" introduces the reason officially given.`,options:[`for`,`from`,`on`,`by`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"For grounds" is not standard.`,3:`"By grounds" is not standard.`,1:`"From grounds" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-557`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After a lifetime of hard work, she was finally able to ___ her feet up and enjoy a quiet retirement.`,explanation:`"Put one's feet up" means to relax.`,options:[`take`,`get`,`put`,`set`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Get her feet up" is not the idiom.`,3:`"Set her feet up" is not the idiom.`,0:`"Take her feet up" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-558`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`If you ___ find yourself in the neighbourhood, do call in and see us.`,explanation:`"Should" in an if-clause signals a remote possibility.`,options:[`would`,`shall`,`should`,`might to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Would find" does not form the formal conditional.`,1:`"Shall find" is not used here.`,3:`"Might to" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-559`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The inquiry was set up ___ the aftermath of the disaster.`,explanation:`"In the aftermath of" is the fixed phrase.`,options:[`by`,`on`,`in`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"On the aftermath" is not standard.`,3:`"At the aftermath" is not standard.`,0:`"By the aftermath" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-560`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Her speech was received ___ polite but unenthusiastic applause.`,explanation:`"Received with" introduces the reaction.`,options:[`with`,`in`,`to`,`by`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Received by applause" suggests the applause was the receiver.`,1:`"Received in applause" is not standard.`,2:`"Received to applause" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-561`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`His promises turned out to be nothing more than ___ air; he never delivered on any of them.`,explanation:`"Hot air" means empty, boastful talk.`,options:[`hot`,`thin`,`cold`,`fresh`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Thin air" means nothing, but "hot air" means empty talk.`,3:`"Fresh air" is literal.`,2:`"Cold air" is literal.`}},{id:`c1-mc-562`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The board ___ the proposal on the grounds that it was too risky.`,explanation:`"Reject" means to refuse.`,options:[`rejoiced`,`reflected`,`resented`,`rejected`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Reflected" means thought or mirrored.`,0:`"Rejoiced" means celebrated.`,2:`"Resented" means felt bitter about.`}},{id:`c1-mc-563`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The government has ___ a ban on the sale of the pesticide.`,explanation:`"Impose a ban" is a standard collocation.`,options:[`imposed`,`proposed`,`deposed`,`supposed`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Proposed" means suggested.`,3:`"Supposed" means assumed.`,2:`"Deposed" means removed from power.`}},{id:`c1-mc-564`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The tourist board has launched a campaign to ___ the region's cultural heritage.`,explanation:`"Promote" means to publicise.`,options:[`prohibit`,`prompt`,`promote`,`provoke`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Prompt" means to cause.`,0:`"Prohibit" means to ban.`,3:`"Provoke" means to cause a reaction.`}},{id:`c1-mc-565`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`There is a ___ possibility that the talks may break down altogether.`,explanation:`"A distinct possibility" means a real chance.`,options:[`distinctive`,`distinguished`,`distinct`,`distant`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Distant" means far away.`,0:`"Distinctive" means characteristic.`,1:`"Distinguished" means eminent.`}},{id:`c1-mc-566`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Each ___ the two proposals has its advantages and disadvantages.`,explanation:`"Each of" is followed by a plural noun.`,options:[`to`,`from`,`in`,`of`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Each from" is not grammatical.`,2:`"Each in" is not grammatical.`,0:`"Each to" is not grammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-567`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Although the firm ___ profitable for years, it has recently run into difficulties.`,explanation:`The past perfect marks a state before the recent difficulties.`,options:[`is`,`has been`,`was`,`had been`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect would mark an earlier period.`,2:`The simple past does not link to the present.`,0:`A present tense does not fit the concession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-568`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The new policy is likely to have far-___ consequences for the entire education system.`,explanation:`"Far-reaching" means having wide effects.`,options:[`looking`,`ranging`,`going`,`reaching`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Far-ranging" is possible but "far-reaching" is the fixed phrase.`,2:`"Far-going" is not standard.`,0:`"Far-looking" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-569`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`If you ___ to see him, please give him my regards.`,explanation:`"Should happen to" expresses a remote possibility.`,options:[`are happening`,`would happen`,`should happen`,`happen`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Would happen to see" is not the formal conditional.`,0:`A continuous form does not fit.`,3:`"Happen" alone lacks the tentative sense.`}},{id:`c1-mc-570`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`At this time next week, I ___ on a beach in Thailand without a care in the world.`,explanation:`The future continuous shows an action in progress at a future time.`,options:[`will lie`,`am lying`,`lie`,`will be lying`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The future simple does not show an action in progress.`,2:`A present tense does not fit.`,1:`The present continuous does not point to the future.`}},{id:`c1-mc-571`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The new bridge will be completed ___ the end of next year at the latest.`,explanation:`"By" shows a deadline.`,options:[`by`,`to`,`in`,`until`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Until" shows continuing action.`,1:`"To the end" has a different sense.`,2:`"In the end" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-572`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Despite repeated warnings, the company ___ ahead with its controversial restructuring plan.`,explanation:`"Press ahead" means to proceed with determination.`,options:[`pushed`,`pressed`,`pitched`,`pulled`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Pushed ahead" is possible but "pressed ahead" is the idiom.`,3:`"Pulled ahead" means overtook.`,2:`"Pitched ahead" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-573`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`He is a bit of a ___ and spends most evenings alone with his books.`,explanation:`A "recluse" avoids other people.`,options:[`recruit`,`recluse`,`rebel`,`refugee`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A "refugee" flees danger.`,0:`A "recruit" is a new member.`,2:`A "rebel" resists authority.`}},{id:`c1-mc-574`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The ancient city is a popular tourist ___ and attracts visitors throughout the year.`,explanation:`A "hotspot" is a popular place.`,options:[`hotshot`,`hothouse`,`hotspot`,`hotbed`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A "hotbed" is a place where something develops.`,0:`A "hotshot" is a talented person.`,1:`A "hothouse" is a warm greenhouse.`}},{id:`c1-mc-575`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The new manager is determined to ___ some order into the chaos of the department.`,explanation:`"Bring order into chaos" is a natural collocation.`,options:[`put`,`bring`,`make`,`take`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Take order into" is not the idiom.`,0:`"Put order into" is not the idiom.`,2:`"Make order into" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-576`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Heavy rain has ___ the risk of flooding in the region.`,explanation:`"Heightened" means increased.`,options:[`hastened`,`highlighted`,`hardened`,`heightened`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Highlighted" means emphasised.`,2:`"Hardened" means made stiff.`,0:`"Hastened" means speeded up.`}},{id:`c1-mc-577`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The manager decided to ___ the meeting until all the relevant data had been collected.`,explanation:`"Postpone" means to delay.`,options:[`postpone`,`impose`,`suppose`,`propose`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Impose" means to force.`,3:`"Propose" means to suggest.`,2:`"Suppose" means to assume.`}},{id:`c1-mc-578`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`They ___ well have anticipated the reaction, but they said nothing at the time.`,explanation:`"May well have" expresses a strong possibility.`,options:[`must`,`may`,`would`,`shall`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Would well have" does not express probability.`,0:`"Must well have" is not idiomatic.`,3:`"Shall well have" is not idiomatic.`}},{id:`c1-mc-579`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The firm's success is largely ___ to the energy and vision of its founder.`,explanation:`"Attributable to" means caused by.`,options:[`attribute`,`attributable`,`attributed`,`attributive`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Attributed to" is a participle; the question uses an adjective.`,3:`"Attributive" is a grammatical term.`,0:`"Attribute" is a verb or noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-580`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The report is based ___ interviews with more than two hundred employees.`,explanation:`"Based on" shows the source.`,options:[`on`,`upon the`,`at`,`in`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Based in" means located in.`,2:`"Based at" means located at.`,1:`The extra article breaks the phrase.`}},{id:`c1-mc-581`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After the funeral, the family gathered to ___ memories of the old man.`,explanation:`"Share memories" means to exchange them.`,options:[`share`,`shard`,`shear`,`spare`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Spare" means to give up.`,2:`"Shear" means to cut wool.`,1:`A "shard" is a broken piece.`}},{id:`c1-mc-582`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`He gave me the impression of ___ who had never doubted his own abilities.`,explanation:`"Someone who" describes a type of person.`,options:[`whom`,`someone`,`one`,`himself`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"One who" would be formal but "someone" fits the structure.`,0:`"Whom" is not a pronoun of this kind.`,3:`"Himself" is reflexive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-583`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Please give my ___ to your parents when you see them.`,explanation:`"Give my regards to" is a fixed greeting.`,options:[`regarding`,`regard`,`regards`,`regarded`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The greeting uses the plural.`,0:`"Regarding" is a participle.`,3:`"Regarded" is a participle.`}},{id:`c1-mc-584`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The negotiations, which ___ since January, are expected to conclude shortly.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous shows duration up to now.`,options:[`went on`,`are going on`,`go on`,`have been going on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The present simple does not show duration.`,1:`The present continuous does not show duration since a point.`,0:`The simple past does not link to now.`}},{id:`c1-mc-585`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I got to the station, the train ___ and the platform was deserted.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,options:[`left`,`had left`,`was leaving`,`has left`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-mc-586`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The old and the young alike ___ benefited from the reforms.`,explanation:`"The old and the young" takes a plural verb.`,options:[`has`,`having`,`have`,`had been`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"The old and the young" is plural.`,1:`A finite verb is needed.`,3:`The present perfect fits the sense.`}},{id:`c1-mc-587`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The experiment was designed to ___ whether the effect was caused by temperature.`,explanation:`"Ascertain" means to find out.`,options:[`ascertain`,`assure`,`ensure`,`insure`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Assure" means to promise.`,2:`"Ensure" means to make certain.`,3:`"Insure" means to cover with insurance.`}},{id:`c1-mc-588`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She was ___ with grief and unable to speak at her husband's funeral.`,explanation:`"Overcome with" means overwhelmed.`,options:[`overcoming`,`overcame`,`overcome`,`overcomes`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A participle is needed.`,0:`"Overcoming" is a participle.`,3:`"Overcomes" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-589`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The coach company has no control ___ the weather.`,explanation:`"Control over" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`of`,`to`,`over`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Control of" is possible but changes the sense.`,1:`"Control to" is not standard.`,3:`"Control at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-590`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Had it not been for the rescue team, the climbers ___ the night on the mountain.`,explanation:`The inverted third conditional uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`had not survived`,`will not have survived`,`would not survive`,`would not have survived`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`This refers to the present.`,1:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`,0:`The result clause needs a modal.`}},{id:`c1-mc-591`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company has been operating ___ a loss for the past three years.`,explanation:`"At a loss" is the standard collocation for business.`,options:[`in`,`at`,`on`,`with`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"In a loss" is not standard.`,2:`"On a loss" is not standard.`,3:`"With a loss" is not the fixed phrase.`}},{id:`c1-mc-592`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Many elderly people feel ___ from society because they lack access to digital services.`,explanation:`"Marginalised" means pushed to the edge of society.`,options:[`marginal`,`margined`,`marginalised`,`marginalise`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Marginal" is an adjective meaning small.`,1:`This word does not exist.`,3:`"Marginalise" is the base verb.`}},{id:`c1-mc-593`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The government's failure to act decisively has ___ public confidence in its ability to handle the crisis.`,explanation:`"Eroded" means gradually weakened.`,options:[`eroded`,`erased`,`erupted`,`erred`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Erased" means rubbed out.`,2:`"Erupted" means burst out.`,3:`"Erred" means made a mistake.`}},{id:`c1-mc-594`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The software ___ a number of security flaws that left users' data vulnerable.`,explanation:`"Contained" means had inside it.`,options:[`detained`,`retained`,`contented`,`contained`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Contented" means satisfied.`,1:`"Retained" means kept.`,0:`"Detained" means held in custody.`}},{id:`c1-mc-595`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ to have left earlier; now we will miss the train.`,explanation:`"Ought to have" expresses regret.`,options:[`ought`,`would`,`must`,`should to`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Should" is not followed by "to".`,2:`"Must to have" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Would to have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-596`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We apologise for any inconvenience caused ___ the temporary closure of the library.`,explanation:`"Caused by" introduces the agent or reason.`,options:[`by`,`from`,`through`,`with`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Caused with" is not standard.`,1:`"Caused from" is not standard.`,2:`"Caused through" is less usual.`}},{id:`c1-mc-597`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The committee ___ have reached its decision hastily, given how little time the members had to consider the evidence.`,explanation:`"May well have" expresses a strong probability about the past.`,options:[`might as well`,`may as well`,`can well`,`may well`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"May as well" suggests that there is nothing better to do, not a probability.`,0:`"Might as well" also means there is no good alternative.`,2:`"Can well" is not an idiomatic way to express probability.`}},{id:`c1-mc-598`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The final decision rests ___ the board, which will meet next week.`,explanation:`"Rest with" means to be the responsibility of.`,options:[`in`,`on`,`to`,`with`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Rests on" means depends on.`,0:`"Rests in" is not standard here.`,2:`"Rests to" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-599`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She hopes to ___ a career in diplomacy after completing her degree.`,explanation:`"Embark on" means to begin.`,options:[`embark at`,`embark in`,`embark for`,`embark on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Embark at" is not standard.`,2:`"Embark for" refers to leaving by ship.`,1:`"Embark in" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-600`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She has been working ___ the clock to meet the deadline.`,explanation:`"Against the clock" means in a hurry because of limited time.`,options:[`beyond`,`against`,`around`,`over`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Around the clock" has a different meaning.`,0:`"Beyond the clock" is not the idiom.`,3:`"Over the clock" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-601`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This time next month, the exhibition ___ and the gallery will be empty again.`,explanation:`"This time next month" suggests a completed event.`,options:[`closes`,`will have closed`,`will close`,`has closed`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The future perfect shows completion.`,0:`A present tense does not fit.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit a future time.`}},{id:`c1-mc-602`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`He claims that he ___ the money to charity before the investigation began.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an action before another past event.`,options:[`has donated`,`was donating`,`had donated`,`donated`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`,1:`The continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-mc-603`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The committee has been charged ___ drafting a code of conduct for all members.`,explanation:`"Charged with" introduces a task or an accusation.`,options:[`of`,`to`,`with`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Charged for" would mean being billed for.`,0:`"Charged of" is not standard here.`,1:`"Charged to" is not standard here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-604`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I was about to leave when the telephone ___.`,explanation:`The simple past reports the event that interrupted.`,options:[`was ringing`,`rang`,`had rung`,`has rung`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The simple past reports the interrupting event.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The past perfect would show an earlier event.`}},{id:`c1-mc-605`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The airline offered passengers compensation for the ___ caused by the cancellation.`,explanation:`"Inconvenience" is the noun.`,options:[`inconvenience`,`inconveniently`,`inconvenient`,`convenience`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Inconvenient" is an adjective.`,1:`"Inconveniently" is an adverb.`,3:`"Convenience" is the opposite meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-606`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The children's laughter echoed ___ the empty corridors of the old school.`,explanation:`"Through" suggests passing along the length of a space.`,options:[`across`,`through`,`beyond`,`within`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Across" suggests a surface.`,2:`"Beyond" suggests further than.`,3:`"Within" suggests inside limits.`}},{id:`c1-mc-607`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The policy, ___ was widely criticised at the time, has since been vindicated.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`what`,`that`,`whose`,`which`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,1:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-608`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The documents ___ for several weeks before anyone noticed they were missing.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`have been lying`,`had been lying`,`had lain to`,`were lain`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"To" is not used here.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`"Lie" is intransitive and has no passive.`}},{id:`c1-mc-609`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The new software is intended to ___ the process of recruitment.`,explanation:`"Streamline" means to make more efficient.`,options:[`straighten`,`stream`,`streamline`,`strengthen`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Stream" is a noun or verb.`,0:`"Straighten" means to make straight.`,3:`"Strengthen" means to make stronger.`}},{id:`c1-mc-610`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The minister was accused ___ misleading Parliament about the true cost of the scheme.`,explanation:`"Accused of" introduces the alleged offence.`,options:[`for`,`on`,`with`,`of`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Accused for" is not standard.`,2:`"Accused with" is not standard.`,1:`"Accused on" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-611`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She tried to ___ her disappointment, but her voice betrayed her.`,explanation:`"Mask" means to hide.`,options:[`masque`,`mash`,`mast`,`mask`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`A "masque" is a dramatic entertainment.`,1:`"Mash" means to crush.`,2:`A "mast" is a pole on a ship.`}},{id:`c1-mc-612`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`What ___ you ___ when I rang?`,explanation:`The past continuous shows an action in progress at a past moment.`,options:[`are / doing`,`were / doing`,`did / do`,`have / done`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The past continuous shows an action in progress.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,0:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-613`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It is not the cost ___ worries me, but the delay.`,explanation:`"That" introduces a cleft clause.`,options:[`that`,`whose`,`what`,`which`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"What" cannot follow a noun.`,3:`"Which" is less natural in a cleft.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-614`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The inspector took exception ___ the way the data had been presented.`,explanation:`"Take exception to" means to object to.`,options:[`to`,`at`,`with`,`for`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Take exception at" is not standard.`,2:`"Take exception with" is not standard.`,3:`"Take exception for" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-615`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The region is famed for its breathtaking ___, from snow-capped peaks to remote fjords.`,explanation:`"Scenery" refers to natural views.`,options:[`scenery`,`scenic`,`scene`,`sceneries`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A "scene" is a single view or event.`,3:`"Scenery" is uncountable.`,1:`"Scenic" is an adjective.`}},{id:`c1-mc-616`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`It is difficult to see how the scheme could be made to work ___ extra funding.`,explanation:`"Without" expresses absence.`,options:[`lacking`,`except`,`without`,`free`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Except extra funding" is possible but less natural.`,0:`"Lacking" would need "in".`,3:`"Free extra funding" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-mc-617`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Visitors are asked to ___ from feeding the animals.`,explanation:`"Refrain from" means to avoid doing.`,options:[`refrain`,`retain`,`restrain`,`remain`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Restrain" means to hold back by force.`,1:`"Retain" means to keep.`,3:`"Remain" means to stay.`}},{id:`c1-mc-618`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The innovation was hailed as a ___ in medical imaging that would transform diagnosis.`,explanation:`A "breakthrough" is an important discovery.`,options:[`breakup`,`breakdown`,`breakthrough`,`breakout`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A "breakdown" is a failure.`,3:`A "breakout" is an escape.`,0:`A "breakup" is an end.`}},{id:`c1-mc-619`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We had hoped the negotiations would be brief; as it turned out, they ___ go on for months.`,explanation:`"Were to" expresses a future event seen from a past viewpoint.`,options:[`would to`,`were to`,`had to be`,`were`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`Without "to" the verb does not express the unexpected future in the past.`,0:`"Would to" is not grammatical here.`,2:`"Had to be" means it was necessary, not that fate decreed it.`}},{id:`c1-mc-620`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The negotiations ___ for weeks before an agreement was finally reached.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`had been continuing`,`were continued`,`continued`,`have been continuing`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The present perfect does not fit a past agreement.`,2:`The simple past does not show duration before the agreement.`,1:`The negotiations are the doers.`}},{id:`c1-mc-621`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The old house had a ___ atmosphere that made visitors lower their voices.`,explanation:`"Hushed" means quiet and still.`,options:[`hushed`,`hushing`,`hush`,`hushes`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Hush" is a verb or noun.`,1:`"Hushing" is a participle.`,3:`"Hushes" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-622`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`We booked a ___ package that included flights, accommodation and car hire.`,explanation:`"Comprehensive" means including everything.`,options:[`comprehending`,`comprised`,`comprehensive`,`comprehensible`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Comprehensible" means understandable.`,0:`"Comprehending" is a participle.`,1:`"Comprised" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-623`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The composer is ___ to have written the symphony in a single week, although manuscripts suggest it took months.`,explanation:`"Is said to" is a reporting structure.`,options:[`told`,`talked`,`spoken`,`said`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Told to have written" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Spoken to have written" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Talked to have written" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-624`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The company has been ___ for years, and its managers refuse to adapt to new technology.`,explanation:`"Complacent" means smug and unwilling to change.`,options:[`complacent`,`compliant`,`complicit`,`complaisant`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Compliant" means obeying rules.`,3:`"Complaisant" means eager to please.`,2:`"Complicit" means involved in wrongdoing.`}},{id:`c1-mc-625`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Several species of bird are on the ___ of extinction because of habitat loss.`,explanation:`"On the verge of" means very close to.`,options:[`vergence`,`verge`,`verb`,`verse`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`A "verb" is a word class.`,3:`A "verse" is part of a poem.`,0:`This is not a standard word.`}},{id:`c1-mc-626`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The mountain village is cut ___ for weeks every winter when the pass is blocked by snow.`,explanation:`"Cut off" means isolated.`,options:[`up`,`down`,`off`,`out`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Cut out" means to remove or stop.`,1:`"Cut down" means to fell or reduce.`,0:`"Cut up" means to divide.`}},{id:`c1-mc-627`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The scheme has been designed ___ mind the needs of elderly residents.`,explanation:`"With ... in mind" is a fixed structure.`,options:[`to`,`in`,`by`,`with`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"In mind" lacks the preposition required before the noun phrase.`,0:`"To mind" is not standard.`,2:`"By mind" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-628`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It was in 1989 ___ the wall came down.`,explanation:`"It was ... that" is a cleft structure.`,options:[`that`,`when`,`what`,`which`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"It was ... when" is informal.`,3:`"Which" is ungrammatical here.`,2:`"What" is ungrammatical here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-629`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We ___ to sell the house, but in the end we decided to stay.`,explanation:`"Was going to" expresses an unfulfilled intention.`,options:[`went`,`were going`,`had gone`,`have gone`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The past continuous with "going to" expresses an unrealised plan.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The past perfect would imply the plan was completed.`}},{id:`c1-mc-630`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ well be late, so please start without us if we haven't arrived.`,explanation:`"May well" expresses a strong possibility.`,options:[`ought`,`may`,`shall`,`will to`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Will" is not followed by "to".`,2:`"Shall well be" is not idiomatic.`,0:`"Ought well be" lacks "to".`}},{id:`c1-mc-631`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The restructuring was presented as a cost-cutting exercise, but many employees saw it as a thinly veiled attempt to ___ older staff.`,explanation:`"Get rid of" means to remove or dismiss.`,options:[`get through`,`get round`,`get rid of`,`get over`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Get over" means to recover from.`,0:`"Get through" means to finish or survive.`,1:`"Get round" means to avoid or circumvent.`}},{id:`c1-mc-632`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It was the director ___ insisted on a full investigation, not the board.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject in a cleft sentence.`,options:[`whose`,`which`,`who`,`whom`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Whom" is an object form.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`,0:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-mc-633`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He claimed to have no recollection of ___ meeting in question.`,explanation:`"The ... in question" identifies a specific item.`,options:[`a`,`the`,`that`,`–`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"A meeting in question" is not idiomatic.`,2:`"That meeting in question" is awkward.`,3:`A singular countable noun needs a determiner.`}},{id:`c1-mc-634`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I am writing ___ response to your letter of 12 March.`,explanation:`"In response to" is the fixed phrase.`,options:[`on`,`at`,`in`,`by`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"On response" is not standard.`,1:`"At response" is not standard.`,3:`"By response" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-635`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Despite the government's assurances, many remain ___ about the safety of the new vaccine.`,explanation:`"Sceptical" means doubtful.`,options:[`sceptic`,`scepticly`,`scepticism`,`sceptical`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Scepticism" is a noun.`,0:`A "sceptic" is a person.`,1:`This word does not exist.`}},{id:`c1-mc-636`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I wasn't expecting the proposal, but I ___ say I find it quite appealing.`,explanation:`"I must say" is a fixed way of adding emphasis to an opinion.`,options:[`should`,`need`,`ought`,`must`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Should say" gives advice, not an admission.`,2:`"Ought say" lacks "to".`,1:`"Need say" does not express an admission.`}},{id:`c1-mc-637`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The report highlights ___ number of serious weaknesses in the existing system.`,explanation:`"A number of" means several.`,options:[`an`,`some`,`the`,`a`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Number" begins with a consonant sound.`,2:`"The number of" takes a singular verb and has a different meaning.`,1:`"Some number of" is not idiomatic.`}},{id:`c1-mc-638`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Visitors can expect a warm ___ from the locals, who pride themselves on their hospitality.`,explanation:`"A warm welcome" is a fixed collocation.`,options:[`welcome`,`welcomes`,`welcoming`,`welcomed`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Welcomed" is a participle.`,2:`"Welcoming" is an adjective.`,1:`"Welcomes" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-mc-639`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ number of complaints has risen sharply since the new system was introduced.`,explanation:`"The number of" takes a singular verb.`,options:[`The`,`Some`,`A`,`Many`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"A number of" takes a plural verb, but "has" is singular.`,1:`"Some number of" is not idiomatic here.`,3:`"Many number" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-640`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`He has two brothers, ___ is a doctor.`,explanation:`"One of whom" joins the clauses.`,options:[`one of them`,`one of whom`,`one of which`,`one of who`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Them" cannot join the clauses.`,3:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,2:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-mc-641`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The boy ___ parents were away for the weekend was staying with his grandmother.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`which`,`whose`,`who`,`whom`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Who parents" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Whom parents" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Which parents" does not show possession for a person.`}},{id:`c1-mc-642`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I would like to make ___ few suggestions before the meeting ends.`,explanation:`"A few" means some.`,options:[`the`,`one`,`a`,`–`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"The few" has a different meaning.`,1:`"One few" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"A few" requires the article.`}},{id:`c1-mc-643`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`We were given ___ little information that it was impossible to make an informed decision.`,explanation:`"So little ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`very`,`so`,`too`,`such`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Such little information" is awkward.`,0:`"Very little ... that" needs "so".`,2:`"Too" does not take "that".`}},{id:`c1-mc-644`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`After the argument, the two friends decided to bury the ___ and start again.`,explanation:`"Bury the hatchet" means to end a quarrel.`,options:[`sword`,`knife`,`axe`,`hatchet`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Bury the axe" is not the idiom.`,0:`"Bury the sword" is not the idiom.`,1:`"Bury the knife" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-645`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The discovery was hailed as a major ___ in the treatment of cancer.`,explanation:`A "breakthrough" is an important advance.`,options:[`breakup`,`breakdown`,`breakthrough`,`breakout`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A "breakdown" is a failure.`,3:`A "breakout" is an escape.`,0:`A "breakup" is a separation.`}},{id:`c1-mc-646`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I'm sure you ___ the new proposal by the time we meet tomorrow.`,explanation:`"By the time" + future perfect shows completion.`,options:[`read`,`will read`,`are reading`,`will have read`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The future perfect shows completion before a future time.`,0:`A past form does not fit.`,2:`The present continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-mc-647`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ have told me earlier that you were coming; I would have prepared a proper meal.`,explanation:`"Could have" can express a reproach about a missed opportunity.`,options:[`mustn't`,`could`,`can't`,`needn't`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Needn't have" says telling me was unnecessary.`,0:`"Mustn't have" is not used for criticism.`,2:`"Can't have" is a negative deduction.`}},{id:`c1-mc-648`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ for a taxi for twenty minutes before I realised that the service had been suspended.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past moment.`,options:[`had been waiting`,`was waiting`,`waited`,`have been waiting`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before the realisation.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit past events.`,2:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-mc-649`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The more she studied, the more she ___ that she knew very little.`,explanation:`The correlative uses the simple past in both parts.`,options:[`had realised`,`realised`,`realises`,`was realising`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The correlative structure uses the same tense.`,3:`The continuous weakens the parallel.`,2:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-mc-650`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ of the two proposals addresses the underlying problem, so we must start again.`,explanation:`"Neither" refers to two items and is negative.`,options:[`Both`,`None`,`Either`,`Neither`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"None" is for three or more.`,2:`"Either" would suggest at least one does.`,0:`"Both" would suggest each addresses it.`}},{id:`c1-mc-651`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The committee has two members, both of ___ are lawyers.`,explanation:`"Both of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`which`,`whom`,`them`,`who`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,0:`"Which" refers to things.`,2:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-652`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The campaign aims to ___ awareness of the dangers of drinking and driving.`,explanation:`"Raise awareness" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`arise`,`arouse`,`raise`,`rise`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Rise" does not take an object.`,0:`"Arise" means to appear.`,1:`"Arouse" means to stir feelings.`}},{id:`c1-mc-653`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The two brothers have never seen eye to ___ on money matters.`,explanation:`"See eye to eye" means to agree.`,options:[`hand`,`ear`,`face`,`eye`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"See face to face" is a different idiom.`,0:`"See hand to eye" is not an idiom.`,1:`"See ear to eye" is not an idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-654`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She is ___ authority on medieval manuscripts and is frequently consulted by museums.`,explanation:`"An authority on" introduces one of several experts.`,options:[`the`,`some`,`an`,`–`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"The authority" would suggest she is the only one.`,3:`A singular noun needs a determiner.`,1:`"Some" does not fit a single expert.`}},{id:`c1-mc-655`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Were it ___ for his intervention, the negotiations would have collapsed.`,explanation:`"Were it not for" means without.`,options:[`none`,`not`,`never`,`no`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Were it no" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Were it never" changes the meaning.`,0:`"Were it none" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-656`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The children were ___ with excitement as they waited for the fireworks to begin.`,explanation:`"Bursting with" means full of a strong emotion.`,options:[`bursting`,`boiling`,`bubbling`,`breaking`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Breaking with excitement" is not a collocation.`,2:`"Bubbling with" is possible but less usual.`,1:`"Boiling with excitement" is not idiomatic.`}},{id:`c1-mc-657`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I'll lend you the book on condition that you ___ it back by Friday.`,explanation:`A condition clause uses a present form for the future.`,options:[`will bring`,`would bring`,`brought`,`bring`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Will" is not used after "on condition that".`,2:`A past form changes the meaning.`,1:`"Would" is not used here.`}},{id:`c1-mc-658`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`We cannot afford to waste ___ more time on this issue.`,explanation:`"Any more" is used after a negative.`,options:[`some`,`many`,`a`,`any`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Some" is not used in negatives like this.`,2:`"A more time" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Time" is uncountable.`}},{id:`c1-mc-659`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`His claims are ___ odds with the findings of the independent inquiry.`,explanation:`"At odds with" means in conflict with.`,options:[`by`,`at`,`in`,`on`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"In odds" is not the idiom.`,3:`"On odds" is not the idiom.`,0:`"By odds" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-mc-660`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Visitors ___ not leave the building without signing out at the reception desk.`,explanation:`"Are not to" is the formal way of stating a prohibition.`,options:[`are to`,`may as well`,`ought`,`would rather`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Ought not leave" lacks "to" and is not a regulation.`,1:`"May as well not" suggests there is nothing to lose.`,3:`"Would rather not" expresses a preference, not a rule.`}},{id:`c1-mc-661`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The data ___ that the average temperature has risen by one degree.`,explanation:`"Data" takes a plural verb in careful usage.`,options:[`indicating`,`indicated`,`indicate`,`indicates`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`An auxiliary is missing.`,3:`In careful usage "data" takes a plural verb.`,1:`The sentence states a current finding.`}},{id:`c1-mc-662`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The introduction of the sugar tax was widely seen as a ___ step towards tackling obesity.`,explanation:`"Pioneering" means innovative.`,options:[`pioneer`,`pioneered`,`pioneers`,`pioneering`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`A "pioneer" is a person.`,1:`"Pioneered" is a past participle.`,2:`"Pioneers" is a plural noun.`}},{id:`c1-mc-663`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The old couple had been ___ for over fifty years and still held hands everywhere they went.`,explanation:`"Inseparable" describes people who are always together.`,options:[`indivisible`,`inseparate`,`inescapable`,`inseparable`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`This word does not exist.`,0:`"Indivisible" means cannot be divided mathematically or politically.`,2:`"Inescapable" means unavoidable.`}},{id:`c1-mc-664`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The firm was found guilty ___ breaching safety regulations.`,explanation:`"Guilty of" introduces the offence.`,options:[`with`,`on`,`of`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Guilty for" is not standard.`,0:`"Guilty with" is not standard.`,1:`"Guilty on" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-665`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Applicants must have a ___ understanding of financial regulations.`,explanation:`"Sound" means thorough and reliable.`,options:[`sounding`,`sonic`,`sound`,`sounded`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Sounded" is a past form.`,0:`"Sounding" is a participle.`,1:`"Sonic" relates to sound waves.`}},{id:`c1-mc-666`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The proposal, ___ details were kept secret until the last moment, was approved without debate.`,explanation:`"Whose" can show possession for things as well as people.`,options:[`which`,`whom`,`whose`,`of which`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Of which details" would need a different order.`,0:`"Which details" does not show possession here.`,1:`"Whom details" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-mc-667`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The decision was made ___ the advice of senior colleagues.`,explanation:`"Against the advice of" means ignoring it.`,options:[`for`,`on`,`by`,`against`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"On the advice" means following it.`,0:`"For the advice" is not standard.`,2:`"By the advice" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-mc-668`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The scientists, several of ___ had worked on the project for decades, were reluctant to abandon it.`,explanation:`"Several of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`who`,`which`,`whom`,`them`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`,3:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-mc-669`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The union has threatened to ___ industrial action if the dispute is not settled.`,explanation:`"Take industrial action" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`make`,`take`,`do`,`hold`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Make industrial action" is not the collocation.`,2:`"Do industrial action" is not the collocation.`,3:`"Hold industrial action" is not the collocation.`}},{id:`c1-mc-670`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`We need to ___ a decision before the end of the week.`,explanation:`"Reach a decision" is a standard collocation.`,options:[`achieve`,`gain`,`reach`,`arrive`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Arrive" does not take a direct object.`,0:`"Achieve a decision" is not natural.`,1:`"Gain a decision" is not natural.`}},{id:`c1-mc-671`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The trek is not for the ___ of heart; it involves several days of steep climbing at altitude.`,explanation:`"Not for the faint-hearted" is a fixed idiom.`,options:[`feint`,`fainted`,`fine`,`faint`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Fainted" is a participle.`,0:`A "feint" is a deceptive movement.`,2:`"Fine" does not fit the idiom.`}}]};export{e as default};